{"surah": 17, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "سُبْحَٰنَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِۦ لَيْلًا مِّنَ ٱلْمَسْجِدِ ٱلْحَرَامِ إِلَى ٱلْمَسْجِدِ ٱلْأَقْصَا ٱلَّذِى بَٰرَكْنَا حَوْلَهُۥ لِنُرِيَهُۥ مِنْ ءَايَٰتِنَآۚ إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلسَّمِيعُ ٱلْبَصِيرُ ", "translation": "Exalted[1] is He who took His Servant [i.e., Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ)] by night from al-Masjid al-Ḥarām to al-Masjid al-Aqṣā,[2] whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs. Indeed, He is the Hearing,[3] the Seeing.[4]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsَaʾ", "root": "سري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِعَبْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْbَʿعِb", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَيْلًا", "translit": "āًlْyَl", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسْجِدِ", "translit": "ِdِjgْsَmْlٱ", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرَامِ", "translit": "ِmāَrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسْجِدِ", "translit": "ِdِjgْsَmْlٱ", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْأَقْصَا", "translit": "āَṣْqَaʾْlٱ", "root": "قصو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَٰرَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَrَٰb", "root": "برك"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "حَوْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْwَḥ", "root": "حول"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِنُرِيَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَyِrُnِl", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمِيعُ", "translit": "ُʿعyِmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْبَصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَbْlٱ", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَى ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَجَعَلْنَٰهُ هُدًى لِّبَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ أَلَّا تَتَّخِذُوا۟ مِن دُونِى وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "And We gave Moses the Scripture and made it a guidance for the Children of Israel that you not take other than Me as Disposer of affairs,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُوسَى", "translit": "àَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُدًى", "translit": "àًdُh", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّبَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَbِّl", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَتَّخِذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdِhkَّtَt", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دُونِى", "translit": "àِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "ذُرِّيَّةَ مَنْ حَمَلْنَا مَعَ نُوحٍۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ عَبْدًا شَكُورًا ", "translation": "O descendants of those We carried [in the ship] with Noah. Indeed, he was a grateful servant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَمَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَmَḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَبْدًا", "translit": "āًdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَكُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُkَhs", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَقَضَيْنَآ إِلَىٰ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ لَتُفْسِدُنَّ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ مَرَّتَيْنِ وَلَتَعْلُنَّ عُلُوًّا كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "And We conveyed[1] to the Children of Israel in the Scripture that, \"You will surely cause corruption on the earth twice, and you will surely reach [a degree of] great haughtiness.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَضَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḍَqَw", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَتُفْسِدُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُdِsْfُtَl", "root": "فسد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَرَّتَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَtَّrَm", "root": "مرر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَتَعْلُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُlْʿعَtَlَw", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عُلُوًّا", "translit": "āًّwُlُʿع", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ أُولَىٰهُمَا بَعَثْنَا عَلَيْكُمْ عِبَادًا لَّنَآ أُو۟لِى بَأْسٍ شَدِيدٍ فَجَاسُوا۟ خِلَٰلَ ٱلدِّيَارِۚ وَكَانَ وَعْدًا مَّفْعُولًا ", "translation": "So when the [time of] promise came for the first of them,[1] We sent against you servants of Ours - those of great military might, and they probed [even] into the homes,[2] and it was a promise fulfilled.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُولَىٰهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhٰàَlwُaʾ", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَعَثْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhtَʿعَb", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِبَادًا", "translit": "āًdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أُو۟لِى", "translit": "àِl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَأْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْaʾَb", "root": "باس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "شَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَجَاسُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُsāَjgَf", "root": "جوس"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خِلَٰلَ", "translit": "َlَٰlِhk", "root": "خلل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلدِّيَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَyِّdlٱ", "root": "دور"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَعْدًا", "translit": "āًdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَّفْعُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُʿعْfَّm", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "ثُمَّ رَدَدْنَا لَكُمُ ٱلْكَرَّةَ عَلَيْهِمْ وَأَمْدَدْنَٰكُم بِأَمْوَٰلٍ وَبَنِينَ وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ أَكْثَرَ نَفِيرًا ", "translation": "Then We gave back to you a return victory over them. And We reinforced you with wealth and sons and made you more numerous in manpower.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَدَدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdَdَr", "root": "ردد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكَرَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّrَkْlٱ", "root": "كرر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَمْدَدْنَٰكُم", "translit": "mُkَٰnْdَdْmَaʾَw", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِأَمْوَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰwْmَaʾِb", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَبَنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnَbَw", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَٰnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَكْثَرَ", "translit": "َrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَفِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِfَn", "root": "نفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "إِنْ أَحْسَنتُمْ أَحْسَنتُمْ لِأَنفُسِكُمْۖ وَإِنْ أَسَأْتُمْ فَلَهَاۚ فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ لِيَسُۥٓـُٔوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ وَلِيَدْخُلُوا۟ ٱلْمَسْجِدَ كَمَا دَخَلُوهُ أَوَّلَ مَرَّةٍ وَلِيُتَبِّرُوا۟ مَا عَلَوْا۟ تَتْبِيرًا ", "translation": "[And said], \"If you do good, you do good for yourselves; and if you do evil, [you do it] to them [i.e., yourselves].\" Then when the final [i.e., second] promise came, [We sent your enemies] to sadden your faces and to enter the masjid [i.e., the temple in Jerusalem], as they entered it the first time, and to destroy what they had taken over with [total] destruction.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَحْسَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَحْسَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِأَنفُسِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِsُfnَaʾِl", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَسَأْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْaʾَsَaʾ", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِيَسُۥٓـُٔوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُٔـٓۥُsَyِl", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وُجُوهَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلِيَدْخُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُhkْdَyِlَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسْجِدَ", "translit": "َdِjgْsَmْlٱ", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "دَخَلُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُlَhkَd", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَوَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّwَaʾ", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَرَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّrَm", "root": "مرر"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَلِيُتَبِّرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِّbَtُyِlَw", "root": "تبر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "عَلَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَlَʿع", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "تَتْبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbْtَt", "root": "تبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "عَسَىٰ رَبُّكُمْ أَن يَرْحَمَكُمْۚ وَإِنْ عُدتُّمْ عُدْنَاۘ وَجَعَلْنَا جَهَنَّمَ لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ حَصِيرًا ", "translation": "[Then Allāh said], \"It is expected, [if you repent], that your Lord will have mercy upon you. But if you return [to sin], We will return [to punishment]. And We have made Hell, for the disbelievers, a prison-bed.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَرْحَمَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَmَḥْrَy", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عُدتُّمْ", "translit": "ْmُّtdُʿع", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عُدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdُʿع", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "حَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَḥ", "root": "حصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "إِنَّ هَٰذَا ٱلْقُرْءَانَ يَهْدِى لِلَّتِى هِىَ أَقْوَمُ وَيُبَشِّرُ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ٱلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ أَنَّ لَهُمْ أَجْرًا كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "Indeed, this Qur’ān guides to that which is most suitable and gives good tidings to the believers who do righteous deeds that they will have a great reward", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَقْوَمُ", "translit": "ُmَwْqَaʾ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيُبَشِّرُ", "translit": "ُrِّhsَbُyَw", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَجْرًا", "translit": "āًrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "وَأَنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ أَعْتَدْنَا لَهُمْ عَذَابًا أَلِيمًا ", "translation": "And that those who do not believe in the Hereafter - We have prepared for them a painful punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱِb", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَعْتَدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdَtْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عتد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَيَدْعُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ بِٱلشَّرِّ دُعَآءَهُۥ بِٱلْخَيْرِۖ وَكَانَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ عَجُولًا ", "translation": "And man supplicates for evil [when angry] as he supplicates for good, and man is ever hasty.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَدْعُ", "translit": "ُʿعْdَyَw", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلشَّرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَّhslٱِb", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دُعَآءَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَءٓāَʿعُd", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْخَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَhkْlٱِb", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَجُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُjgَʿع", "root": "عجل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَا ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ ءَايَتَيْنِۖ فَمَحَوْنَآ ءَايَةَ ٱلَّيْلِ وَجَعَلْنَآ ءَايَةَ ٱلنَّهَارِ مُبْصِرَةً لِّتَبْتَغُوا۟ فَضْلًا مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ وَلِتَعْلَمُوا۟ عَدَدَ ٱلسِّنِينَ وَٱلْحِسَابَۚ وَكُلَّ شَىْءٍ فَصَّلْنَٰهُ تَفْصِيلًا ", "translation": "And We have made the night and day two signs, and We erased the sign of the night and made the sign of the day visible[1] that you may seek bounty from your Lord and may know the number of years and the account [of time]. And everything We have set out in detail.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَتَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَtَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَمَحَوْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْwَḥَmَf", "root": "محو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَايَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ءَايَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُبْصِرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrِṣْbُm", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِّتَبْتَغُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُغَtْbَtِّl", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَضْلًا", "translit": "āًlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "رَّبِّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلِتَعْلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlْʿعَtِlَw", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَدَدَ", "translit": "َdَdَʿع", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلسِّنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِّslٱ", "root": "سنو"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱلْحِسَابَ", "translit": "َbāَsِḥْlٱَw", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَكُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فَصَّلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَّṣَf", "root": "فصل"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "تَفْصِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِṣْfَt", "root": "فصل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَكُلَّ إِنسَٰنٍ أَلْزَمْنَٰهُ طَٰٓئِرَهُۥ فِى عُنُقِهِۦۖ وَنُخْرِجُ لَهُۥ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ كِتَٰبًا يَلْقَىٰهُ مَنشُورًا ", "translation": "And [for] every person We have imposed his fate upon his neck,[1] and We will produce for him on the Day of Resurrection a record which he will encounter spread open.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنسَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰsnِiʾ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَلْزَمْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْmَzْlَaʾ", "root": "لزم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "طَٰٓئِرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrِyʾَٰٓṭ", "root": "طير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عُنُقِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِqُnُʿع", "root": "عنق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَنُخْرِجُ", "translit": "ُjgِrْhkُnَw", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كِتَٰبًا", "translit": "āًbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَلْقَىٰهُ", "translit": "ُhٰàَqْlَy", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَنشُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُhsnَm", "root": "نشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "ٱقْرَأْ كِتَٰبَكَ كَفَىٰ بِنَفْسِكَ ٱلْيَوْمَ عَلَيْكَ حَسِيبًا ", "translation": "[It will be said], \"Read your record. Sufficient is yourself against you this Day as accountant.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱقْرَأْ", "translit": "ْaʾَrْqٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كِتَٰبَكَ", "translit": "َkَbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَk", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِنَفْسِكَ", "translit": "َkِsْfَnِb", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَسِيبًا", "translit": "āًbyِsَḥ", "root": "حسب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "مَّنِ ٱهْتَدَىٰ فَإِنَّمَا يَهْتَدِى لِنَفْسِهِۦۖ وَمَن ضَلَّ فَإِنَّمَا يَضِلُّ عَلَيْهَاۚ وَلَا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰۗ وَمَا كُنَّا مُعَذِّبِينَ حَتَّىٰ نَبْعَثَ رَسُولًا ", "translation": "Whoever is guided is only guided for [the benefit of] his soul. And whoever errs only errs against it. And no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another. And never would We punish until We sent a messenger.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّنِ", "translit": "ِnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱهْتَدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdَtْhٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَهْتَدِى", "translit": "àِdَtْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِنَفْسِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِsْfَnِl", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَضِلُّ", "translit": "ُّlِḍَy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "تَزِرُ", "translit": "ُrِzَt", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَازِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِzāَw", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وِزْرَ", "translit": "َrْzِw", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مُعَذِّبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِّhdَʿعُm", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "نَبْعَثَ", "translit": "َhtَʿعْbَn", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "رَسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَإِذَآ أَرَدْنَآ أَن نُّهْلِكَ قَرْيَةً أَمَرْنَا مُتْرَفِيهَا فَفَسَقُوا۟ فِيهَا فَحَقَّ عَلَيْهَا ٱلْقَوْلُ فَدَمَّرْنَٰهَا تَدْمِيرًا ", "translation": "And when We intend to destroy a city, We command its affluent[1] but they defiantly disobey therein; so the word [i.e., deserved decree] comes into effect upon it, and We destroy it with [complete] destruction.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَدْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْdَrَaʾ", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نُّهْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْhُّn", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْيَةً", "translit": "ًẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَمَرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُتْرَفِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِfَrْtُm", "root": "ترف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَفَسَقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَsَfَf", "root": "فسق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَحَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥَf", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلُ", "translit": "ُlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَدَمَّرْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْrَّmَdَf", "root": "دمر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَدْمِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِmْdَt", "root": "دمر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا مِنَ ٱلْقُرُونِ مِنۢ بَعْدِ نُوحٍۗ وَكَفَىٰ بِرَبِّكَ بِذُنُوبِ عِبَادِهِۦ خَبِيرًۢا بَصِيرًا ", "translation": "And how many have We destroyed from the generations after Noah. And sufficient is your Lord, concerning the sins of His servants, as Aware and Seeing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَمْ", "translit": "ْmَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَكَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَkَw", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِرَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِذُنُوبِ", "translit": "ِbwُnُhdِb", "root": "ذنب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عِبَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خَبِيرًۢا", "translit": "āًۢryِbَhk", "root": "خبر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَb", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "مَّن كَانَ يُرِيدُ ٱلْعَاجِلَةَ عَجَّلْنَا لَهُۥ فِيهَا مَا نَشَآءُ لِمَن نُّرِيدُ ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَا لَهُۥ جَهَنَّمَ يَصْلَىٰهَا مَذْمُومًا مَّدْحُورًا ", "translation": "Whoever should desire the immediate[1] - We hasten for him from it what We will to whom We intend. Then We have made for him Hell, which he will [enter to] burn, censured and banished.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُy", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْعَاجِلَةَ", "translit": "َẗَlِjgāَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَجَّلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَّjgَʿع", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَn", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نُّرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُّn", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَصْلَىٰهَا", "translit": "āَhٰàَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَذْمُومًا", "translit": "āًmwُmْhdَm", "root": "ذمم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَّدْحُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُḥْdَّm", "root": "دحر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "وَمَنْ أَرَادَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ وَسَعَىٰ لَهَا سَعْيَهَا وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ كَانَ سَعْيُهُم مَّشْكُورًا ", "translation": "But whoever desires the Hereafter and exerts the effort due to it while he is a believer - it is those whose effort is ever appreciated [by Allāh].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَادَ", "translit": "َdāَrَaʾ", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَسَعَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَʿعَsَw", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَعْيَهَا", "translit": "āَhَyْʿعَs", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُؤْمِنٌ", "translit": "ٌnِmْwʾُm", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَعْيُهُم", "translit": "mُhُyْʿعَs", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّشْكُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُkْhsَّm", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "كُلًّا نُّمِدُّ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ وَهَٰٓؤُلَآءِ مِنْ عَطَآءِ رَبِّكَۚ وَمَا كَانَ عَطَآءُ رَبِّكَ مَحْظُورًا ", "translation": "To each [category] We extend - to these and to those - from the gift of your Lord. And never has the gift of your Lord been restricted.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُلًّا", "translit": "āًّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُّمِدُّ", "translit": "ُّdِmُّn", "root": "مدد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَهَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَطَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَṭَʿع", "root": "عطو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَطَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَṭَʿع", "root": "عطو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَحْظُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُẓْḥَm", "root": "حظر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "ٱنظُرْ كَيْفَ فَضَّلْنَا بَعْضَهُمْ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۚ وَلَلْءَاخِرَةُ أَكْبَرُ دَرَجَٰتٍ وَأَكْبَرُ تَفْضِيلًا ", "translation": "Look how We have favored [in provision] some of them over others. But the Hereafter is greater in degrees [of difference] and greater in distinction.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱنظُرْ", "translit": "ْrُẓnٱ", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَضَّلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَّḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَعْضَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَلْءَاخِرَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَrِhkāَءْlَlَw", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَكْبَرُ", "translit": "ُrَbْkَaʾ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "دَرَجَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰjgَrَd", "root": "درج"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَكْبَرُ", "translit": "ُrَbْkَaʾَw", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَفْضِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِḍْfَt", "root": "فضل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "لَّا تَجْعَلْ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَٰهًا ءَاخَرَ فَتَقْعُدَ مَذْمُومًا مَّخْذُولًا ", "translation": "Do not make [as equal] with Allāh another deity and [thereby] become censured and forsaken.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَجْعَلْ", "translit": "ْlَʿعْjgَt", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَٰهًا", "translit": "āًhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاخَرَ", "translit": "َrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَتَقْعُدَ", "translit": "َdُʿعْqَtَf", "root": "قعد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَذْمُومًا", "translit": "āًmwُmْhdَm", "root": "ذمم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّخْذُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُhdْhkَّm", "root": "خذل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "وَقَضَىٰ رَبُّكَ أَلَّا تَعْبُدُوٓا۟ إِلَّآ إِيَّاهُ وَبِٱلْوَٰلِدَيْنِ إِحْسَٰنًاۚ إِمَّا يَبْلُغَنَّ عِندَكَ ٱلْكِبَرَ أَحَدُهُمَآ أَوْ كِلَاهُمَا فَلَا تَقُل لَّهُمَآ أُفٍّ وَلَا تَنْهَرْهُمَا وَقُل لَّهُمَا قَوْلًا كَرِيمًا ", "translation": "And your Lord has decreed that you worship not except Him, and to parents, good treatment. Whether one or both of them reach old age [while] with you, say not to them [so much as], \"uff,\"[1] and do not repel them but speak to them a noble word.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَضَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḍَqَw", "root": "قضي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَعْبُدُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdُbْʿعَt", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِيَّاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَّyِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَبِٱلْوَٰلِدَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَdِlَٰwْlٱِbَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِحْسَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰsْḥِiʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَبْلُغَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَغُlْbَy", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عِندَكَ", "translit": "َkَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْكِبَرَ", "translit": "َrَbِkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَحَدُهُمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmُhُdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كِلَاهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhāَlِk", "root": "كلو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تَقُل", "translit": "lُqَt", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَّهُمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أُفٍّ", "translit": "ٍّfُaʾ", "root": "افف"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَنْهَرْهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhْrَhْnَt", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "لَّهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "قَوْلًا", "translit": "āًlْwَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "كَرِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِrَk", "root": "كرم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "وَٱخْفِضْ لَهُمَا جَنَاحَ ٱلذُّلِّ مِنَ ٱلرَّحْمَةِ وَقُل رَّبِّ ٱرْحَمْهُمَا كَمَا رَبَّيَانِى صَغِيرًا ", "translation": "And lower to them the wing of humility out of mercy and say, \"My Lord, have mercy upon them as they brought me up [when I was] small.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱخْفِضْ", "translit": "ْḍِfْhkٱَw", "root": "خفض"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَنَاحَ", "translit": "َḥāَnَjg", "root": "جنح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلذُّلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُّhdlٱ", "root": "ذلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱرْحَمْهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhْmَḥْrٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَبَّيَانِى", "translit": "àِnāَyَّbَr", "root": "ربو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "صَغِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِغَṣ", "root": "صغر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "رَّبُّكُمْ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا فِى نُفُوسِكُمْۚ إِن تَكُونُوا۟ صَٰلِحِينَ فَإِنَّهُۥ كَانَ لِلْأَوَّٰبِينَ غَفُورًا ", "translation": "Your Lord is most knowing of what is within yourselves. If you should be righteous [in intention] - then indeed He is ever, to the often returning [to Him], Forgiving.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُفُوسِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِswُfُn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَكُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُkَt", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَٰلِحِينَ", "translit": "َnyِḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِلْأَوَّٰبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbَّٰwَaʾْlِl", "root": "اوب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "غَفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "وَءَاتِ ذَا ٱلْقُرْبَىٰ حَقَّهُۥ وَٱلْمِسْكِينَ وَٱبْنَ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَلَا تُبَذِّرْ تَبْذِيرًا ", "translation": "And give the relative his right, and [also] the poor and the traveler, and do not spend wastefully.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَءَاتِ", "translit": "ِtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذَا", "translit": "āَhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْبَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَbْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَقَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّqَḥ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْمِسْكِينَ", "translit": "َnyِkْsِmْlٱَw", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱبْنَ", "translit": "َnْbٱَw", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَّslٱ", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تُبَذِّرْ", "translit": "ْrِّhdَbُt", "root": "بذر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "تَبْذِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhdْbَt", "root": "بذر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْمُبَذِّرِينَ كَانُوٓا۟ إِخْوَٰنَ ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِۖ وَكَانَ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ لِرَبِّهِۦ كَفُورًا ", "translation": "Indeed, the wasteful are brothers of the devils, and ever has Satan been to his Lord ungrateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُبَذِّرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِّhdَbُmْlٱ", "root": "بذر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَانُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِخْوَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰwْhkِiʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيَٰطِينِ", "translit": "ِnyِṭَٰyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِرَبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَrِl", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfَk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَإِمَّا تُعْرِضَنَّ عَنْهُمُ ٱبْتِغَآءَ رَحْمَةٍ مِّن رَّبِّكَ تَرْجُوهَا فَقُل لَّهُمْ قَوْلًا مَّيْسُورًا ", "translation": "And if you [must] turn away from them [i.e., the needy] awaiting mercy from your Lord which you expect,[1] then speak to them a gentle word.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُعْرِضَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَḍِrْʿعُt", "root": "عرض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱبْتِغَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَغِtْbٱ", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَحْمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَرْجُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُjgْrَt", "root": "رجو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَقُل", "translit": "lُqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَوْلًا", "translit": "āًlْwَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّيْسُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُsْyَّm", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "وَلَا تَجْعَلْ يَدَكَ مَغْلُولَةً إِلَىٰ عُنُقِكَ وَلَا تَبْسُطْهَا كُلَّ ٱلْبَسْطِ فَتَقْعُدَ مَلُومًا مَّحْسُورًا ", "translation": "And do not make your hand [as] chained to your neck[1] or extend it completely[2] and [thereby] become blamed and insolvent.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَجْعَلْ", "translit": "ْlَʿعْjgَt", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَدَكَ", "translit": "َkَdَy", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَغْلُولَةً", "translit": "ًẗَlwُlْغَm", "root": "غلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عُنُقِكَ", "translit": "َkِqُnُʿع", "root": "عنق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَبْسُطْهَا", "translit": "āَhْṭُsْbَt", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْبَسْطِ", "translit": "ِṭْsَbْlٱ", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَتَقْعُدَ", "translit": "َdُʿعْqَtَf", "root": "قعد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَلُومًا", "translit": "āًmwُlَm", "root": "لوم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَّحْسُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُsْḥَّm", "root": "حسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "إِنَّ رَبَّكَ يَبْسُطُ ٱلرِّزْقَ لِمَن يَشَآءُ وَيَقْدِرُۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ بِعِبَادِهِۦ خَبِيرًۢا بَصِيرًا ", "translation": "Indeed, your Lord extends provision for whom He wills and restricts [it]. Indeed He is ever, concerning His servants, Aware and Seeing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَبْسُطُ", "translit": "ُṭُsْbَy", "root": "بسط"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلرِّزْقَ", "translit": "َqْzِّrlٱ", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَيَقْدِرُ", "translit": "ُrِdْqَyَw", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِعِبَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَbِʿعِb", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَبِيرًۢا", "translit": "āًۢryِbَhk", "root": "خبر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَb", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَلَا تَقْتُلُوٓا۟ أَوْلَٰدَكُمْ خَشْيَةَ إِمْلَٰقٍۖ نَّحْنُ نَرْزُقُهُمْ وَإِيَّاكُمْۚ إِنَّ قَتْلَهُمْ كَانَ خِطْـًٔا كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "And do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you. Indeed, their killing is ever a great sin.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَقْتُلُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlُtْqَt", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَوْلَٰدَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَdَٰlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَشْيَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyْhsَhk", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِمْلَٰقٍ", "translit": "ٍqَٰlْmِiʾ", "root": "ملق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَّحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَّn", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نَرْزُقُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُqُzْrَn", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِيَّاكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkāَّyِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَتْلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlْtَq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خِطْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْṭِhk", "root": "خطا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ ٱلزِّنَىٰٓۖ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ فَٰحِشَةً وَسَآءَ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "And do not approach unlawful sexual intercourse.[1] Indeed, it is ever an immorality and is evil as a way.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَقْرَبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَrْqَt", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلزِّنَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَnِّzlٱ", "root": "زني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٰحِشَةً", "translit": "ًẗَhsِḥَٰf", "root": "فحش"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَسَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَsَw", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "وَلَا تَقْتُلُوا۟ ٱلنَّفْسَ ٱلَّتِى حَرَّمَ ٱللَّهُ إِلَّا بِٱلْحَقِّۗ وَمَن قُتِلَ مَظْلُومًا فَقَدْ جَعَلْنَا لِوَلِيِّهِۦ سُلْطَٰنًا فَلَا يُسْرِف فِّى ٱلْقَتْلِۖ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ مَنصُورًا ", "translation": "And do not kill the soul [i.e., person] which Allāh has forbidden, except by right.[1] And whoever is killed unjustly - We have given his heir authority,[2] but let him not exceed limits in [the matter of] taking life. Indeed, he has been supported [by the law].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَقْتُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُtْqَt", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّفْسَ", "translit": "َsْfَّnlٱ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَرَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّrَḥ", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قُتِلَ", "translit": "َlِtُq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَظْلُومًا", "translit": "āًmwُlْẓَm", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِوَلِيِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّyِlَwِl", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُسْرِف", "translit": "fِrْsُy", "root": "سرف"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فِّى", "translit": "àِّf", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْقَتْلِ", "translit": "ِlْtَqْlٱ", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مَنصُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُṣnَm", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ مَالَ ٱلْيَتِيمِ إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ أَشُدَّهُۥۚ وَأَوْفُوا۟ بِٱلْعَهْدِۖ إِنَّ ٱلْعَهْدَ كَانَ مَسْـُٔولًا ", "translation": "And do not approach the property of an orphan, except in the way that is best,[1] until he reaches maturity. And fulfill [every] commitment. Indeed, the commitment is ever [that about which one will be] questioned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَقْرَبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَrْqَt", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَالَ", "translit": "َlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْيَتِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِtَyْlٱ", "root": "يتم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَبْلُغَ", "translit": "َغُlْbَy", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَشُدَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّdُhsَaʾ", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَوْفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfْwَaʾَw", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَهْدِ", "translit": "ِdْhَʿعْlٱِb", "root": "عهد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْعَهْدَ", "translit": "َdْhَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عهد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَسْـُٔولًا", "translit": "āًlwُٔـْsَm", "root": "سال"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "وَأَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ إِذَا كِلْتُمْ وَزِنُوا۟ بِٱلْقِسْطَاسِ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمِۚ ذَٰلِكَ خَيْرٌ وَأَحْسَنُ تَأْوِيلًا ", "translation": "And give full measure when you measure, and weigh with an even [i.e., honest] balance. That is the best [way] and best in result.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَوْفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfْwَaʾَw", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْكَيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَkْlٱ", "root": "كيل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كِلْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْlِk", "root": "كيل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَزِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِzَw", "root": "وزن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلْقِسْطَاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَṭْsِqْlٱِb", "root": "قسطس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِqَtْsُmْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾَw", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَأْوِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِwْaʾَt", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَلَا تَقْفُ مَا لَيْسَ لَكَ بِهِۦ عِلْمٌۚ إِنَّ ٱلسَّمْعَ وَٱلْبَصَرَ وَٱلْفُؤَادَ كُلُّ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ كَانَ عَنْهُ مَسْـُٔولًا ", "translation": "And do not pursue[1] that of which you have no knowledge. Indeed, the hearing, the sight and the heart - about all those [one] will be questioned.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَقْفُ", "translit": "ُfْqَt", "root": "قفو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عِلْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَصَرَ", "translit": "َrَṣَbْlٱَw", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلْفُؤَادَ", "translit": "َdāَwʾُfْlٱَw", "root": "فاد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَسْـُٔولًا", "translit": "āًlwُٔـْsَm", "root": "سال"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "وَلَا تَمْشِ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ مَرَحًاۖ إِنَّكَ لَن تَخْرِقَ ٱلْأَرْضَ وَلَن تَبْلُغَ ٱلْجِبَالَ طُولًا ", "translation": "And do not walk upon the earth exultantly. Indeed, you will never tear the earth [apart], and you will never reach the mountains in height.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَمْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْmَt", "root": "مشي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَرَحًا", "translit": "āًḥَrَm", "root": "مرح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَن", "translit": "nَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَخْرِقَ", "translit": "َqِrْhkَt", "root": "خرق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَن", "translit": "nَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَبْلُغَ", "translit": "َغُlْbَt", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْجِبَالَ", "translit": "َlāَbِjgْlٱ", "root": "جبل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "طُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُṭ", "root": "طول"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "كُلُّ ذَٰلِكَ كَانَ سَيِّئُهُۥ عِندَ رَبِّكَ مَكْرُوهًا ", "translation": "All that [i.e., the aforementioned] - its evil is ever, in the sight of your Lord, detested.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَيِّئُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُyʾِّyَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَكْرُوهًا", "translit": "āًhwُrْkَm", "root": "كره"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ مِمَّآ أَوْحَىٰٓ إِلَيْكَ رَبُّكَ مِنَ ٱلْحِكْمَةِۗ وَلَا تَجْعَلْ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَٰهًا ءَاخَرَ فَتُلْقَىٰ فِى جَهَنَّمَ مَلُومًا مَّدْحُورًا ", "translation": "That is from what your Lord has revealed to you, [O Muḥammad], of wisdom. And, [O mankind], do not make [as equal] with Allāh another deity, lest you be thrown into Hell, blamed and banished.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِمَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَوْحَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْحِكْمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmْkِḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجْعَلْ", "translit": "ْlَʿعْjgَt", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَٰهًا", "translit": "āًhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ءَاخَرَ", "translit": "َrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَتُلْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْlُtَf", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَلُومًا", "translit": "āًmwُlَm", "root": "لوم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَّدْحُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُḥْdَّm", "root": "دحر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "أَفَأَصْفَىٰكُمْ رَبُّكُم بِٱلْبَنِينَ وَٱتَّخَذَ مِنَ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ إِنَٰثًاۚ إِنَّكُمْ لَتَقُولُونَ قَوْلًا عَظِيمًا ", "translation": "Then, has your Lord chosen you for [having] sons and taken [i.e., adopted] from among the angels daughters? Indeed, you say a grave saying.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَأَصْفَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَfْṣَaʾَfَaʾ", "root": "صفو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبُّكُم", "translit": "mُkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnَbْlٱِb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱتَّخَذَ", "translit": "َhdَhkَّtٱَw", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَٰثًا", "translit": "āًhtَٰnِiʾ", "root": "انث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَتَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَtَl", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَوْلًا", "translit": "āًlْwَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَظِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "وَلَقَدْ صَرَّفْنَا فِى هَٰذَا ٱلْقُرْءَانِ لِيَذَّكَّرُوا۟ وَمَا يَزِيدُهُمْ إِلَّا نُفُورًا ", "translation": "And We have certainly diversified [the contents] in this Qur’ān that they [i.e., mankind] may be reminded, but it does not increase them [i.e., the disbelievers] except in aversion.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَرَّفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَّrَṣ", "root": "صرف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِيَذَّكَّرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَّkَّhdَyِl", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَزِيدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdyِzَy", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "نُفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfُn", "root": "نفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "قُل لَّوْ كَانَ مَعَهُۥٓ ءَالِهَةٌ كَمَا يَقُولُونَ إِذًا لَّٱبْتَغَوْا۟ إِلَىٰ ذِى ٱلْعَرْشِ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Say, [O Muḥammad], \"If there had been with Him [other] gods, as they say, then they [each] would have sought to the Owner of the Throne a way.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّوْ", "translit": "ْwَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَعَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَالِهَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَhِlāَء", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّٱبْتَغَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَغَtْbٱَّl", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذِى", "translit": "àِhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعَرْشِ", "translit": "ِhsْrَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عرش"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "سُبْحَٰنَهُۥ وَتَعَٰلَىٰ عَمَّا يَقُولُونَ عُلُوًّا كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "Exalted is He and high above what they say by great sublimity.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَتَعَٰلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَٰʿعَtَw", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عُلُوًّا", "translit": "āًّwُlُʿع", "root": "علو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "تُسَبِّحُ لَهُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ ٱلسَّبْعُ وَٱلْأَرْضُ وَمَن فِيهِنَّۚ وَإِن مِّن شَىْءٍ إِلَّا يُسَبِّحُ بِحَمْدِهِۦ وَلَٰكِن لَّا تَفْقَهُونَ تَسْبِيحَهُمْۗ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ حَلِيمًا غَفُورًا ", "translation": "The seven heavens and the earth and whatever is in them exalt Him. And there is not a thing except that it exalts [Allāh] by His praise, but you do not understand their [way of] exalting. Indeed, He is ever Forbearing[1] and Forgiving.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تُسَبِّحُ", "translit": "ُḥِّbَsُt", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُ", "translit": "ُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبْعُ", "translit": "ُʿعْbَّslٱ", "root": "سبع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِيهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُسَبِّحُ", "translit": "ُḥِّbَsُy", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِحَمْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْmَḥِb", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تَفْقَهُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhَqْfَt", "root": "فقه"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تَسْبِيحَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَḥyِbْsَt", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "حَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَḥ", "root": "حلم"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "غَفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "وَإِذَا قَرَأْتَ ٱلْقُرْءَانَ جَعَلْنَا بَيْنَكَ وَبَيْنَ ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ حِجَابًا مَّسْتُورًا ", "translation": "And when you recite the Qur’ān, We put between you and those who do not believe in the Hereafter a concealed partition.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَرَأْتَ", "translit": "َtْaʾَrَq", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَكَ", "translit": "َkَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَبَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَbَw", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱِb", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "حِجَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَjgِḥ", "root": "حجب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّسْتُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُtْsَّm", "root": "ستر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "وَجَعَلْنَا عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمْ أَكِنَّةً أَن يَفْقَهُوهُ وَفِىٓ ءَاذَانِهِمْ وَقْرًاۚ وَإِذَا ذَكَرْتَ رَبَّكَ فِى ٱلْقُرْءَانِ وَحْدَهُۥ وَلَّوْا۟ عَلَىٰٓ أَدْبَٰرِهِمْ نُفُورًا ", "translation": "And We have placed over their hearts coverings, lest they understand it, and in their ears deafness. And when you mention your Lord alone in the Qur’ān, they turn back in aversion.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قُلُوبِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَكِنَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّnِkَaʾ", "root": "كنن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَفْقَهُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُhَqْfَy", "root": "فقه"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَفِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِfَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ءَاذَانِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnāَhdāَء", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَقْرًا", "translit": "āًrْqَw", "root": "وقر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَكَرْتَ", "translit": "َtْrَkَhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَحْدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdْḥَw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَّوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَّlَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَدْبَٰرِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrَٰbْdَaʾ", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "نُفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfُn", "root": "نفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "نَّحْنُ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا يَسْتَمِعُونَ بِهِۦٓ إِذْ يَسْتَمِعُونَ إِلَيْكَ وَإِذْ هُمْ نَجْوَىٰٓ إِذْ يَقُولُ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ إِن تَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا رَجُلًا مَّسْحُورًا ", "translation": "We are most knowing of how they listen to it when they listen to you and [of] when they are in private conversation, when the wrongdoers say, \"You follow not but a man affected by magic.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "نَّحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَّn", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَسْتَمِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِmَtْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَسْتَمِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِmَtْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَجْوَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَwْjgَn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تَتَّبِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِbَّtَt", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "رَجُلًا", "translit": "āًlُjgَr", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَّسْحُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُḥْsَّm", "root": "سحر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "ٱنظُرْ كَيْفَ ضَرَبُوا۟ لَكَ ٱلْأَمْثَالَ فَضَلُّوا۟ فَلَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Look how they strike for you comparisons;[1] but they have strayed, so they cannot [find] a way.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱنظُرْ", "translit": "ْrُẓnٱ", "root": "نظر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَk", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ضَرَبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَrَḍ", "root": "ضرب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْثَالَ", "translit": "َlāَhtْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَضَلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlَḍَf", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَسْتَطِيعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعyِṭَtْsَy", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "وَقَالُوٓا۟ أَءِذَا كُنَّا عِظَٰمًا وَرُفَٰتًا أَءِنَّا لَمَبْعُوثُونَ خَلْقًا جَدِيدًا ", "translation": "And they say, \"When we are bones and crumbled particles, will we [truly] be resurrected as a new creation?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَءِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عِظَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰẓِʿع", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرُفَٰتًا", "translit": "āًtَٰfُrَw", "root": "رفت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَءِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَمَبْعُوثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtwُʿعْbَmَl", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَلْقًا", "translit": "āًqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَjg", "root": "جدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "قُلْ كُونُوا۟ حِجَارَةً أَوْ حَدِيدًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Be you stones or iron[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حِجَارَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrāَjgِḥ", "root": "حجر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَḥ", "root": "حدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "أَوْ خَلْقًا مِّمَّا يَكْبُرُ فِى صُدُورِكُمْۚ فَسَيَقُولُونَ مَن يُعِيدُنَاۖ قُلِ ٱلَّذِى فَطَرَكُمْ أَوَّلَ مَرَّةٍۚ فَسَيُنْغِضُونَ إِلَيْكَ رُءُوسَهُمْ وَيَقُولُونَ مَتَىٰ هُوَۖ قُلْ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُونَ قَرِيبًا ", "translation": "Or [any] creation of that which is great[1] within your breasts.\" And they will say, \"Who will restore us?\" Say, \"He who brought you forth the first time.\" Then they will nod their heads toward you[2] and say, \"When is that?\" Say, \"Perhaps it will be soon -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلْقًا", "translit": "āًqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَكْبُرُ", "translit": "ُrُbْkَy", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "صُدُورِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِrwُdُṣ", "root": "صدر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَسَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَsَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُعِيدُنَا", "translit": "āَnُdyِʿعُy", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَطَرَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَrَṭَf", "root": "فطر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَوَّلَ", "translit": "َlَّwَaʾ", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَرَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّrَm", "root": "مرر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَسَيُنْغِضُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḍِغْnُyَsَf", "root": "نغض"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "رُءُوسَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَswُءُr", "root": "راس"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "قَرِيبًا", "translit": "āًbyِrَq", "root": "قرب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "يَوْمَ يَدْعُوكُمْ فَتَسْتَجِيبُونَ بِحَمْدِهِۦ وَتَظُنُّونَ إِن لَّبِثْتُمْ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "On the Day He will call you and you will respond with praise of Him and think that you had not remained [in the world] except for a little.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَدْعُوكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkwُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَسْتَجِيبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbyِjgَtْsَtَf", "root": "جوب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِحَمْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْmَḥِb", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَتَظُنُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّnُẓَtَw", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَّبِثْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْhtِbَّl", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "وَقُل لِّعِبَادِى يَقُولُوا۟ ٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُۚ إِنَّ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ يَنزَغُ بَيْنَهُمْۚ إِنَّ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ كَانَ لِلْإِنسَٰنِ عَدُوًّا مُّبِينًا ", "translation": "And tell My servants to say that which is best. Indeed, Satan induces [dissension] among them. Indeed Satan is ever, to mankind, a clear enemy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِّعِبَادِى", "translit": "àِdāَbِʿعِّl", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَقُولُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُ", "translit": "ُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَنزَغُ", "translit": "ُغَznَy", "root": "نزغ"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِلْإِنسَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰsnِiʾْlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَدُوًّا", "translit": "āًّwُdَʿع", "root": "عدو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مُّبِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "رَّبُّكُمْ أَعْلَمُ بِكُمْۖ إِن يَشَأْ يَرْحَمْكُمْ أَوْ إِن يَشَأْ يُعَذِّبْكُمْۚ وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "Your Lord is most knowing of you. If He wills, He will have mercy upon you; or if He wills, He will punish you. And We have not sent you, [O Muḥammad], over them as a manager.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَشَأْ", "translit": "ْaʾَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَرْحَمْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْmَḥْrَy", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَشَأْ", "translit": "ْaʾَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يُعَذِّبْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْbِّhdَʿعُy", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "وَرَبُّكَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۗ وَلَقَدْ فَضَّلْنَا بَعْضَ ٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ عَلَىٰ بَعْضٍۖ وَءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ زَبُورًا ", "translation": "And your Lord is most knowing of whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And We have made some of the prophets exceed others [in various ways], and to David We gave the book [of Psalms].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَرَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَrَw", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمَن", "translit": "nَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَضَّلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَّḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَعْضَ", "translit": "َḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ", "translit": "َnۦِّyِbَّnlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "دَاوُۥدَ", "translit": "َdۥُwāَd", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "زَبُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُbَz", "root": "زبر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "قُلِ ٱدْعُوا۟ ٱلَّذِينَ زَعَمْتُم مِّن دُونِهِۦ فَلَا يَمْلِكُونَ كَشْفَ ٱلضُّرِّ عَنكُمْ وَلَا تَحْوِيلًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Invoke those you have claimed [as gods] besides Him, for they do not possess the [ability for] removal of adversity from you or [for its] transfer [to someone else].\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdٱ", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "زَعَمْتُم", "translit": "mُtْmَʿعَz", "root": "زعم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَمْلِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِlْmَy", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَشْفَ", "translit": "َfْhsَk", "root": "كشف"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلضُّرِّ", "translit": "ِّrُّḍlٱ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَنكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُknَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَحْوِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِwْḥَt", "root": "حول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ يَبْتَغُونَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِمُ ٱلْوَسِيلَةَ أَيُّهُمْ أَقْرَبُ وَيَرْجُونَ رَحْمَتَهُۥ وَيَخَافُونَ عَذَابَهُۥٓۚ إِنَّ عَذَابَ رَبِّكَ كَانَ مَحْذُورًا ", "translation": "Those whom they invoke[1] seek means of access to their Lord, [striving as to] which of them would be nearest, and they hope for His mercy and fear His punishment. Indeed, the punishment of your Lord is ever feared.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعْdَy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَبْتَغُونَ", "translit": "َnwُغَtْbَy", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْوَسِيلَةَ", "translit": "َẗَlyِsَwْlٱ", "root": "وسل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَيُّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُّyَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَقْرَبُ", "translit": "ُbَrْqَaʾ", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَيَرْجُونَ", "translit": "َnwُjgْrَyَw", "root": "رجو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَحْمَتَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَtَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيَخَافُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfāَhkَyَw", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَذَابَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَذَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَحْذُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُhdْḥَm", "root": "حذر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "وَإِن مِّن قَرْيَةٍ إِلَّا نَحْنُ مُهْلِكُوهَا قَبْلَ يَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ أَوْ مُعَذِّبُوهَا عَذَابًا شَدِيدًاۚ كَانَ ذَٰلِكَ فِى ٱلْكِتَٰبِ مَسْطُورًا ", "translation": "And there is no city but that We will destroy it[1] before the Day of Resurrection or punish it with a severe punishment. That has ever been in the Register[2] inscribed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُهْلِكُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُkِlْhُm", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُعَذِّبُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُbِّhdَʿعُm", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَسْطُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُṭْsَm", "root": "سطر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "وَمَا مَنَعَنَآ أَن نُّرْسِلَ بِٱلْءَايَٰتِ إِلَّآ أَن كَذَّبَ بِهَا ٱلْأَوَّلُونَۚ وَءَاتَيْنَا ثَمُودَ ٱلنَّاقَةَ مُبْصِرَةً فَظَلَمُوا۟ بِهَاۚ وَمَا نُرْسِلُ بِٱلْءَايَٰتِ إِلَّا تَخْوِيفًا ", "translation": "And nothing has prevented Us from sending signs [i.e., miracles] except that the former peoples denied them. And We gave Thamūd the she-camel as a visible sign, but they wronged her. And We send not the signs except as a warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنَعَنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnَʿعَnَm", "root": "منع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نُّرْسِلَ", "translit": "َlِsْrُّn", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَءْlٱِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوَّلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَّwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ثَمُودَ", "translit": "َdwُmَht", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاقَةَ", "translit": "َẗَqāَّnlٱ", "root": "نوق"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مُبْصِرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrِṣْbُm", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَظَلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlَẓَf", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "نُرْسِلُ", "translit": "ُlِsْrُn", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَءْlٱِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَخْوِيفًا", "translit": "āًfyِwْhkَt", "root": "خوف"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "وَإِذْ قُلْنَا لَكَ إِنَّ رَبَّكَ أَحَاطَ بِٱلنَّاسِۚ وَمَا جَعَلْنَا ٱلرُّءْيَا ٱلَّتِىٓ أَرَيْنَٰكَ إِلَّا فِتْنَةً لِّلنَّاسِ وَٱلشَّجَرَةَ ٱلْمَلْعُونَةَ فِى ٱلْقُرْءَانِۚ وَنُخَوِّفُهُمْ فَمَا يَزِيدُهُمْ إِلَّا طُغْيَٰنًا كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "And [remember, O Muḥammad], when We told you, \"Indeed, your Lord has encompassed the people.\"[1] And We did not make the sight which We showed you[2] except as a trial for the people, as was the accursed tree [mentioned] in the Qur’ān. And We threaten [i.e., warn] them, but it increases them not except in great transgression.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَحَاطَ", "translit": "َṭāَḥَaʾ", "root": "حوط"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱِb", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلرُّءْيَا", "translit": "āَyْءُّrlٱ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِtَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَرَيْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْyَrَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِتْنَةً", "translit": "ًẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِّلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِّl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّجَرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrَjgَّhslٱَw", "root": "شجر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلْعُونَةَ", "translit": "َẗَnwُʿعْlَmْlٱ", "root": "لعن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَنُخَوِّفُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُfِّwَhkُnَw", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "يَزِيدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdyِzَy", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "طُغْيَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰyْغُṭ", "root": "طغي"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "وَإِذْ قُلْنَا لِلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ ٱسْجُدُوا۟ لِءَادَمَ فَسَجَدُوٓا۟ إِلَّآ إِبْلِيسَ قَالَ ءَأَسْجُدُ لِمَنْ خَلَقْتَ طِينًا ", "translation": "And [mention] when We said to the angels, \"Prostrate to Adam,\" and they prostrated, except for Iblees.[1] He said, \"Should I prostrate to one You created from clay?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlِl", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱسْجُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُjgْsٱ", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِءَادَمَ", "translit": "َmَdāَءِl", "root": "ادم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَسَجَدُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdَjgَsَf", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِبْلِيسَ", "translit": "َsyِlْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ءَأَسْجُدُ", "translit": "ُdُjgْsَaʾَء", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خَلَقْتَ", "translit": "َtْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "طِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِṭ", "root": "طين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "قَالَ أَرَءَيْتَكَ هَٰذَا ٱلَّذِى كَرَّمْتَ عَلَىَّ لَئِنْ أَخَّرْتَنِ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ لَأَحْتَنِكَنَّ ذُرِّيَّتَهُۥٓ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "[Iblees] said, \"Do You see this one whom You have honored above me? If You delay me [i.e., my death] until the Day of Resurrection, I will surely destroy[1] his descendants, except for a few.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَءَيْتَكَ", "translit": "َkَtْyَءَrَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَرَّمْتَ", "translit": "َtْmَّrَk", "root": "كرم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَئِنْ", "translit": "ْnِyʾَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَخَّرْتَنِ", "translit": "ِnَtْrَّhkَaʾ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَأَحْتَنِكَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَkِnَtْḥَaʾَl", "root": "حنك"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ذُرِّيَّتَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَtَّyِّrُhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "قَالَ ٱذْهَبْ فَمَن تَبِعَكَ مِنْهُمْ فَإِنَّ جَهَنَّمَ جَزَآؤُكُمْ جَزَآءً مَّوْفُورًا ", "translation": "[Allāh] said, \"Go, for whoever of them follows you, indeed Hell will be the recompense of [all of] you - an ample recompense.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱذْهَبْ", "translit": "ْbَhْhdٱ", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَبِعَكَ", "translit": "َkَʿعِbَt", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَزَآؤُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُwʾٓāَzَjg", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَزَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَzَjg", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَّوْفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfْwَّm", "root": "وفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "وَٱسْتَفْزِزْ مَنِ ٱسْتَطَعْتَ مِنْهُم بِصَوْتِكَ وَأَجْلِبْ عَلَيْهِم بِخَيْلِكَ وَرَجِلِكَ وَشَارِكْهُمْ فِى ٱلْأَمْوَٰلِ وَٱلْأَوْلَٰدِ وَعِدْهُمْۚ وَمَا يَعِدُهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ إِلَّا غُرُورًا ", "translation": "And incite [to senselessness] whoever you can among them with your voice and assault them with your horses and foot soldiers and become a partner in their wealth and their children and promise them.\" But Satan does not promise them except delusion.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَفْزِزْ", "translit": "ْzِzْfَtْsٱَw", "root": "فزز"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنِ", "translit": "ِnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱسْتَطَعْتَ", "translit": "َtْʿعَṭَtْsٱ", "root": "طوع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِصَوْتِكَ", "translit": "َkِtْwَṣِb", "root": "صوت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَجْلِبْ", "translit": "ْbِlْjgَaʾَw", "root": "جلب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِخَيْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْyَhkِb", "root": "خيل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَرَجِلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlِjgَrَw", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَشَارِكْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْkِrāَhsَw", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْوَٰلِ", "translit": "ِlَٰwْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَوْلَٰدِ", "translit": "ِdَٰlْwَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَعِدْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْdِʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَعِدُهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhُdِʿعَy", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "غُرُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُrُغ", "root": "غرر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "إِنَّ عِبَادِى لَيْسَ لَكَ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطَٰنٌۚ وَكَفَىٰ بِرَبِّكَ وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "Indeed, over My [believing] servants there is for you no authority. And sufficient is your Lord as Disposer of affairs.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عِبَادِى", "translit": "àِdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنٌ", "translit": "ٌnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَkَw", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِرَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "رَّبُّكُمُ ٱلَّذِى يُزْجِى لَكُمُ ٱلْفُلْكَ فِى ٱلْبَحْرِ لِتَبْتَغُوا۟ مِن فَضْلِهِۦٓۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ بِكُمْ رَحِيمًا ", "translation": "It is your Lord who drives the ship for you through the sea that you may seek of His bounty. Indeed, He is ever, to you, Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّبُّكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkُّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُزْجِى", "translit": "àِjgْzُy", "root": "زجو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْفُلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlُfْlٱ", "root": "فلك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْبَحْرِ", "translit": "ِrْḥَbْlٱ", "root": "بحر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِتَبْتَغُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُغَtْbَtِl", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَضْلِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَحِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِḥَr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "وَإِذَا مَسَّكُمُ ٱلضُّرُّ فِى ٱلْبَحْرِ ضَلَّ مَن تَدْعُونَ إِلَّآ إِيَّاهُۖ فَلَمَّا نَجَّىٰكُمْ إِلَى ٱلْبَرِّ أَعْرَضْتُمْۚ وَكَانَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ كَفُورًا ", "translation": "And when adversity touches you at sea, lost are [all] those you invoke except for Him. But when He delivers you to the land, you turn away [from Him]. And ever is man ungrateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَسَّكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَّsَm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلضُّرُّ", "translit": "ُّrُّḍlٱ", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْبَحْرِ", "translit": "ِrْḥَbْlٱ", "root": "بحر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعْdَt", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِيَّاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَّyِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَجَّىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَّjgَn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَbْlٱ", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَعْرَضْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْḍَrْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عرض"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "كَفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfَk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "أَفَأَمِنتُمْ أَن يَخْسِفَ بِكُمْ جَانِبَ ٱلْبَرِّ أَوْ يُرْسِلَ عَلَيْكُمْ حَاصِبًا ثُمَّ لَا تَجِدُوا۟ لَكُمْ وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "Then do you feel secure that [instead] He will not cause a part of the land to swallow you or send against you a storm of stones? Then you would not find for yourselves an advocate.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَأَمِنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnِmَaʾَfَaʾ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَخْسِفَ", "translit": "َfِsْhkَy", "root": "خسف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَانِبَ", "translit": "َbِnāَjg", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَbْlٱ", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُرْسِلَ", "translit": "َlِsْrُy", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَاصِبًا", "translit": "āًbِṣāَḥ", "root": "حصب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَجِدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "أَمْ أَمِنتُمْ أَن يُعِيدَكُمْ فِيهِ تَارَةً أُخْرَىٰ فَيُرْسِلَ عَلَيْكُمْ قَاصِفًا مِّنَ ٱلرِّيحِ فَيُغْرِقَكُم بِمَا كَفَرْتُمْۙ ثُمَّ لَا تَجِدُوا۟ لَكُمْ عَلَيْنَا بِهِۦ تَبِيعًا ", "translation": "Or do you feel secure that He will not send you back into it [i.e., the sea] another time and send upon you a hurricane of wind and drown you for what you denied?[1] Then you would not find for yourselves against Us an avenger.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمِنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnِmَaʾ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُعِيدَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَdyِʿعُy", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَارَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrāَt", "root": "تور"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَيُرْسِلَ", "translit": "َlِsْrُyَf", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "قَاصِفًا", "translit": "āًfِṣāَq", "root": "قصف"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلرِّيحِ", "translit": "ِḥyِّrlٱ", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَيُغْرِقَكُم", "translit": "mُkَqِrْغُyَf", "root": "غرق"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "كَفَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَجِدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "تَبِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِbَt", "root": "تبع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 70, "text": "وَلَقَدْ كَرَّمْنَا بَنِىٓ ءَادَمَ وَحَمَلْنَٰهُمْ فِى ٱلْبَرِّ وَٱلْبَحْرِ وَرَزَقْنَٰهُم مِّنَ ٱلطَّيِّبَٰتِ وَفَضَّلْنَٰهُمْ عَلَىٰ كَثِيرٍ مِّمَّنْ خَلَقْنَا تَفْضِيلًا ", "translation": "And We have certainly honored the children of Adam and carried them on the land and sea and provided for them of the good things and preferred them over much of what We have created, with [definite] preference.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَرَّمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَّrَk", "root": "كرم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَادَمَ", "translit": "َmَdāَء", "root": "ادم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَحَمَلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَmَḥَw", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِّ", "translit": "ِّrَbْlٱ", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَحْرِ", "translit": "ِrْḥَbْlٱَw", "root": "بحر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَرَزَقْنَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰnْqَzَrَw", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلطَّيِّبَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰbِّyَّṭlٱ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَفَضَّلْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْlَّḍَfَw", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَثِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِّمَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّmِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تَفْضِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِḍْfَt", "root": "فضل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 71, "text": "يَوْمَ نَدْعُوا۟ كُلَّ أُنَاسٍۭ بِإِمَٰمِهِمْۖ فَمَنْ أُوتِىَ كِتَٰبَهُۥ بِيَمِينِهِۦ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ يَقْرَءُونَ كِتَٰبَهُمْ وَلَا يُظْلَمُونَ فَتِيلًا ", "translation": "[Mention, O Muḥammad], the Day We will call forth every people with their record [of deeds].[1] Then whoever is given his record in his right hand - those will read their records, and injustice will not be done to them, [even] as much as a thread [inside the date seed].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdَn", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُنَاسٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭsāَnُaʾ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِإِمَٰمِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِmَٰmِiʾِb", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُوتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كِتَٰبَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِيَمِينِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnyِmَyِb", "root": "يمن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَقْرَءُونَ", "translit": "َnwُءَrْqَy", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كِتَٰبَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُظْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْẓُy", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَتِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِtَf", "root": "فتل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 72, "text": "وَمَن كَانَ فِى هَٰذِهِۦٓ أَعْمَىٰ فَهُوَ فِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ أَعْمَىٰ وَأَضَلُّ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "And whoever is blind[1] in this [life] will be blind in the Hereafter and more astray in way.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَٰذِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْمَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَعْمَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَضَلُّ", "translit": "ُّlَḍَaʾَw", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 73, "text": "وَإِن كَادُوا۟ لَيَفْتِنُونَكَ عَنِ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْكَ لِتَفْتَرِىَ عَلَيْنَا غَيْرَهُۥۖ وَإِذًا لَّٱتَّخَذُوكَ خَلِيلًا ", "translation": "And indeed, they were about to tempt you away from that which We revealed to you in order to [make] you invent about Us something else; and then they would have taken you as a friend.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَادُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdāَk", "root": "كود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَيَفْتِنُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُnِtْfَyَl", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِتَفْتَرِىَ", "translit": "َàِrَtْfَtِl", "root": "فري"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "غَيْرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَإِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّٱتَّخَذُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُhdَhkَّtٱَّl", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَhk", "root": "خلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 74, "text": "وَلَوْلَآ أَن ثَبَّتْنَٰكَ لَقَدْ كِدتَّ تَرْكَنُ إِلَيْهِمْ شَيْـًٔا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "And if We had not strengthened you, you would have almost inclined to them a little.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ثَبَّتْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْtَّbَht", "root": "ثبت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كِدتَّ", "translit": "َّtdِk", "root": "كود"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَرْكَنُ", "translit": "ُnَkْrَt", "root": "ركن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 75, "text": "إِذًا لَّأَذَقْنَٰكَ ضِعْفَ ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ وَضِعْفَ ٱلْمَمَاتِ ثُمَّ لَا تَجِدُ لَكَ عَلَيْنَا نَصِيرًا ", "translation": "Then [if you had], We would have made you taste double [punishment in] life and double [after] death. Then you would not find for yourself against Us a helper.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّأَذَقْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْqَhdَaʾَّl", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ضِعْفَ", "translit": "َfْʿعِḍ", "root": "ضعف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَضِعْفَ", "translit": "َfْʿعِḍَw", "root": "ضعف"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمَمَاتِ", "translit": "ِtāَmَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجِدُ", "translit": "ُdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 76, "text": "وَإِن كَادُوا۟ لَيَسْتَفِزُّونَكَ مِنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ لِيُخْرِجُوكَ مِنْهَاۖ وَإِذًا لَّا يَلْبَثُونَ خِلَٰفَكَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "And indeed, they were about to provoke [i.e., drive] you from the land [i.e., Makkah] to evict you therefrom. And then [when they do], they will not remain [there] after you, except for a little.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَادُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdāَk", "root": "كود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَيَسْتَفِزُّونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُّzِfَtْsَyَl", "root": "فزز"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِيُخْرِجُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُjgِrْhkُyِl", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَلْبَثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtَbْlَy", "root": "لبث"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خِلَٰفَكَ", "translit": "َkَfَٰlِhk", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 77, "text": "سُنَّةَ مَن قَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا قَبْلَكَ مِن رُّسُلِنَاۖ وَلَا تَجِدُ لِسُنَّتِنَا تَحْوِيلًا ", "translation": "[That is Our] established way for those We had sent before you of Our messengers; and you will not find in Our way any alteration.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سُنَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّnُs", "root": "سنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رُّسُلِنَا", "translit": "āَnِlُsُّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجِدُ", "translit": "ُdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِسُنَّتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَّnُsِl", "root": "سنن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَحْوِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِwْḥَt", "root": "حول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 78, "text": "أَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ لِدُلُوكِ ٱلشَّمْسِ إِلَىٰ غَسَقِ ٱلَّيْلِ وَقُرْءَانَ ٱلْفَجْرِۖ إِنَّ قُرْءَانَ ٱلْفَجْرِ كَانَ مَشْهُودًا ", "translation": "Establish prayer at the decline of the sun [from its meridian] until the darkness of the night[1] and [also] the Qur’ān [i.e., recitation] of dawn.[2] Indeed, the recitation of dawn is ever witnessed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَقِمِ", "translit": "ِmِqَaʾ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِدُلُوكِ", "translit": "ِkwُlُdِl", "root": "دلك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسِ", "translit": "ِsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "غَسَقِ", "translit": "ِqَsَغ", "root": "غسق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqَw", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْفَجْرِ", "translit": "ِrْjgَfْlٱ", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُq", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْفَجْرِ", "translit": "ِrْjgَfْlٱ", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَشْهُودًا", "translit": "āًdwُhْhsَm", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 79, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلَّيْلِ فَتَهَجَّدْ بِهِۦ نَافِلَةً لَّكَ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُودًا ", "translation": "And from [part of] the night, pray[1] with it [i.e., recitation of the Qur’ān] as additional [worship] for you; it is expected that[2] your Lord will resurrect you to a praised station.[3]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَهَجَّدْ", "translit": "ْdَّjgَhَtَf", "root": "هجد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَافِلَةً", "translit": "ًẗَlِfāَn", "root": "نفل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَsَʿع", "root": "عسي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَبْعَثَكَ", "translit": "َkَhtَʿعْbَy", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَقَامًا", "translit": "āًmāَqَm", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّحْمُودًا", "translit": "āًdwُmْḥَّm", "root": "حمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 80, "text": "وَقُل رَّبِّ أَدْخِلْنِى مُدْخَلَ صِدْقٍ وَأَخْرِجْنِى مُخْرَجَ صِدْقٍ وَٱجْعَل لِّى مِن لَّدُنكَ سُلْطَٰنًا نَّصِيرًا ", "translation": "And say, \"My Lord, cause me to enter a sound entrance[1] and to exit a sound exit[2] and grant me from Yourself a supporting authority.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُل", "translit": "lُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّبِّ", "translit": "ِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَدْخِلْنِى", "translit": "àِnْlِhkْdَaʾ", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُدْخَلَ", "translit": "َlَhkْdُm", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "صِدْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَخْرِجْنِى", "translit": "àِnْjgِrْhkَaʾَw", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُخْرَجَ", "translit": "َjgَrْhkُm", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صِدْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱجْعَل", "translit": "lَʿعْjgٱَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لِّى", "translit": "àِّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّدُنكَ", "translit": "َknُdَّl", "root": "لدن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نَّصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَّn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 81, "text": "وَقُلْ جَآءَ ٱلْحَقُّ وَزَهَقَ ٱلْبَٰطِلُۚ إِنَّ ٱلْبَٰطِلَ كَانَ زَهُوقًا ", "translation": "And say, \"Truth has come, and falsehood has departed. Indeed is falsehood, [by nature], ever bound to depart.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُلْ", "translit": "ْlُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَزَهَقَ", "translit": "َqَhَzَw", "root": "زهق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْبَٰطِلُ", "translit": "ُlِṭَٰbْlٱ", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْبَٰطِلَ", "translit": "َlِṭَٰbْlٱ", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "زَهُوقًا", "translit": "āًqwُhَz", "root": "زهق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 82, "text": "وَنُنَزِّلُ مِنَ ٱلْقُرْءَانِ مَا هُوَ شِفَآءٌ وَرَحْمَةٌ لِّلْمُؤْمِنِينَۙ وَلَا يَزِيدُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ إِلَّا خَسَارًا ", "translation": "And We send down of the Qur’ān that which is healing and mercy for the believers, but it does not increase the wrongdoers except in loss.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنُنَزِّلُ", "translit": "ُlِّzَnُnَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شِفَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَfِhs", "root": "شفي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَرَحْمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmْḥَrَw", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِّلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlِّl", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَزِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِzَy", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَسَارًا", "translit": "āًrāَsَhk", "root": "خسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 83, "text": "وَإِذَآ أَنْعَمْنَا عَلَى ٱلْإِنسَٰنِ أَعْرَضَ وَنَـَٔا بِجَانِبِهِۦۖ وَإِذَا مَسَّهُ ٱلشَّرُّ كَانَ يَـُٔوسًا ", "translation": "And when We bestow favor upon man [i.e., the disbeliever], he turns away and distances himself; and when evil touches him, he is ever despairing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنْعَمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَʿعْnَaʾ", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْرَضَ", "translit": "َḍَrْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عرض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَنَـَٔا", "translit": "āَٔـَnَw", "root": "ناي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِجَانِبِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِbِnāَjgِb", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَسَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّsَm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلشَّرُّ", "translit": "ُّrَّhslٱ", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَـُٔوسًا", "translit": "āًswُٔـَy", "root": "ياس"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 84, "text": "قُلْ كُلٌّ يَعْمَلُ عَلَىٰ شَاكِلَتِهِۦ فَرَبُّكُمْ أَعْلَمُ بِمَنْ هُوَ أَهْدَىٰ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Each works according to his manner, but your Lord is most knowing of who is best guided in way.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُلٌّ", "translit": "ٌّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَعْمَلُ", "translit": "ُlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَاكِلَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَlِkāَhs", "root": "شكل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَرَبُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّbَrَf", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَهْدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdْhَaʾ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 85, "text": "وَيَسْـَٔلُونَكَ عَنِ ٱلرُّوحِۖ قُلِ ٱلرُّوحُ مِنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّى وَمَآ أُوتِيتُم مِّنَ ٱلْعِلْمِ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "And they ask you, [O Muḥammad], about the soul. Say, \"The soul is of the affair [i.e., concern] of my Lord. And you [i.e., mankind] have not been given of knowledge except a little.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَسْـَٔلُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُlَٔـْsَyَw", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرُّوحِ", "translit": "ِḥwُّrlٱ", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلرُّوحُ", "translit": "ُḥwُّrlٱ", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أُوتِيتُم", "translit": "mُtyِtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 86, "text": "وَلَئِن شِئْنَا لَنَذْهَبَنَّ بِٱلَّذِىٓ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْكَ ثُمَّ لَا تَجِدُ لَكَ بِهِۦ عَلَيْنَا وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "And if We willed, We could surely do away with that which We revealed to you. Then you would not find for yourself concerning it an advocate against Us.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَئِن", "translit": "nِyʾَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شِئْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyʾِhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَنَذْهَبَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَbَhْhdَnَl", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجِدُ", "translit": "ُdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 87, "text": "إِلَّا رَحْمَةً مِّن رَّبِّكَۚ إِنَّ فَضْلَهُۥ كَانَ عَلَيْكَ كَبِيرًا ", "translation": "Except [We have left it with you] as a mercy from your Lord. Indeed, His favor upon you has ever been great.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَحْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَضْلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَبِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِbَk", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 88, "text": "قُل لَّئِنِ ٱجْتَمَعَتِ ٱلْإِنسُ وَٱلْجِنُّ عَلَىٰٓ أَن يَأْتُوا۟ بِمِثْلِ هَٰذَا ٱلْقُرْءَانِ لَا يَأْتُونَ بِمِثْلِهِۦ وَلَوْ كَانَ بَعْضُهُمْ لِبَعْضٍ ظَهِيرًا ", "translation": "Say, \"If mankind and the jinn gathered in order to produce the like of this Qur’ān, they could not produce the like of it, even if they were to each other assistants.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّئِنِ", "translit": "ِnِyʾَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱجْتَمَعَتِ", "translit": "ِtَʿعَmَtْjgٱ", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسُ", "translit": "ُsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْجِنُّ", "translit": "ُّnِjgْlٱَw", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَأْتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِمِثْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhtِmِb", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَأْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِمِثْلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlْhtِmِb", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بَعْضُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُḍْʿعَb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِl", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ظَهِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhَẓ", "root": "ظهر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 89, "text": "وَلَقَدْ صَرَّفْنَا لِلنَّاسِ فِى هَٰذَا ٱلْقُرْءَانِ مِن كُلِّ مَثَلٍ فَأَبَىٰٓ أَكْثَرُ ٱلنَّاسِ إِلَّا كُفُورًا ", "translation": "And We have certainly diversified for the people in this Qur’ān from every [kind of] example, but most of the people refused except disbelief.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَرَّفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَّrَṣ", "root": "صرف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَثَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَhtَm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَأَبَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَbَaʾَf", "root": "ابي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَكْثَرُ", "translit": "ُrَhtْkَaʾ", "root": "كثر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كُفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfُk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 90, "text": "وَقَالُوا۟ لَن نُّؤْمِنَ لَكَ حَتَّىٰ تَفْجُرَ لَنَا مِنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ يَنۢبُوعًا ", "translation": "And they say, \"We will not believe you until you break open for us from the ground a spring", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَن", "translit": "nَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُّؤْمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmْwʾُّn", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَفْجُرَ", "translit": "َrُjgْfَt", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَنۢبُوعًا", "translit": "āًʿعwُbۢnَy", "root": "نبع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 91, "text": "أَوْ تَكُونَ لَكَ جَنَّةٌ مِّن نَّخِيلٍ وَعِنَبٍ فَتُفَجِّرَ ٱلْأَنْهَٰرَ خِلَٰلَهَا تَفْجِيرًا ", "translation": "Or [until] you have a garden of palm trees and grapes and make rivers gush forth within them in force [and abundance]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَt", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَنَّةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَّnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَّخِيلٍ", "translit": "ٍlyِhkَّn", "root": "نخل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَعِنَبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَnِʿعَw", "root": "عنب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَتُفَجِّرَ", "translit": "َrِّjgَfُtَf", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرَ", "translit": "َrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خِلَٰلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlَٰlِhk", "root": "خلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَفْجِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِjgْfَt", "root": "فجر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 92, "text": "أَوْ تُسْقِطَ ٱلسَّمَآءَ كَمَا زَعَمْتَ عَلَيْنَا كِسَفًا أَوْ تَأْتِىَ بِٱللَّهِ وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ قَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Or you make the heaven fall upon us in fragments as you have claimed or you bring Allāh and the angels before [us]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُسْقِطَ", "translit": "َṭِqْsُt", "root": "سقط"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "زَعَمْتَ", "translit": "َtْmَʿعَz", "root": "زعم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كِسَفًا", "translit": "āًfَsِk", "root": "كسف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَأْتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtْaʾَt", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱَw", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَq", "root": "قبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 93, "text": "أَوْ يَكُونَ لَكَ بَيْتٌ مِّن زُخْرُفٍ أَوْ تَرْقَىٰ فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ وَلَن نُّؤْمِنَ لِرُقِيِّكَ حَتَّىٰ تُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْنَا كِتَٰبًا نَّقْرَؤُهُۥۗ قُلْ سُبْحَانَ رَبِّى هَلْ كُنتُ إِلَّا بَشَرًا رَّسُولًا ", "translation": "Or you have a house of ornament [i.e., gold] or you ascend into the sky. And [even then], we will not believe in your ascension until you bring down to us a book we may read.\" Say, \"Exalted is my Lord! Was I ever but a human messenger?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيْتٌ", "translit": "ٌtْyَb", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "زُخْرُفٍ", "translit": "ٍfُrْhkُz", "root": "زخرف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَرْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْrَt", "root": "رقي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَن", "translit": "nَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نُّؤْمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmْwʾُّn", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِرُقِيِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّyِqُrِl", "root": "رقي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تُنَزِّلَ", "translit": "َlِّzَnُt", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "كِتَٰبًا", "translit": "āًbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "نَّقْرَؤُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُwʾَrْqَّn", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "سُبْحَانَ", "translit": "َnāَḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "رَبِّى", "translit": "àِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "كُنتُ", "translit": "ُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بَشَرًا", "translit": "āًrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "رَّسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 94, "text": "وَمَا مَنَعَ ٱلنَّاسَ أَن يُؤْمِنُوٓا۟ إِذْ جَآءَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ إِلَّآ أَن قَالُوٓا۟ أَبَعَثَ ٱللَّهُ بَشَرًا رَّسُولًا ", "translation": "And what prevented the people from believing when guidance came to them except that they said, \"Has Allāh sent a human messenger?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَnَm", "root": "منع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "جَآءَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَdُhْlٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَبَعَثَ", "translit": "َhtَʿعَbَaʾ", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَشَرًا", "translit": "āًrَhsَb", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "رَّسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 95, "text": "قُل لَّوْ كَانَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ مَلَٰٓئِكَةٌ يَمْشُونَ مُطْمَئِنِّينَ لَنَزَّلْنَا عَلَيْهِم مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَلَكًا رَّسُولًا ", "translation": "Say, \"If there were upon the earth angels walking securely,[1] We would have sent down to them from the heaven an angel [as a] messenger.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّوْ", "translit": "ْwَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَلَٰٓئِكَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَمْشُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhsْmَy", "root": "مشي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُطْمَئِنِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّnِyʾَmْṭُm", "root": "طمان"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَنَزَّلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَّzَnَl", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَلَكًا", "translit": "āًkَlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَّسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 96, "text": "قُلْ كَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ شَهِيدًۢا بَيْنِى وَبَيْنَكُمْۚ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ بِعِبَادِهِۦ خَبِيرًۢا بَصِيرًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Sufficient is Allāh as Witness between me and you. Indeed He is ever, concerning His servants, Aware and Seeing.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَk", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "شَهِيدًۢا", "translit": "āًۢdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنِى", "translit": "àِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَبَيْنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnْyَbَw", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِعِبَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَbِʿعِb", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَبِيرًۢا", "translit": "āًۢryِbَhk", "root": "خبر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَb", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 97, "text": "وَمَن يَهْدِ ٱللَّهُ فَهُوَ ٱلْمُهْتَدِۖ وَمَن يُضْلِلْ فَلَن تَجِدَ لَهُمْ أَوْلِيَآءَ مِن دُونِهِۦۖ وَنَحْشُرُهُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ عَلَىٰ وُجُوهِهِمْ عُمْيًا وَبُكْمًا وَصُمًّاۖ مَّأْوَىٰهُمْ جَهَنَّمُۖ كُلَّمَا خَبَتْ زِدْنَٰهُمْ سَعِيرًا ", "translation": "And whoever Allāh guides - he is the [rightly] guided; and whoever He sends astray[1] - you will never find for them protectors besides Him, and We will gather them on the Day of Resurrection [fallen] on their faces - blind, dumb and deaf. Their refuge is Hell; every time it subsides, We increase [for] them blazing fire.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَهْدِ", "translit": "ِdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمُهْتَدِ", "translit": "ِdَtْhُmْlٱ", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُضْلِلْ", "translit": "ْlِlْḍُy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَلَن", "translit": "nَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجِدَ", "translit": "َdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَوْلِيَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَyِlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَنَحْشُرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrُhsْḥَnَw", "root": "حشر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وُجُوهِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عُمْيًا", "translit": "āًyْmُʿع", "root": "عمي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَبُكْمًا", "translit": "āًmْkُbَw", "root": "بكم"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَصُمًّا", "translit": "āًّmُṣَw", "root": "صمم"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مَّأْوَىٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhٰàَwْaʾَّm", "root": "اوي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمُ", "translit": "ُmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "كُلَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "خَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَhk", "root": "خبو"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "زِدْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْdِz", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "سَعِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِʿعَs", "root": "سعر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 98, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ جَزَآؤُهُم بِأَنَّهُمْ كَفَرُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا وَقَالُوٓا۟ أَءِذَا كُنَّا عِظَٰمًا وَرُفَٰتًا أَءِنَّا لَمَبْعُوثُونَ خَلْقًا جَدِيدًا ", "translation": "That is their recompense because they disbelieved in Our verses and said, \"When we are bones and crumbled particles, will we [truly] be resurrected [in] a new creation?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَزَآؤُهُم", "translit": "mُhُwʾٓāَzَjg", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِأَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَءِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُنَّا", "translit": "āَّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِظَٰمًا", "translit": "āًmَٰẓِʿع", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَرُفَٰتًا", "translit": "āًtَٰfُrَw", "root": "رفت"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَءِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِءَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَمَبْعُوثُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhtwُʿعْbَmَl", "root": "بعث"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "خَلْقًا", "translit": "āًqْlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "جَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَjg", "root": "جدد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 99, "text": "أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا۟ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ قَادِرٌ عَلَىٰٓ أَن يَخْلُقَ مِثْلَهُمْ وَجَعَلَ لَهُمْ أَجَلًا لَّا رَيْبَ فِيهِ فَأَبَى ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ إِلَّا كُفُورًا ", "translation": "Do they not see that Allāh, who created the heavens and earth, is [the one] Able to create the likes of them? And He has appointed for them a term, about which there is no doubt. But the wrongdoers refuse except disbelief.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrَy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "قَادِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِdāَq", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَخْلُقَ", "translit": "َqُlْhkَy", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِثْلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlْhtِm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَجَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjgَw", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَجَلًا", "translit": "āًlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "رَيْبَ", "translit": "َbْyَr", "root": "ريب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فَأَبَى", "translit": "àَbَaʾَf", "root": "ابي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "كُفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfُk", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 100, "text": "قُل لَّوْ أَنتُمْ تَمْلِكُونَ خَزَآئِنَ رَحْمَةِ رَبِّىٓ إِذًا لَّأَمْسَكْتُمْ خَشْيَةَ ٱلْإِنفَاقِۚ وَكَانَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ قَتُورًا ", "translation": "Say [to them], \"If you possessed the depositories of the mercy of my Lord, then you would withhold out of fear of spending.\" And ever has man been stingy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُل", "translit": "lُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّوْ", "translit": "ْwَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَمْلِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِlْmَt", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَزَآئِنَ", "translit": "َnِyʾٓāَzَhk", "root": "خزن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَحْمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَّأَمْسَكْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْkَsْmَaʾَّl", "root": "مسك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَشْيَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyْhsَhk", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنفَاقِ", "translit": "ِqāَfnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَتُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُtَq", "root": "قتر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 101, "text": "وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَىٰ تِسْعَ ءَايَٰتٍۭ بَيِّنَٰتٍۖ فَسْـَٔلْ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ إِذْ جَآءَهُمْ فَقَالَ لَهُۥ فِرْعَوْنُ إِنِّى لَأَظُنُّكَ يَٰمُوسَىٰ مَسْحُورًا ", "translation": "And We had certainly given Moses nine evident signs, so ask the Children of Israel [about] when he came to them and Pharaoh said to him, \"Indeed I think, O Moses, that you are affected by magic.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تِسْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْsِt", "root": "تسع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَيِّنَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰnِّyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَسْـَٔلْ", "translit": "ْlَٔـْsَf", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "جَآءَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِرْعَوْنُ", "translit": "ُnْwَʿعْrِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَأَظُنُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّnُẓَaʾَl", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يَٰمُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُmَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَسْحُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُḥْsَm", "root": "سحر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 102, "text": "قَالَ لَقَدْ عَلِمْتَ مَآ أَنزَلَ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ إِلَّا رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ بَصَآئِرَ وَإِنِّى لَأَظُنُّكَ يَٰفِرْعَوْنُ مَثْبُورًا ", "translation": "[Moses] said, \"You have already known that none has sent down these [signs] except the Lord of the heavens and the earth as evidence, and indeed I think,[1] O Pharaoh, that you are destroyed.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلِمْتَ", "translit": "َtْmِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَصَآئِرَ", "translit": "َrِyʾٓāَṣَb", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَإِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَأَظُنُّكَ", "translit": "َkُّnُẓَaʾَl", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَٰفِرْعَوْنُ", "translit": "ُnْwَʿعْrِfَٰy", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَثْبُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُbْhtَm", "root": "ثبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 103, "text": "فَأَرَادَ أَن يَسْتَفِزَّهُم مِّنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ فَأَغْرَقْنَٰهُ وَمَن مَّعَهُۥ جَمِيعًا ", "translation": "So he intended to drive them from the land, but We drowned him and those with him all together.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَرَادَ", "translit": "َdāَrَaʾَf", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَسْتَفِزَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّzِfَtْsَy", "root": "فزز"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَغْرَقْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْqَrْغَaʾَf", "root": "غرق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَّعَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 104, "text": "وَقُلْنَا مِنۢ بَعْدِهِۦ لِبَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ ٱسْكُنُوا۟ ٱلْأَرْضَ فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ جِئْنَا بِكُمْ لَفِيفًا ", "translation": "And We said after him [i.e., Pharaoh] to the Children of Israel, \"Dwell in the land, and when there comes the promise [i.e., appointment] of the Hereafter, We will bring you forth in [one] gathering.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِبَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَbِl", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱسْكُنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnُkْsٱ", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "جِئْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyʾِjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَفِيفًا", "translit": "āًfyِfَl", "root": "لفف"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 105, "text": "وَبِٱلْحَقِّ أَنزَلْنَٰهُ وَبِٱلْحَقِّ نَزَلَۗ وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ إِلَّا مُبَشِّرًا وَنَذِيرًا ", "translation": "And with the truth We have sent it [i.e., the Qur’ān] down, and with the truth it has descended. And We have not sent you, [O Muḥammad], except as a bringer of good tidings and a warner.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَبِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِbَw", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَبِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِbَw", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَزَلَ", "translit": "َlَzَn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَٰكَ", "translit": "َkَٰnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُبَشِّرًا", "translit": "āًrِّhsَbُm", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَنَذِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhdَnَw", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 106, "text": "وَقُرْءَانًا فَرَقْنَٰهُ لِتَقْرَأَهُۥ عَلَى ٱلنَّاسِ عَلَىٰ مُكْثٍ وَنَزَّلْنَٰهُ تَنزِيلًا ", "translation": "And [it is] a Qur’ān which We have separated [by intervals] that you might recite it to the people over a prolonged period. And We have sent it down progressively.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُرْءَانًا", "translit": "āًnāَءْrُqَw", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَرَقْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْqَrَf", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِتَقْرَأَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَaʾَrْqَtِl", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُكْثٍ", "translit": "ٍhtْkُm", "root": "مكث"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَنَزَّلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَّzَnَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَنزِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِznَt", "root": "نزل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 107, "text": "قُلْ ءَامِنُوا۟ بِهِۦٓ أَوْ لَا تُؤْمِنُوٓا۟ۚ إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْعِلْمَ مِن قَبْلِهِۦٓ إِذَا يُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْ يَخِرُّونَ لِلْأَذْقَانِ سُجَّدًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Believe in it or do not believe.\" Indeed, those who were given knowledge before it[1] - when it is recited to them, they fall upon their faces in prostration,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُؤْمِنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnِmْwʾُt", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أُوتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمَ", "translit": "َmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قَبْلِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُy", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَخِرُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّrِhkَy", "root": "خرر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "لِلْأَذْقَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَqْhdَaʾْlِl", "root": "ذقن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "سُجَّدًا", "translit": "āًdَّjgُs", "root": "سجد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 108, "text": "وَيَقُولُونَ سُبْحَٰنَ رَبِّنَآ إِن كَانَ وَعْدُ رَبِّنَا لَمَفْعُولًا ", "translation": "And they say, \"Exalted is our Lord! Indeed, the promise of our Lord has been fulfilled.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعْدُ", "translit": "ُdْʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّنَا", "translit": "āَnِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَمَفْعُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُʿعْfَmَl", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 109, "text": "وَيَخِرُّونَ لِلْأَذْقَانِ يَبْكُونَ وَيَزِيدُهُمْ خُشُوعًا۩ ", "translation": "And they fall upon their faces weeping, and it [i.e., the Qur’ān] increases them in humble submission.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَخِرُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّrِhkَyَw", "root": "خرر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْأَذْقَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَqْhdَaʾْlِl", "root": "ذقن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَبْكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkْbَy", "root": "بكي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَزِيدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdyِzَyَw", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خُشُوعًا", "translit": "āًʿعwُhsُhk", "root": "خشع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 110, "text": "قُلِ ٱدْعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ أَوِ ٱدْعُوا۟ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنَۖ أَيًّا مَّا تَدْعُوا۟ فَلَهُ ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰۚ وَلَا تَجْهَرْ بِصَلَاتِكَ وَلَا تُخَافِتْ بِهَا وَٱبْتَغِ بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Say, \"Call upon Allāh or call upon the Most Merciful [ar-Raḥmān]. Whichever [name] you call - to Him belong the best names.\" And do not recite [too] loudly in your prayer or [too] quietly but seek between that an [intermediate] way.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdٱ", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوِ", "translit": "ِwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdٱ", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَيًّا", "translit": "āًّyَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُʿعْdَt", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَلَهُ", "translit": "ُhَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَmْsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَجْهَرْ", "translit": "ْrَhْjgَt", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِصَلَاتِكَ", "translit": "َkِtāَlَṣِb", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "تُخَافِتْ", "translit": "ْtِfāَhkُt", "root": "خفت"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱبْتَغِ", "translit": "ِغَtْbٱَw", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 111, "text": "وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّ مِّنَ ٱلذُّلِّۖ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًۢا ", "translation": "And say, \"Praise to Allāh, who has not taken a son and has had no partner in [His] dominion and has no [need of a] protector out of weakness; and glorify Him with [great] glorification.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقُلِ", "translit": "ِlُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمْدُ", "translit": "ُdْmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَتَّخِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِhkَّtَy", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَدًا", "translit": "āًdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "شَرِيكٌ", "translit": "ٌkyِrَhs", "root": "شرك"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْمُلْكِ", "translit": "ِkْlُmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَلِىٌّ", "translit": "ٌّàِlَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلذُّلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُّhdlٱ", "root": "ذلل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَكَبِّرْهُ", "translit": "ُhْrِّbَkَw", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "تَكْبِيرًۢا", "translit": "āًۢryِbْkَt", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"12": [{"arabic": "والشكر لا غير وقال تعالى {وجعلنا الليل والنهار آيتين فمحونا آية الليل وجعلنا آية النهار مبصرة لتبتغوا فضلا من ربكم ولتعلموا عدد السنين والحساب} وإنما الفضل المبتغى هو الثواب والمغفرة ونسأل الله حسن التوفيق لما يرضيه بيان أعداد الأوراد وترتيبها اعلم أن أوراد النهار سبعة فما بين طلوع الصبح إلى طلوع قرص الشمس ورد وما بين طلوع الشمس إلى الزوال وردان وما بين الزوال إلى وقت العصر وردان وما بين العصر إلى المغرب وردان والليل ينقسم إلى أربعة أوراد وردان من المغرب إلى وقت نوم الناس ووردان من النصف الأخير من الليل إلى طلوع الفجر فلنذكر فضيلة كل ورد ووظيفته وما يتعلق به فالورد الأول ما ب", "english": "{«آناء الليل ساجدا وقائما يحذر الآخرة ويرجو رحمة ربه قل هل يستوي الذين يعلمون والذين لا يعلمون»} — \"During the hours of the night, prostrating and standing, fearing the Hereafter and hoping for the mercy of his Lord. Say: Are those who know equal to those who do not know?\" (Q. 39:9). And God Most High said: {«تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا»} — \"Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope\" (Q. 32:16). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«والذين يبيتون لربهم سجدا وقياما»} — \"And those who pass the night prostrating and standing before their Lord\" (Q. 25:64). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون وبالأسحار هم يستغفرون»} — \"They used to sleep but little of the night, and in the dawn-hours they would seek forgiveness\" (Q. 51:17-18). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«فسبحان الله حين تمسون وحين تصبحون»} — \"So glory be to God when you enter the evening and when you enter the morning\" (Q. 30:17). And God Most High said: {«ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه»} — \"And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, desiring His Face\" (Q. 6:52). All of this makes clear to you that the path to God Most High is the watchfulness over the times (murāqabat al-awqāt) and the cultivation of them with the appointed devotions (al-awrād) in a perpetual manner.", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 258", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 258: The Virtues of the Watches (Awrād) and Their Ordering", "section_index": 258, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the quenching of thirst at a watering place; al-Wird (pl. Awrād) denotes a set portion or appointed round of devotional recitation, by extension from the regular \"arrival\" at a source.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Wurūd is originally \"the intending of water,\" then used for other things. The theological nuance: the awrād are the appointed \"watering places\" of the soul — fixed times at which the servant presents himself before God to drink from the fountain of remembrance (dhikr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"warda al-mā'a\" — to arrive at water. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"warada ʿalayhi amrun lam yuṭiqhu\" — an affair he could not bear arrived upon him; \"waradat-hu al-ḥummā\" — fever came upon him. Here, the awrād are the appointed times that \"come upon\" the servant, demanding their due of devotion.\n- Root: طلع (ṭ-l-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To rise, to appear above the horizon (of the sun); to ascend; to come upon suddenly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ṭalaʿa al-shams ṭulūʿan wa-maṭlaʿan\" — the sun rose. From this is borrowed: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — so-and-so came upon us. The maṭlaʿ is the place of rising.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ṭalaʿat al-shams\" — the sun rose. Metaphorical: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — he descended upon us suddenly. The \"rising of the sun\" is the boundary-marker of the first watch.\n- Root: ليل (l-y-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The night — from the time of sunset to sunrise; its root suggests \"enveloping\" and \"concealing.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The night (al-layl) and the day (al-nahār) are two signs (āyatān) of God; the night is for stillness and the day for seeking provision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the period of darkness. Metaphorical: \"layl al-hamm\" — the night of anxiety (a dark affair). The night is divided into watches for the one who stands in prayer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else. Applied here: \"the purpose of the sun and moon's course is not for worldly matters, but only (innamā) for knowing the measures of the times.\"", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "14": [{"arabic": "وعذاب القبر والصراط والميزان والحساب ومعنى قوله تعالى {اقرأ كتابك كفى بنفسك اليوم عليك حسيبا} ومعنى قوله تعالى {وإن الدار الآخرة لهي الحيوان لو كانوا يعلمون} ومعنى لقاء الله عز وجل والنظر إلى وجهه الكريم ومعنى القرب منه والنزول في جواره ومعنى حصول السعادة بمرافقة الملأ الأعلى ومقارنة الملائكة والنبيين ومعنى تفاوت درجات أهل الجنان حتى يرى بعضهم البعض كما يرى الكوكب الدري في جوف السماء إلى غير ذلك مما يطول تفصيله إذ للناس في معاني هذه الأمور بعد التصديق بأصولها مقامات شتى فبعضهم يرى أن جميع ذلك أمثلة وأن الذي أعده الله لعباده الصالحين ما ل", "english": "He, exalted, said: {«لهم قلوب لا يفقهون بها»} [They have hearts with which they do not understand] (Q 7:179), meaning thereby the realities of faith, not legal fatwas. By my life, jurisprudence and understanding (fahm) are in language two names with one meaning; the discussion concerns customary usage, both ancient and modern.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 16", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 16: The Science of Unveiling (ʿIlm al-Mukāshafah) and the Science of Practical Conduct (ʿIlm al-Muʿāmalah)", "section_index": 16, "anchors": "- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; unveiling, disclosure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The lifting of a veil (ghishāwah) from the heart such that the realities of things become manifest as if witnessed directly (muʿāyanah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): removal of a physical covering. Metaphorical (majāz): the illumination of the heart with the light of certainty, dispelling the darkness of doubt and heedlessness.\n- Root: عرف (ʿarafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know by reflection and trace; to recognize.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Maʿrifah\" is the perception of a thing through reflection upon its effects; it is more specific than ʿilm and is the knowledge of God attained by the servants through His signs, not by grasping His essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to recognize a person or thing. Metaphorical: the knowledge of God that necessitates reverential fear and love, as opposed to mere information.\n- Root: عمل (ʿamila)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To do, to act, to perform.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The act (ʿamal) is the movement of the servant toward a goal; in religious usage, it is the movement of the heart and limbs in obedience or disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical action. Metaphorical: the actions of the heart (aʿmāl al-qulūb), which are the inward realities of faith.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The distinction between the two sciences is a restriction of the term \"science\" (ʿilm) to its two true referents: the science of unveiling (mukāshafah) and the science of practical conduct (muʿāmalah), excluding all else that is falsely named science.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "15": [{"arabic": "وحسناته فإنه من كسبه وغير مؤاخذ بسيئاته فإنه لا تزر وازرة وزر أخرى ولذلك قال تعالى ألحقنا بهم ذرياتهم وما ألتناهم من عملهم من شيء أي ما نقصناهم من أعمالهم وجعلنا أولادهم مزيدا في إحسانهم الوجه الرابع أن يموت الولد قبله فيكون له شفيعا فقد روي عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إن الطفل يجر بأبويه إلى الجنة (5) وفي بعض الأخبار يأخذ بثوبه كما أنا الآن آخذ بثوبك (6) وقال أيضا صلى الله عليه وسلم إن المولود يقال ادخل الجنة فيقف على باب الجنة فيظل محبنطئا أي ممتلئا غيظا وغضبا ويقول لا أدخل الجنة إلا وأبواي معي فيقال أدخلوا", "english": "وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: \"لا يلقى الله أحد بذنب أعظم من جهالة أهله.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 296", "section_title_en": "Section 296", "section_index": 296, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "21": [{"arabic": "ولكن ما أعظم الفرق بينهما وما أعظم الغبن على من يخسر حظه من ذلك {وللآخرة أكبر درجات وأكبر تفضيلا}", "english": "The Hadith—and by this the disparity of people in faith becomes manifest. For if the faith of Abū Bakr were weighed against the faith of all the worlds, save the prophets and messengers, it would preponderate. This too resembles the saying of one who says: \"If the light of the sun were weighed against the light of all lamps, it would preponderate.\" For the light of the common folk (al-ʿawāmm) is like the light of the lamp, and some of them have light like the light of the candle, while the faith of the sincerely truthful (al-ṣiddīqīn) has light like the light of the moon and the stars, and the faith of the prophets is like the sun. And just as in the light of the sun the forms of the horizons are disclosed—[Page V03P022]—with the vastness of their regions, while in the light of a lamp nothing is disclosed save a narrow corner of the house, so likewise is the disparity in the expansion of the breast (sharḥ al-ṣadr) by the gnoses (maʿārif) and the unveiling of the vastness of the Kingdom (malakūt) to the hearts of the knowers (ʿārifīn). For this reason it has come in the report that it will be said on the Day of Resurrection: \"Bring forth from the Fire whoever has in his heart the weight of a grain of faith, and half a grain, and a quarter of a grain, and a barley-corn, and an atom\" (1)—agreed upon (2).", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 593", "section_title_en": "The Disparity of People in Faith and the Degrees of the Hereafter", "section_index": 593, "anchors": "- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of light, illumination, and that by which one sees; the lamp, the moon, the stars—all termed nūr in varying intensities.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light is of two kinds: sensory light perceived by the eye, and intellectual/spiritual light perceived by the eye of insight (baṣīrah)—the latter being the light of reason, of the Qur'an, and of divine illumination granted to hearts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): the radiant light of celestial bodies and lamps. Metaphorical (majāz): \"nawwara al-amr\" (he illuminated the matter)—i.e., he made it clear and manifest; thus faith's light is spoken of as illumination of the heart by knowledge.\n- Root: وزن (w-z-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To weigh, to measure, to balance; the act of comparing weights to determine preponderance (rujḥān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Weighing in the metaphysical sense denotes the just assessment of worth and degree; the preponderance of one thing over another in measure or excellence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of weighing on scales. Metaphorical: the weighing of deeds and faiths in the balance of divine justice, as in the scales of the Hereafter.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending ranks; the path of ascent from one station to a higher one.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Degree (darajah) is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension—used for lofty ranks and elevations in honor and proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: stairs and steps climbed. Metaphorical: \"li-fulān darajah rafīʿah\" (so-and-so has an exalted degree)—i.e., high standing and rank; \"imshi fī madārij al-ḥaqq\" (walk in the paths of truth).\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, perception, and the mark or sign by which something is known; the scholar (ʿālim) is one distinguished by the sign of knowledge.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality (idrāk al-shayʾ bi-ḥaqīqatihi)—either apprehension of the essence itself, or judgment upon it affirming or negating a predicate.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: perception and awareness. Metaphorical: the \"aʿlām\" (landmarks) of guidance—the traces by which the seeker is directed; the scholar is a living landmark of the religion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd)—the use of syntactic structures to distinguish categories, as in the verse {«Allah will raise those who have believed among you and those who were given knowledge, by degrees»} (58:11), where the two groups are syntactically distinguished to show that faith without knowledge is one rank, and knowledge added to faith is another.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "22": [{"arabic": "الله ولقوله ولا تجعلوا مع الله إلها آخر فمن لم يره في كل شيء فقد رأى غيره وكل ما التفت إليه العبد سوى الله تعالى تضمن التفاته شيئا من الشرك الخفي بل التوحيد الخالص أن لا يرى في كل شيء إلا الله عز وجل العاشر التبري وأعني به أن يتبرأ من حوله وقوته والالتفات إلى نفسه بين الرضا والتزكية فإذا تلا بآيات الوعد والمدح للصالحين فلا يشهد نفسه عند ذلك بل يشهد الموقنين والصديقين فيها ويتشوف إلى أن يلحقه الله عز وجل بهم وإذا تلا آيات المقت وذم العصاة والمقصرين شهد على نفسه هناك وقدر أنه المخا", "english": "For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ said: \"I intend to recite the Qur'an, but when I recall what is in it, I fear abhorrence (al-maqt), so I turn instead to glorification (al-tasbīḥ) and seeking forgiveness (al-istighfār).\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 231", "section_title_en": "** The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 231: On the Inward Conditions of Qur'anic Recitation (Continued)", "section_index": 231, "anchors": "**\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the stirring of fear/apprehension within\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\" — the theological state of the heart's trembling upon remembrance of God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — the physical/emotional state of fear. Metaphorical (Majaz): The heart's trembling as the seat of spiritual perception\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning and transforming\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه، كقلب الثوب، وقلب الإنسان\" — the heart as that which is constantly turning/transforming between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical turning. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" — the heart's turning toward or away from spiritual realities\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit — that which is breathed\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul as the animating principle; also the self as the locus of blameworthy attributes\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — preciousness. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the self as that which must be transcended in spiritual realization\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise delimitation of spiritual states (أحوال) and their hierarchical gradation (درجات)\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "24": [{"arabic": "إلى آخر السورة (3) رب اغفر لي ولوالدي وارحمهما كما ربياني صغيرا واغفر للمؤمنين والمؤمنات والمسلمين والمسلمات الأحياء منهم والأموات (4) رب اغفر وارحم وتجاوز عما تعلم وأنت الأعز الأكرم وأنت خير الراحمين وأنت خير الغافرين وإنا لله وإنا إليه راجعون ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله العلي العظيم وحسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل وصلى الله على محمد خاتم النبيين وآله وصحبه وسلم تسليما كثيرا (5)", "english": "And employ me in righteous deeds which You will accept from me. (1) O God, I ask You for pardon, well-being, good certainty, and soundness in this world and the next. (2) O You Whom sins do not harm and from Whom forgiveness does not diminish anything — grant me that which will not harm You, and give me that which will not diminish You. Our Lord, pour upon us patience and cause us to die as Muslims. You are our Protector in this world and the next; cause me to die as a Muslim and join me with the righteous. You are our Protector, so forgive us and have mercy upon us, and You are the Best of forgivers. And write for us good in this world and in the Hereafter — indeed, we have turned to You in repentance. Our Lord, upon You we rely, to You we turn in repentance, and to You is the final return. Our Lord, make us not a trial for the wrongdoing people. Our Lord, make us not a trial for those who disbelieve, and forgive us, our Lord — indeed, You are the Mighty, the Wise. Our Lord, forgive us our sins and our excesses in our affairs, make firm our feet, and grant us victory over the disbelieving people. Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith, and place not in our hearts any rancor toward those who believe — our Lord, indeed You are Kind, Merciful. Our Lord, grant us from Yourself mercy and prepare for us from our affair right guidance. Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the chastisement of the Fire. Our Lord, indeed we have heard a caller calling to faith — to His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {«إنك لا تخلف الميعاد»} [آل عمران/ 194] — Our Lord, do not take us to task if we forget or err. Our Lord, to the end of the surah. (3) My Lord, forgive me and my parents, and have mercy upon them as they raised me when I was small; and forgive the believing men and the believing women, and the Muslim men and the Muslim women, the living among them and the dead. (4) My Lord, forgive, have mercy, and overlook what You know — and You are the Most Exalted, the Most Generous, the Best of the merciful, and the Best of the forgivers. And indeed, to God we belong and to Him we return; and there is no might and no power except through God, the Exalted, the Magnificent; and God suffices us, and He is the Best Disposer of affairs. And may God send blessings upon Muhammad, the Seal of the Prophets, and upon his family and his companions, and grant them abundant peace. (5)", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 252", "section_title_en": "Section 252 — Supplications for Morning and Evening: Comprehensive Prayers of the Prophet (ﷺ)", "section_index": 252, "anchors": "- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swallowing/ingesting (iltihām); from this derives the sense of something being cast directly into the innermost recess of the heart (al-rawʿ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإلهام: إلقاء الشيء في الروع\" — ilhām is the casting of a thing into the innermost consciousness, specifically from God or the higher assembly (al-malaʾ al-aʿlā). It is that which the Prophet (ﷺ) called \"the touch of the angel\" (limat al-malak) and \"the breath of the Holy Spirit in my innermost heart\" (nafth rūḥ al-qudus fī rawʿī).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ألهمه الله الخير: ألقاه في روعه\" — God inspired him with good: He cast it into his innermost consciousness. Metaphorical (majāz): A horse that \"swallows the ground\" (yaltaḥimu al-arḍ) in swiftness; thus ilhām is the swift, direct ingress of divine guidance into the soul without intermediary effort.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes praise arising from recognition of voluntary excellence and beneficence; al-ḥamd is commendation of the beloved for their attributes and acts of goodness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Praise (ḥamd) is the acknowledgment of beneficence (niʿmah) with reverence and love; it is distinguished from mere thanks (shukr) in that it is directed to the essence and attributes of the praised one, not solely to the benefit received.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" — I praise God with all His praiseworthy attributes. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت صنيعه\" — I found his deed praiseworthy; thus the tongue's praise must be matched by the heart's recognition of the praised one's perfection.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes covering and shielding; al-mighfar is the helmet that protects the head in battle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفر: إلباس ما يصونه عن الدنس\" — Forgiveness is the clothing of that which shields from filth. God's forgiveness (maghfirah) is His shielding of the servant from the touch of chastisement, covering the sin so that it is as though it never were.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"اللهم غفرًا\" — O God, forgive. Metaphorical: The she-beasts that \"did not forgive\" their heedlessness (lam taghfir la-hā ghaflātuhā) — i.e., they did not shield their young from destruction; thus forgiveness is active protection, not mere erasure.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which breathes and has vital spirit; also used for the very essence of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul is the spirit; and in God's saying {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} [آل عمران/ 28], \"His Self\" means His very essence (dhāt), which — though grammatically expressed as an annexation implying distinction — is in reality identical, with no multiplicity in the Divine Essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس\" — a precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — he poured out his blood/life; thus nafs is the seat of life, passion, and moral responsibility.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الدعاء والخبر (The Chapter of Supplication and Assertion) — Supplications (duʿāʾ) are grammatically constructed as optative sentences (inshāʾ ṭalabī) using the imperative (rabbighfir), the jussive (lā tuʾākhidhnā), and the vocative (rabbanā), yet they may embed assertive clauses (khabar) of praise (al-ḥamdu lillāh) and attestation (lā ilāha illā anta). The periodic structure of these prayers alternates between direct address to God (munādāh) and self-referential confession, requiring the translator to preserve the vocative force of \"O God\" and \"Our Lord\" without flattening into mere description.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "26": [{"arabic": "على شيء وقال تعالى ولا تبذر تبذيرا إن المبذرين كانوا إخوان الشياطين وكذلك قال عز وجل والذين إذا أنفقوا لم يسرفوا ولم يقتروا فمن يسرف هذا الإسراف ينكر عليه ويجب على القاضي أن يحجر عليه إلا إذا كان الرجل وحده وكان له قوة في التوكل صادقة فله أن ينفق جميع ماله في أبواب البر ومن له عيال أو كان عاجزا عن التوكل فليس له أن يتصدق بجميع ماله وكذلك لو صرف جميع ماله إلى نقوش حيطانه وتزيين بنيانه فهو أيضا إسراف محرم وفعل ذلك ممن له مال كثير ليس بحرام لأن التزيين من الأغراض الصحيحة ولم تزل المساجد تزين وتنقش أبواب", "english": "And excess varies according to circumstances. Thus we say: whoever possesses only one hundred dinars, for example, and has with him his dependents and children, and they have no means of livelihood other than it, and he spends all of it upon a banquet — he is an extravagant one who must be prevented. God, exalted be He, said: «ولا تبسطها كل البسط فتقعد ملوما محسورا» — \"And do not spread it forth with all spreading, lest you sit down blamed and exhausted\" — and this was revealed concerning a man in Medina who divided all his wealth and left nothing for his dependents; he was then demanded for maintenance and could not produce anything. And God, exalted be He, said: «ولا تبذر تبذيرا إن المبذرين كانوا إخوان الشياطين» — \"And do not squander with squandering; indeed, the squanderers are brothers of the devils.\" And likewise He, mighty and majestic, said: «والذين إذا أنفقوا لم يسرفوا ولم يقتروا» — \"And those who, when they spend, are neither extravagant nor niggardly.\" So whoever commits this extravagance is to be denounced, and it is obligatory upon the judge to place him under legal interdiction (ḥajr) — except if the man be alone [without dependents] and possess true strength in trust in God (tawakkul), then he may spend all his wealth upon the gates of righteousness. But whoever has dependents, or is incapable of [true] trust in God, it is not for him to give in charity all his wealth.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 549", "section_title_en": "Section 549 — On Sitting with a Sinner Without Necessity, and Related Rulings on Silk, Gold, Extravagance, and Permissible Merriment", "section_index": 549, "anchors": "- Root: زين (z-y-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of adornment, ornamentation, and beautification — that which renders something attractive or comely.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True adornment (al-zīnah al-ḥaqīqiyyah) is that which never disgraces its bearer in any state, neither in this world nor the next; bodily and external adornments are only partial, being a disgrace in one respect while an ornament in another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes physical beautification (e.g., adorning the earth with vegetation); metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to moral and spiritual adornment, as in faith being made beloved and adorned in the heart.\n- Root: سرف (s-r-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Exceeding the proper limit in any act; the woodworm (surafah) that consumes timber utterly, leaving it hollow and ruined.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Extravagance (isrāf) is transgressing the bound in every deed a person performs, whether in expenditure or otherwise; it is considered with regard to both quantity and quality — spending in disobedience to God is extravagance even if little.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a garment eaten by moths; Metaphorical: one whose intellect is consumed and corrupted, as the worm consumes wood — extravagance ruins wealth just as the insect ruins timber.\n- Root: كذب (k-dh-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Falsehood in speech or deed; that which contradicts reality or intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood is said of both utterance and action; the hypocrites' claim of faith was true in words but false in conviction — hence God declared them liars regarding their belief, not their speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to utter what is contrary to fact; Metaphorical: \"he carried then lied\" — meaning he attacked then retreated, making his charge false; likewise milk that fails to flow is called \"lying.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed complement when the intervening clause is independently meaningful, just as conditional qualifications may be interposed without corrupting the syntactic bond.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "29": [{"arabic": "قال تعالى {وكلوا واشربوا ولا تسرفوا} وقال تعالى {ولا تجعل يدك مغلولة إلى عنقك ولا تبسطها كل البسط} وقد قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم خيركم خيركم لأهله (5) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم دينار أنفقته في سبيل الله ودينار أنفقته في رقبة ودينار تصدقت به على مسكين ودينار أنفقته على أهلك أعظمها أجرا الذي أنفقته على أهلك (6) وقيل كان لعلي رضي الله عنه أربع نسوة فكان يشتري لكل واحدة في كل أربعة أيام لحما بدرهم وقال الحسن رضي الله عنه كانوا في الرجال مخاصيب والإناث والثياب مجاديب وقال ابن سيرين يستحب للرجل أن يعمل لأهله في كل جمعة فالوذجة وكأن الحلاوة وإن", "english": "The first point of etiquette is the wedding feast (walīmah), and it is recommended (mustaḥabbah). Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saw upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may God be pleased with him, a trace of yellow dye, so he said: {«What is this?»} He replied: \"I have married a woman for the weight of a date-stone in gold.\" He said: \"May God bless you. Hold a wedding feast, even if with a single sheep.\" {«...»} And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, held a wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah with dates and sawīq (barley porridge). {«...»} And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The food of the first day is an obligation (ḥaqq), the food of the second day is a Sunnah, and the food of the third day is for show (sumʿah); and whoever seeks to be heard, God will make his deeds heard.\" {«...»} None raised this hadith except Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and it is anomalous (gharīb). It is recommended to offer congratulations to him, and the one who enters upon the husband should say: \"May God bless you and shower blessings upon you, and may He bring you both together in goodness.\" {«...»} Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, reported that he, may God bless him and grant him peace, commanded this. It is also recommended to make the marriage public (iẓhār al-nikāḥ). He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The distinction between the lawful and the unlawful is the drum and the voice.\" {«...»} The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Announce this marriage, hold it in the mosques, and beat drums for it.\" {«...»} And on the authority of al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh, she said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came and entered on the morning of my wedding night, and he sat upon my bedding, and we had young girls with us who were beating their drums and lamenting those of my forefathers who had been killed—until one of them said: \"And among us is a Prophet who knows what will be tomorrow.\" He said to her: \"Stop this, and say what you were saying before.\" {«...»}", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 303", "section_title_en": "On What is Incumbent Upon the Husband and What is Incumbent Upon the Wife", "section_index": 303, "anchors": "- Root: و ل م (w-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core meaning pertains to the wedding feast (walīmah), derived from the concept of gathering and union.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it signifies the communal meal marking the consummation of a marriage contract, a recommended act of public gratitude.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the specific feast; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to any act of publicizing a joyous occasion.\n- Root: ع ش ر (ʿ- sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes association, companionship, and intimacy; the tenth (ʿasharah) of a thing is related, but the primary social sense is cohabitation and mutual dealing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ}, it signifies a sustained, equitable mode of social intercourse grounded in moral rectitude, not mere physical proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the act of living together; metaphorical (Majaz) usage elevates it to a standard of ethical conduct (maʿrūf) that defines the marital bond.\n- Root: ق س م (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root indicates division, apportionment, and distribution; in the marital context, it refers to the equitable division of time among co-wives.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, it is tied to justice (ʿadl) as a divine attribute; for the husband, it is a legal obligation to maintain fairness in material and temporal allotments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the physical act of dividing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage applies to the moral duty of impartiality in affection and provision.\n- Root: غ ي ر (gh-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root conveys alteration and change, but in the moral register, it denotes protective jealousy (ghayrah), a state of aversion to the violation of honor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ghayrah is a praiseworthy emotion when aligned with divine law—jealousy for the sanctity of the family—and blameworthy when it stems from unjust suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the emotional state; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is attributed to God (al-Ghayyār) as a figure for His prohibition of indecencies, distinguishing human passion from divine will.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate specific obligations (e.g., \"the third is that he should increase...\") and to distinguish categories of jealousy and pride, ensuring precise legal and moral demarcation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "مسرف يجب منعه قال تعالى ولا تبسطها كل البسط فتقعد ملوما محسورا نزل هذا في رجل بالمدينة قسم جميع ماله ولم يبق شيئا لعياله فطولب بالنفقة فلم يقدر على شيء وقال تعالى ولا تبذر تبذيرا إن المبذرين كانوا إخوان الشياطين وكذلك قال عز وجل والذين إذا أنفقوا لم يسرفوا ولم يقتروا فمن يسرف هذا الإسراف ينكر عليه ويجب على القاضي أن يحجر عليه إلا إذا كان الرجل وحده وكان له قوة في التوكل صادقة فله أن ينفق جميع ماله في أبواب البر ومن له عيال أو كان عاجزا عن التوكل فليس له أن يتصدق بجميع ماله وكذلك لو صرف جميع ماله إ", "english": "And excess varies according to circumstances. Thus we say: whoever possesses only one hundred dinars, for example, and has with him his dependents and children, and they have no means of livelihood other than it, and he spends all of it upon a banquet — he is an extravagant one who must be prevented. God, exalted be He, said: «ولا تبسطها كل البسط فتقعد ملوما محسورا» — \"And do not spread it forth with all spreading, lest you sit down blamed and exhausted\" — and this was revealed concerning a man in Medina who divided all his wealth and left nothing for his dependents; he was then demanded for maintenance and could not produce anything. And God, exalted be He, said: «ولا تبذر تبذيرا إن المبذرين كانوا إخوان الشياطين» — \"And do not squander with squandering; indeed, the squanderers are brothers of the devils.\" And likewise He, mighty and majestic, said: «والذين إذا أنفقوا لم يسرفوا ولم يقتروا» — \"And those who, when they spend, are neither extravagant nor niggardly.\" So whoever commits this extravagance is to be denounced, and it is obligatory upon the judge to place him under legal interdiction (ḥajr) — except if the man be alone [without dependents] and possess true strength in trust in God (tawakkul), then he may spend all his wealth upon the gates of righteousness. But whoever has dependents, or is incapable of [true] trust in God, it is not for him to give in charity all his wealth.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 549", "section_title_en": "Section 549 — On Sitting with a Sinner Without Necessity, and Related Rulings on Silk, Gold, Extravagance, and Permissible Merriment", "section_index": 549, "anchors": "- Root: زين (z-y-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of adornment, ornamentation, and beautification — that which renders something attractive or comely.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True adornment (al-zīnah al-ḥaqīqiyyah) is that which never disgraces its bearer in any state, neither in this world nor the next; bodily and external adornments are only partial, being a disgrace in one respect while an ornament in another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes physical beautification (e.g., adorning the earth with vegetation); metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to moral and spiritual adornment, as in faith being made beloved and adorned in the heart.\n- Root: سرف (s-r-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Exceeding the proper limit in any act; the woodworm (surafah) that consumes timber utterly, leaving it hollow and ruined.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Extravagance (isrāf) is transgressing the bound in every deed a person performs, whether in expenditure or otherwise; it is considered with regard to both quantity and quality — spending in disobedience to God is extravagance even if little.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a garment eaten by moths; Metaphorical: one whose intellect is consumed and corrupted, as the worm consumes wood — extravagance ruins wealth just as the insect ruins timber.\n- Root: كذب (k-dh-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Falsehood in speech or deed; that which contradicts reality or intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Falsehood is said of both utterance and action; the hypocrites' claim of faith was true in words but false in conviction — hence God declared them liars regarding their belief, not their speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to utter what is contrary to fact; Metaphorical: \"he carried then lied\" — meaning he attacked then retreated, making his charge false; likewise milk that fails to flow is called \"lying.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed complement when the intervening clause is independently meaningful, just as conditional qualifications may be interposed without corrupting the syntactic bond.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "36": [{"arabic": "يحاسبكم به الله فيغفر لمن يشاء ويعذب من يشاء وقوله تعالى {ولا تقف ما ليس لك به علم إن السمع والبصر والفؤاد كل أولئك كان عنه مسؤولا} فدل على أن عمل الفؤاد كعمل السمع والبصر فلا يعفى عنه وقوله تعالى {ولا تكتموا الشهادة ومن يكتمها فإنه آثم قلبه} وقوله تعالى {لا يؤاخذكم الله باللغو في أيمانكم ولكن يؤاخذكم بما كسبت قلوبكم} والحق عندنا في هذه المسألة لا يوقف عليه ما لم تقع الإحاطة بتفصيل أعمال القلوب من مبدأ ظهورها إلى أن يظهر العمل على الجوارح فنقول أول ما يرد على القلب الخاطر كما لو خطر له مثلا صورة امرأة وأنها وراء ظهره في الطريق", "english": "Know that this is a subtle matter, and there have come down concerning it verses and reports that are mutually conflicting, such that the way of reconciling between them becomes obscure — save for the master-brokers of the scholars of the Sacred Law. For it has been related from the Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — that he said: \"God has pardoned my community for what their souls whisper to them, so long as they do not speak of it or act upon it.\" (1) And Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God — peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"God — exalted is He — says to the guardian angels: When My servant intends a sin, do not write it against him; but if he commits it, then write it as one sin. And when he intends a good deed and does not perform it, write it for him as one good deed; but if he performs it, write it for him as ten.\" (2) This has been recorded by al-Bukhārī and Muslim in the two Ṣaḥīḥs, and it is evidence for the pardon of the heart's act and its intending of a sin. And in another wording: \"Whoever intends a good deed and does not perform it, it is written for him as a good deed; and whoever intends a good deed and performs it, it is written for him as up to seven hundred multiples. And whoever intends a sin and does not perform it, it is not written against him; but if he performs it, it is written.\" And in yet another wording: \"When My servant speaks within himself of committing a sin, I forgive him for it so long as he does not commit it.\" And all of this indicates the pardon.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 606", "section_title_en": "The Heart's Whisperings and the Divine Pardon — On the Four Stages of Inner Acts", "section_index": 606, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or inner being; that which breathes and harbors concealed thoughts.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs denotes the soul or self (al-rūḥ), and in divine address, \"what your souls whisper\" (mā ḥaddathat bihi nufūsuhā) refers to the inner promptings that arise without deliberate volition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage pertains to the soul's essence; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to inner speech and inclination, as in \"ḥadīth al-nafs\" (self-talk), which is not yet resolved action.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To originate or bring forth something new; an event or occurrence that comes to be after non-existence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥudūth is the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; \"ḥadīth al-nafs\" is the newly arising thought that occurs to the heart without prior acquisition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a recent matter or speech; metaphorical: the inner utterance of the soul, as in the prophetic report, \"God has pardoned my community for what their souls whisper to them.\"\n- Root: هم (h-m-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To intend, resolve, or purpose; the stirring of the will toward an act before its execution.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-hamm is the resolved intention (al-ʿazm) that precedes the deed; it is distinguished from mere inclination (mayl) and passing thought (khāṭir).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul's determination; metaphorical: the initial resolve that, when strengthened, becomes a firm will (irādah jazīmah).\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn or overturn, signifying its fluctuating nature between states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart is the seat of cognition and volition; its \"acts\" (aʿmāl al-qulūb) include thought, belief, and resolve, which are subject to divine reckoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical organ; metaphorical: the spiritual faculty that perceives, believes, and intends, as in \"the heart's sin\" (ithm al-qalb).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The prophetic and Quranic statements employ restriction to delineate the boundary between what is pardoned (the passing thought) and what is reckoned (the resolved intention), requiring precise parsing of the conditional clauses (law, idhā, man) to distinguish the stages of inner acts.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "والعجب والرياء والنفاق والحسد وجملة الخبائث من أعمال القلب بل السمع والبصر والفؤاد كل أولئك كان عنه مسئولا أي ما يدخل تحت الاختيار فلو وقع البصر بغير اختيار على غير ذي محرم لم يؤاخذ به فإن أتبعها نظرة ثانية كان مؤاخذا به لأنه مختار فكذا خواطر القلب تجري هذا المجرى بل القلب أولى بمؤاخذته لأنه الأصل قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم التقوى ههنا وأشار إلى القلب (2) وقال الله تعالى {لن ينال الله لحومها ولا دماؤها ولكن يناله التقوى منكم} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم الإثم حواز القلوب (3) وقال البر ما اطمأن إليه القلب وإن أفتوك وأفتوك (4) حتى إنا نقول إذا حكم القلب", "english": "The proof that the mere thought and the initial inclination are not held against the servant is that which is firmly established from the Messenger of God — peace be upon him — that he said: \"God has pardoned my community for what their souls whisper to them, so long as they do not speak of it or act upon it.\" And he said — peace be upon him: \"When two Muslims meet with their swords, then the killer and the killed are both in the Fire.\" It was said: \"O Messenger of God, this is the killer — but what of the killed?\" He said: \"Because he intended to kill his companion.\" {«التقى المسلمان بسيفيهما فالقاتل والمقتول في النار»} — and his being held accountable was solely for his intention (niyyah) and his resolution, not for the mere thought that crossed his heart, for the thought of killing is not within one's capacity to prevent, while the resolution to kill is.", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 607", "section_title_en": "Exposition of What the Servant is Held Accountable For from the Whispers of the Hearts, Their Anxieties, Their Thoughts, and Their Intentions — and What is Pardoned and Not Held Against Him", "section_index": 607, "anchors": "- Root: وسوس (w-s-w-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faint, concealed sound; the subtle, indistinct voice that inclines one toward something\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The insinuation that enters the heart covertly, whether from Satan or from the self's inclination\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the faint jingling of an ornament; Metaphorical — the secret prompting that stirs within the breast\n- Root: خطر (kh-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To come to mind; the passing of a thought across the heart without deliberate pursuit\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sudden occurrence of a notion in the mind, whether invited or uninvited\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — something that presents itself; Metaphorical — the fleeting thought that crosses the heart like a passing traveler\n- Root: هم (h-m-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The intention that inclines one toward an act before resolution is firmly settled\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The initial stirring of the will toward an action, preceding the firm resolve ('azm)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — to intend; Metaphorical — the heart's leaning that, if unchecked, hardens into determination\n- Root: قصد (q-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The deliberate aiming toward a goal; the firm resolution that governs the limbs\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The settled intention that directs action and for which one is justly held accountable\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — to walk a straight path to a destination; Metaphorical — the will's firm orientation that constitutes the essence of moral agency\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين المبتدأ والخبر (The Interposition Between Subject and Predicate) — governing the periodic structure of conditional accountability clauses where the protasis (condition) and apodosis (result) are separated by explanatory parentheticals, preserving the logical dependency of moral judgment upon the specification of the act's nature.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Insight"}], "37": [{"arabic": "أما سمعت قول الله تعالى {ولا تمش في الأرض مرحا إنك لن تخرق الأرض ولن تبلغ الجبال طولا} ومر بالحسن شاب عليه بزة له حسنة فدعاه فقال له ابن آدم معجب بشبابه محب لشمائله كأن القبر قد وارى بدنك وكأنك قد لاقيت عملك ويحك داو قلبك فإن حاجة الله إلى العباد صلاح قلوبهم وروى أن عمر ابن عبد العزيز حج قبل أن يستخلف فنظر إليه طاوس وهو يختال في مشيته فغمز جنبه بأصبعه ثم قال ليست هذه مشية من في بطنه خراء فقال عمر كالمعتذر يا عم لقد ضرب كل عضو مني على هذه المشية حتى تعلمتها ورأى محمد بن واسع ولده يختال فدعاه وقال أتدري من أنت أما أمك فأشت", "english": "The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Allah does not look at a man who drags his lower garment out of vainglory (baṭaran).\" (1) Hadith: \"While a man was strutting proudly in his two striped garments, admiring himself...\" — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh), from the narration of Abū Hurayrah.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 850", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Sin of Vainglorious Strutting (Al-Ikhtiyāl) and the Manifestation of Pride's Traces in Walking and Dragging Garments", "section_index": 850, "anchors": "- Root: خي ل (Kha-Ya-Lam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of self-amazement, haughtiness, and walking with arrogant pride; al-khaylā' is the gait of the conceited.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Ikhtiyāl is the display of self-importance in carriage, a blameworthy trait opposed to humility (tawāḍuʿ); it is a spiritual sickness of the heart manifesting in the limbs.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — the physical strut; Metaphorical (majāz) — the inner state of arrogance that the gait reveals, as the Prophet said: \"Walking is the display of the heart's condition.\"\n- Root: ج ر ر (Jim-Ra-Ra)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To drag or trail along the ground; dragging the garment (jarr al-izār) was a pre-Islamic mark of boastful pride.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The prohibition targets not the garment itself but the intention (niyyah) of self-glorification; the act becomes sin when coupled with khuylā' (vainglory).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — trailing cloth; Metaphorical — the heart's dragging toward worldly esteem.\n- Root: م ش ي (Meem-Shin-Ya)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Mashy is intentional locomotion; al-mayṭīṭā' is a specific censured gait of arrogant swaying, condemned in hadith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — walking; Metaphorical — al-tamashī fī al-arḍ (strutting) as the bodily sign of inner pride.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The conditional clauses and parenthetical warnings (e.g., \"لا ينظر الله إلى...\") are structured to allow rhetorical emphasis through fronting, preserving the threat's gravity.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "39": [{"arabic": "الله ولقوله ولا تجعلوا مع الله إلها آخر فمن لم يره في كل شيء فقد رأى غيره وكل ما التفت إليه العبد سوى الله تعالى تضمن التفاته شيئا من الشرك الخفي بل التوحيد الخالص أن لا يرى في كل شيء إلا الله عز وجل العاشر التبري وأعني به أن يتبرأ من حوله وقوته والالتفات إلى نفسه بين الرضا والتزكية فإذا تلا بآيات الوعد والمدح للصالحين فلا يشهد نفسه عند ذلك بل يشهد الموقنين والصديقين فيها ويتشوف إلى أن يلحقه الله عز وجل بهم وإذا تلا آيات المقت وذم العصاة والمقصرين شهد على نفسه هناك وقدر أنه المخا", "english": "For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ said: \"I intend to recite the Qur'an, but when I recall what is in it, I fear abhorrence (al-maqt), so I turn instead to glorification (al-tasbīḥ) and seeking forgiveness (al-istighfār).\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 231", "section_title_en": "** The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 231: On the Inward Conditions of Qur'anic Recitation (Continued)", "section_index": 231, "anchors": "**\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the stirring of fear/apprehension within\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\" — the theological state of the heart's trembling upon remembrance of God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — the physical/emotional state of fear. Metaphorical (Majaz): The heart's trembling as the seat of spiritual perception\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning and transforming\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه، كقلب الثوب، وقلب الإنسان\" — the heart as that which is constantly turning/transforming between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical turning. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" — the heart's turning toward or away from spiritual realities\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit — that which is breathed\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul as the animating principle; also the self as the locus of blameworthy attributes\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — preciousness. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the self as that which must be transcended in spiritual realization\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise delimitation of spiritual states (أحوال) and their hierarchical gradation (درجات)\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "44": [{"arabic": "ومن هذا قوله تعالى وإن من شيء إلا يسبح بحمده فالبليد يفتقر فيه إلى أن يقدر للجمادات حياة وعقلا ونطقا بصوت وحرف حتى يقول سبحان الله ليتحقق تسبيحه والبصير يعلم أنه ما أريد به نطق اللسان بل كونه مسبحا بوجوده ومقدسا بذاته وشاهدا بوحدانية الله سبحانه كما يقال وفي كل شيء له آية .", "english": "The fifth [division] is that one expresses by the tongue of explicit statement (lisān al-maqāl) what belongs to the tongue of state (lisān al-ḥāl). The one of deficient understanding stops at the outward and believes it to be literal speech, while the one with insight into realities perceives the secret within it. This is like the saying of one who said: \"The wall said to the peg: 'Why have you split me?' It replied: 'Ask the one who strikes me — he did not leave me behind the stone that is behind me.'\" This is an expression of the tongue of state by the tongue of explicit statement.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 85", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Division — Comprehension by Encounter and Taste (Al-Dhawq), and the Fifth Division — The Tongue of State (Lisān al-Ḥāl)", "section_index": 85, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the faculty of knowing, perceiving a thing's reality; encompasses both direct apprehension and judgment about existence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence itself and judgment concerning it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (maʿrifah) from awareness (shuʿūr); here, the distinction between superficial knowledge (ẓāhir) and realized knowledge (bāṭin) is rhetorical-metaphorical, not contradictory.\n- Root: ظهر (ẓahara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes outwardness, visibility, strength of back; the ẓāhir is what is manifest and apparent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ẓāhir is used metaphorically for the outward aspect of a thing, as opposed to its inner reality (bāṭin); the back (ẓahr) carries what is loaded upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz): \"قلبت الأمر ظهرا لبطن\" — turning a matter over, examining its outward and inward aspects; the ẓāhir is the shell (qishr) that contains and is completed by the inner kernel (lubb).\n- Root: وقع (waqaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes falling, occurring, befalling; a thing's occurrence and realization.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوقوع: ثبوت الشيء وسقوطه\" — occurrence is the establishment and falling of a thing; in Quranic usage, often associated with calamities and inevitable events.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz): \"وقع الأمر حصل ووجد\" — the matter occurred, i.e., it became realized and existent; here, the \"occurrence\" of a state (ḥāl) is its actual realization in the soul, as opposed to mere intellectual assent before its descent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure allows for separation between the operative word and its object to achieve rhetorical emphasis, mirroring the semantic distinction between outward apprehension and inward realization.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "ولذلك قال تعالى ولكن لا تفقهون تسبيحهم وأما القاصرون فلا يفقهون أصلا وأما المقربون والعلماء الراسخون فلا يفقهون كنهه وكماله إذ لكل شيء شهادات شتى على تقديس الله سبحانه وتسبيحه ويدرك كل واحد بقدر عقله وبصيرته وتعداد تلك الشهادات لا يليق بعلم المعاملة فهذا الفن أيضا مما يتفاوت أرباب الظواهر وأرباب البصائر في علمه وتظهر به مفارقة الباطن للظاهر وفي هذا المقام لأرباب المقامات إسراف واقتصاد فمن مسرف في رفع الظواهر انتهى إلى تغيير جميع الظواهر والبراهين أو أكثرها حتى حملوا قوله تعالى وتكل", "english": "The fifth [division] is that one expresses by the tongue of explicit statement (lisān al-maqāl) what belongs to the tongue of state (lisān al-ḥāl). The one of deficient understanding stops at the outward and believes it to be literal speech, while the one with insight into realities perceives the secret within it. This is like the saying of one who said: \"The wall said to the peg: 'Why have you split me?' It replied: 'Ask the one who strikes me — he did not leave me behind the stone that is behind me.'\" This is an expression of the tongue of state by the tongue of explicit statement.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 85", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Division — Comprehension by Encounter and Taste (Al-Dhawq), and the Fifth Division — The Tongue of State (Lisān al-Ḥāl)", "section_index": 85, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the faculty of knowing, perceiving a thing's reality; encompasses both direct apprehension and judgment about existence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence itself and judgment concerning it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (maʿrifah) from awareness (shuʿūr); here, the distinction between superficial knowledge (ẓāhir) and realized knowledge (bāṭin) is rhetorical-metaphorical, not contradictory.\n- Root: ظهر (ẓahara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes outwardness, visibility, strength of back; the ẓāhir is what is manifest and apparent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ẓāhir is used metaphorically for the outward aspect of a thing, as opposed to its inner reality (bāṭin); the back (ẓahr) carries what is loaded upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz): \"قلبت الأمر ظهرا لبطن\" — turning a matter over, examining its outward and inward aspects; the ẓāhir is the shell (qishr) that contains and is completed by the inner kernel (lubb).\n- Root: وقع (waqaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes falling, occurring, befalling; a thing's occurrence and realization.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوقوع: ثبوت الشيء وسقوطه\" — occurrence is the establishment and falling of a thing; in Quranic usage, often associated with calamities and inevitable events.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz): \"وقع الأمر حصل ووجد\" — the matter occurred, i.e., it became realized and existent; here, the \"occurrence\" of a state (ḥāl) is its actual realization in the soul, as opposed to mere intellectual assent before its descent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure allows for separation between the operative word and its object to achieve rhetorical emphasis, mirroring the semantic distinction between outward apprehension and inward realization.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "بالوحدانية هي توحيدها وأنواع شاهدات لصانعها بالتقدس هي تسبيحها ولكن لا يفقهون تسبيحها لأنهم لم يسافروا من مضيق سمع الظاهر إلى فضاء سمع الباطن ومن ركاكة لسان المقال إلى فصاحة لسان الحال ولو قدر كل عاجز على مثل هذا السير لما كان سليمان عليه السلام مختصا بفهم منطق الطير ولما كان موسى عليه السلام مختصا بسماع كلام الله تعالى الذي يجب تقديسه عن مشابهة الحروف والأصوات ومن يسافر ليستقرئ هذه الشهادات من الأسطر المكتوبة بالخطوط الإلهية على صفحات الجمادات لم يطل سفره بالبدن بل يستقر في موضع ويفرغ قلبه للتمتع بسماع نغمات التسبيحا", "english": "I say: As for the signs of God in His earth, in contemplating them there are benefits for the discerning seeker of insight. For in them are contiguous tracts, and in them are mountains, wildernesses, seas, and varieties of animals and plants. And there is not a single thing among them but that it bears witness to God of His Oneness and glorifies Him with an eloquent tongue (lisān dhalq) that none perceives save him who gives ear while being present as a witness {«وَأَلْقَى السَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌ»} (And gives ear while he is a witness) [Q. 50:37].", "book_title": "Etiquette of Travel", "section_title_ar": "Section 466", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion — The Benefits of Association and Travel: Association with Increased Occupation and Endurance of Hardships", "section_index": 466, "anchors": "- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of hearing in the ear; also used for the ear itself, and metaphorically for understanding or obedience.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع\" denotes both the physical faculty and, in theological contexts, the inner hearing of the heart by which divine truths are apprehended — distinct from mere auditory perception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): hearing sounds. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"سمع الله لمن حمده\" (God hears him who praises Him) means acceptance and response; \"ألقى السمع\" (gave ear) implies attentive presence of heart.\n- Root: سفر (s-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To uncover, reveal, or travel; the traveler (musāfir) is one who uncovers new horizons.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السفر: كشف الغطاء\" — uncovering the veil; journeying as a means of unveiling hidden realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical travel. Metaphorical: \"وجه مسفر\" (a face radiant with joy); the inner journey from the narrow confines of outward perception to the expanse of inner vision.\n- Root: ظهر (ẓ-h-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The back; outward or visible aspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used metaphorically for the outward, apparent dimension of things, as opposed to the inner (bāṭin).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the back of a beast. Metaphorical: \"قلبت الأمر ظهرا لبطن\" (turned the matter over, back and belly) — examining all aspects; the visible world (ʿālam al-shahādah) as the outward face of reality.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish the exclusive nature of inner hearing (al-samʿ al-bāṭin) as the sole means of apprehending the language of states (lisān al-ḥāl), in contrast to outward hearing shared with beasts.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Travel'", "badge": "Insight"}], "53": [{"arabic": "لإفساد ما بينهما قال الله تعالى وقل لعبادي يقولوا التي هي أحسن إن الشيطان ينزغ بينهم وقال مخبرا عن يوسف من بعد إن نزغ الشيطان بيني وبين إخوتي ويقال ما تواخى اثنان في الله فتفرق بينهما إلا بذنب يرتكبه أحدهما وكان بشر يقول إذا قصر العبد في طاعة الله سلبه الله من يؤنسه وذلك لأن الإخوان مسلاة للهموم وعون على الدين ولذلك قال ابن المبارك ألذ الأشياء مجالسة الإخوان والانقلاب إلى كفاية والمودة الدائمة هي التي تكون في الله وما يكون لغرض يزول بزوال ذلك الغرض ومن ثمرات المودة في الله أن لا تكون مع حسد في دين ودنيا وكيف يحسده وكل ما هو لأخيه فإليه تر", "english": "I say: The meaning of loyalty (al-wafāʾ) is steadfastness upon love and its continuation until death — with him, and after death with his children and his friends. For love is sought only for the Hereafter; if it is severed before death, the deed is rendered void and the striving is lost. For this reason, he ﷺ said concerning the seven whom God will shade in His shade: «ورجلان تحابا في الله اجتمعا على ذلك وتفرقا عليه» — \"And two men who loved each other for God's sake, gathering upon that and parting upon that.\"", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 427", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 427: On the Meaning of Loyalty (al-Wafāʾ) and Its Fruits", "section_index": 427, "anchors": "- Root: وفي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Complete fulfillment, perfect performance of a covenant; to be true to one's bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Loyalty (wafāʾ) is the completion of a promise (ʿahd) and its preservation against dissolution; its opposite is betrayal (ghadr) and severance (qaṭīʿah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وفى بعهده\" — he fulfilled his pact completely. Metaphorical: \"وفى الكيل\" — he gave full measure; loyalty in love is the full measure of brotherhood, for it is the perfection of the covenant of affection.\n- Root: حبب (Ḥ-B-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core love, affection, steadfast amity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True love (maḥabbah) in the theological sense is the inclination of the heart toward what pleases the Beloved; when it is \"for God\" (fī Allāh), it is purely spiritual and enduring, not subject to the vicissitudes of worldly aims.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حبه يحبه حبا\" — he loved him. Metaphorical: \"تحابا في الله\" — they loved each other for God's sake, i.e., their love is bound by the divine bond, not by transient causes.\n- Root: نزغ (N-Z-GH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To incite, to provoke, to instigate discord.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Nazgh\" is the insinuation of corruption and the stirring of strife by Satan; it is the subtle injection of rancor between two hearts united in good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نزغ بينهم\" — he stirred up trouble between them. Metaphorical: Satan's \"nazgh\" is his secret whispering that ignites the fire of separation after the coolness of affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The conditional and restrictive particles (innamā, law, idhā) govern the periodic structure, binding the protasis of loyalty's test to its apodosis of reward or loss.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "57": [{"arabic": "فإذن الناس وإن اختلفت طرقهم في العبادة فكلهم على الصواب أولئك الذين يدعون يبتغون إلى ربهم الوسيلة أيهم أقرب وإنما يتفاوتون في درجات القرب لا في أصله وأقربهم إلى الله تعالى أعرفهم به وأعرفهم به لا بد وأن يكون أعبدهم له فمن عرفه لم يعبد غيره والأصل في الأوراد في حق كل صنف من الناس المداومة فإن المراد منه تغيير الصفات الباطنة وآحاد الأعمال يقل آثارها بل لا يحس بآثارها وإنما يترتب الأثر على المجموع فإذا لم يعقب العمل الواحد أثرا محسوسا ولم يردف بثان وثالث على القرب انمحى الأثر الأول وكان كالفقيه يريد أن يكون فقيه النفس فإنه لا يصير فقيه النفس إلا بتكرار كثير", "english": "The fifth [category]: the ruler (al-wālī) — such as the Imām, the judge (al-qāḍī), and the one who administers the affairs of the Muslims. His undertaking the needs of the Muslims and their interests in accordance with the Sacred Law (al-sharʿ) and with the intention of sincerity (ikhlāṣ) is superior to the aforementioned litanies (al-awrād). It is fitting for him to occupy himself with the rights of the people by day, to content himself with the obligatory prayers (al-maktūbah), and to perform the aforementioned litanies by night — just as ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to do, for he said: \"What have I to do with sleep? If I sleep by day, I waste the Muslims; and if I sleep by night, I waste my own soul.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 269", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 269: On the Superiority of Transitive Acts of Worship and the Paths to God", "section_index": 269, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the quenching of thirst by reaching the source.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — theologically extended to mean the soul's approach to God; the \"litany\" (wird) is the appointed spiritual \"watering\" that sustains the traveler.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"ورد الماء\" (to arrive at water). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد عليه أمر لم يطقه\" (an affair befell him) and \"طرقني الخيال\" (the phantom visited me) — thus the wird is the appointed visitation of the heart by divine remembrance.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which breathes and has vital spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the immaterial reality of the human; in the verse {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} it denotes the very Essence (al-Dhāt), not a physical part.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس سائلة\" (flowing blood/life). Metaphorical: \"أصابته نفس\" (an evil eye afflicted him) — showing the soul's power to effect beyond its own bounds.\n- Root: طرق (t-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To strike, to beat, to knock; a path beaten by feet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل\" — then extended to every path (maslak) a man traverses in deed, whether praiseworthy or blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"طرق الباب\" (to knock at the door). Metaphorical: \"طرقني الخيال\" (the phantom came to me by night) — hence the \"ways to God\" (turūq) are the paths beaten by the feet of the seekers, each leading to the Door.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish the exclusive rank of the muwaḥḥid whose sole concern is God, and to restrict the claim of such a station to those who have first traversed the ordered litanies.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Insight"}], "59": [{"arabic": "كقوله تعالى وآتينا ثمود الناقة مبصرة فظلموا بها معناه آية مبصرة فظلموا أنفسهم بقتلها فالناظر إلى ظاهر العربية يظن أن المراد به أن الناقة كانت مبصرة ولم تكن عمياء ولم يدر أنهم بماذا ظلموا غيرهم أو أنفسهم وقوله تعالى وأشربوا في قلوبهم العجل بكفرهم أي حب العجل فحذف الحب وقوله عز وجل إذا لأذقناك ضعف الحياة وضعف الممات أي ضعف عذاب الأحياء وضعف عذاب الموتى فحذف العذاب وأبدل الأحياء والموتى بذكر الحياة والموت وكل ذلك جائز في فصيح اللغة وقوله تعالى واسئل القرية التي كنا فيها والعير التي أقبلنا فيها", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "61": [{"arabic": "فقال وما هو قال إياك والكبر فإنه أول ذنب عصي الله به ثم قرأ {وإذ قلنا للملائكة اسجدوا لآدم فسجدوا إلا إبليس} الآية وإياك والحرص فإنه أخرج آدم من الجنة أمكنه الله سبحانه من جنة عرضها السموات والأرض يأكل منها إلا شجرة واحدة نهاه الله عنها فأكل منها فأخرجه الله تعالى منها ثم قال {اهبطوا منها} إلى آخر الآية وإياك والحسد فإنما قتل ابن آدم أخاه حين حسده ثم قرأ {واتل عليهم نبأ ابني آدم بالحق} الآيات وإذا ذكر أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فأمسك وإذا ذكر القدر فاسكت وإذا ذكرت النجوم فاسكت وقال بكر بن عبد الله كان رجل يغشى بعض الملوك فيقوم بحذاء الملك ف", "english": "Know that envy (ḥasad) is likewise among the fruits of rancor (ḥiqd), and rancor is among the fruits of anger (ghaḍab) — so envy is a branch of a branch, while anger is the root of the root. Then envy has, among its blameworthy branches, that which can scarcely be enumerated. And there have come down, specifically in condemnation of envy, many reports (akhbār). The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Envy consumes good deeds just as fire consumes wood»} (1). The ḥadīth: {«Do not cut one another off, nor turn your backs on one another, nor hate one another»} — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh), and it has already preceded. (2) The ḥadīth of Anas: \"We were sitting one day with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, when he said: 'There will now come upon you from this mountain pass a man from among the people of Paradise'\" — the ḥadīth in its entirety, and in it: that man said, \"I do not find in myself any rancor toward any of the Muslims, nor envy of anyone for the good that God has given him.\" Narrated by Aḥmad with a sound chain (isnād) upon the condition of the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhārī and Muslim), and narrated by al-Bazzār, and the man was named in one of its narrations as Saʿd — though in it there is Ibn Lahīʿah. (3) The ḥadīth: {«Three things from which no one escapes: suspicion, slander, and envy»} — and in another narration: \"And few are those who escape them.\" It was related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā in his book \"Dhamm al-Ḥasad\" (The Condemnation of Envy) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah — though in its chain there are Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī and Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb al-Zamaʿī, both of whom the majority of scholars have declared weak. The second narration was also related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā from the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah, and it is mursal (broken chain) and weak. Al-Ṭabarānī has something similar from the ḥadīth of Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, and it has already preceded in the section on the afflictions of the tongue. And in one narration: {«Three things from which no one escapes — and few are those who escape them»} — so this narration affirms the possibility of deliverance. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«There has crept upon you the disease of the nations before you: envy and hatred. And hatred is the clipper — I do not say it clips the hair, but rather it clips the religion. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, you shall not enter Paradise until you believe, and you shall not believe until you love one another. Shall I not inform you of that which will establish this for you? Spread peace (salām) among you»} (4). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Poverty almost becomes unbelief, and envy almost overcomes destiny (qadar)»} (5). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, my community will be afflicted with the disease of the nations.» They said: \"And what is the disease of the nations?\" He said: {«Extravagance, arrogance, accumulation, rivalry in worldly things, mutual distance, and mutual envy — until transgression (baghy) comes, and then destruction (harj)»} (6). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Do not show gloating (shamātah) over your brother, lest God heal him and afflict you»} (7). And it is narrated that Moses, peace be upon him, when he hastened to his Lord, Exalted is He, saw a man in the shade of the Throne (ʿArsh) and envied him his station (ghabaṭahu), saying: \"This one is honored before his Lord.\" So he asked his Lord, Exalted is He, to inform him of his name, but He did not inform him, and said: \"I shall tell you of three of his deeds: he did not envy people for what God had given them of His bounty, he was not undutiful to his parents, and he did not go about with slander (namīmah).\" And Zechariah, peace be upon him, said: God, Exalted is He, said: {«The envier is an enemy of My blessing, resentful of My decree, dissatisfied with My apportioning which I have apportioned among My servants»}. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«The thing I most fear for my community is that wealth will increase among them, and they will envy one another and fight one another»} (1). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Seek help in fulfilling your needs by keeping them secret, for everyone who possesses a blessing is envied»} (2). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, the blessings of God have enemies.» It was said: \"And who are they?\" He said: {«Those who envy people for what God has given them of His bounty»} (3). The ḥadīth: {«Six will enter the Fire before the Reckoning by a year.» It was said: \"O Messenger of God, who are they?\" He said: {«The unjust rulers»} — the ḥadīth — and in it: {«And the scholars who envy»}. Narrated by Abū Manṣūr al-Daylamī from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and Anas with two weak chains of transmission.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 718", "section_title_en": "Section 718 — On Envy (Ḥasad): Its Roots, Branches, and Blameworthy Fruits", "section_index": 718, "anchors": "- Root: حسد (Ḥ-S-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of wishing for removal of a blessing from its possessor, rooted in spite and resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — Envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who rightly deserves it, sometimes accompanied by active striving to effect its removal. The believer envies not, but the hypocrite does.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — to envy him for God's blessing. Metaphorical (majāz): \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body, as fire consumes wood, indicating its destructive spiritual effect.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥ-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; bringing into being after nothingness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination is a thing's coming to be after it was not. The ḥadīth (report) is so-called for its temporal proximity to the event it narrates.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" — he is a youth among youths. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — they became subjects of talk, indicating fame or infamy spread by tongues.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses and disposes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — dominion is disposal by command and prohibition. Two kinds: actual possession/sovereignty, and the power/capacity to exercise it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ملكت فلانة\" — he married her (possessed her in wedlock). Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — he mastered himself at the moment of anger, i.e., self-restraint.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the Prophet (ﷺ) says \"إنما قتل ابن آدم أخاه حين حسده\" (It was only envy that led the son of Adam to kill his brother), the restrictive particle إنما confines the cause of the first murder to envy alone, isolating it as the sole efficient cause of that primordial transgression.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "65": [{"arabic": "عبادي ليس لك عليهم سلطان} والسماع سبب لصفاء القلب وهو شبكة للحق بواسطة الصفاء وعلى هذا يدل ما روى أن ذا النون المصري رحمه الله دخل بغداد فاجتمع إليه قوم من الصوفية ومعهم قوال فاستأذنوه في أن يقول لهم شيئا فأذن لهم في ذلك فأنشأ يقول صغير هواك عذبني .", "english": "For this reason, he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Beware the discernment of the believer, for he looks with the light of God.\" (2)", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 509", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 509: On the Vision of Spiritual Realities Through Purity of Heart", "section_index": 509, "anchors": "- Root: صفا (ṣafā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of clarity, purity, and transparency — free from turbidity or admixture.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Denotes the heart's purification from base attributes, rendering it receptive to divine disclosures (mukāshafāt).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clarity of water or wine. Metaphorical: purity of the heart as the condition for spiritual perception.\n- Root: مثل (mathala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To stand before, to appear, to take shape or form.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for representation and formation — the appearing of a thing in a form (ṣūrah) that may or may not correspond to its true reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing erect before someone. Metaphorical: the self-representation of spiritual beings (arwāḥ) in sensible forms perceptible to the purified heart.\n- Root: نظر (naẓar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Vision, sight, and also reflection and consideration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Distinguishes between baṣar (physical sight) and baṣīrah (insight); the latter perceives spiritual realities (malakūt).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: seeing with the eye. Metaphorical: \"looking with the light of God\" (al-naẓar bi-nūr Allāh) — perception through divine illumination.\n- Root: فرس (farasa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To prey upon, to seize — the ferāsah (discernment) is so named because it seizes upon the inner reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intuitive perception of inner states — a form of inspired knowledge (ʿilm ladunī).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the lion preying on its quarry. Metaphorical: the believer's discernment that pierces through outward appearances to inward truths.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to confine the activity of devils to hearts filled with blameworthy attributes, and to restrict the vision of the Kingdom of Heaven to purified hearts.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Insight"}], "72": [{"arabic": "تعمي القلوب التي في الصدور وقال تعالى ومن كان في هذه أعمى فهو في الآخرة أعمى وأضل سبيلا وهذه الأمور التي كشفت للأنبياء بعضها كان بالبصر وبعضها كان بالبصيرة وسمي الكل رؤية وبالجملة من لم تكن بصيرته الباطنة ثاقبة لم يعلق به من الدين إلا قشوره وأمثلته دون لبابه وحقائقه فهذه أقسام ما ينطلق اسم العقل عليها", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "74": [{"arabic": "تخاف فتنته وقد قيل لسيد المرسلين صلى الله عليه وسلم ولولا أن ثبتناك لقد كدت تركن إليهم شيئا قليلا وقال سهل رحمه الله العلم كله دنيا والآخرة منه العمل به والعمل كله هباء إلا الإخلاص وقال الناس كلهم موتى إلا العلماء والعلماء سكارى إلا العاملين والعاملون كلهم مغرورون إلا المخلصين والمخلص على وجل حتى يدري ماذا يختم له به وقال أبو سليمان الداراني رحمه الله إذا طلب الرجل الحديث أو تزوج أو سافر في طلب المعاش فقد ركن إلى الدنيا وإنما أراد به طلب الأسانيد العالية أو طلب الحديث الذي لا يحتاج إليه في طلب الآخرة وقال عيسى عليه السلام كيف يكون من أهل العلم", "english": "[Root: ملح]\n  • Lisan al-Arab: \"الملح: ما يصلح الطعام، وجمعه ملوح وأملاح. والمليح: الحسن. والملاحة: الحسن. وملح الرجل يملح ملاحة: حسن. وتملح: تزين.\"\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الملح: ما يحفظ الطعام ويمنع فساده، ويستعار لما يحفظ الإيمان والعلم من الفساد.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ملح الماء: إذا كان فيه ملوحة. وملح الطعام: جعل فيه الملح. وهذا على الحقيقة، ويستعار الملح لما يصلح به الأمر.\"\n  • Kitab al-Ayn: \"الملح: معروف، والمليح: الحسن من كل شيء.\"", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 52", "section_title_en": "Section 52", "section_index": 52, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "75": [{"arabic": "وأشربوا في قلوبهم العجل بكفرهم أي حب العجل فحذف الحب وقوله عز وجل إذا لأذقناك ضعف الحياة وضعف الممات أي ضعف عذاب الأحياء وضعف عذاب الموتى فحذف العذاب وأبدل الأحياء والموتى بذكر الحياة والموت وكل ذلك جائز في فصيح اللغة وقوله تعالى واسئل القرية التي كنا فيها والعير التي أقبلنا فيها أي أهل العير فالأهل فيهما محذوف مضمر وقوله عز وجل ثقلت في السموات والأرض معناه خفيت على أهل السموات والأرض والشيء إذا خفي ثقل فأبدل اللفظ به وأقيم في مقام على وأضمر الأهل وحذف وقوله تعالى وتجعلون رزقكم أنكم تكذبون أي شكر رزقكم وقوله عز وجل وآتنا ما وعدتنا على رسلك", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "80": [{"arabic": "متواضعا معظما وليقل بسم الله وعلى ملة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم رب أدخلني مدخل صدق وأخرجني مخرج صدق واجعل لي من لدنك سلطانا نصيرا ثم يقصد المسجد ويدخله ويصلي بجنب المنبر ركعتين ويجعل عمود المنبر حذاء منكبه الأيمن ويستقبل السارية التي إلى جانبها الصندوق وتكون الدائرة التي في قبلة المسجد بين عينيه فذلك موقف رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قبل أن يغير المسجد وليجتهد أن يصلي في المسجد الأول قبل أن يزاد فيه ثم يأتي قبر النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فيقف عند وجهه وذلك بأن يستدبر القبلة ويستقبل جدار القبر على نحو من أربعة أذرع من السارية التي في زاوية جدار القبر ويجعل القنديل على رأس", "english": "I say: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever visits me after my death, it is as though he visited me during my life.\" {من زارني بعد وفاتي فكأنما زارني في حياتي}", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 210", "section_title_en": "Section 210 — The Etiquette of Visiting the Prophet's Mosque and Grave in Medina", "section_index": 210, "anchors": "- Root: زور (z-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of inclining toward, visiting, or intending a place or person; the visitor (zā'ir) is one who turns away from his own affairs toward another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, ziyārah (visitation) of the Prophet ﷺ after death is an act of spiritual devotion (taqarrub), not a mere physical journey; its reward is tied to the sincerity of intention (niyyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage denotes physical approach; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to seeking intercession (shafā'ah) and spiritual connection with the visited.\n- Root: سلم (s-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes safety, peace, and deliverance from harm; the greeting \"al-salāmu ʿalaykum\" is a prayer for the recipient's immunity from all deficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The greeting upon the Prophet ﷺ is a form of supplication (duʿā') and acknowledgment of his rank; it is also a renewal of the covenant (mīthāq) of obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the exchange of safety; metaphorical usage in the visitation context signifies the visitor's submission and the Prophet's reciprocal blessing.\n- Root: شفع (sh-f-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes pairing or joining; shafā'ah (intercession) is the act of the intercessor joining his own plea to that of the petitioner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, shafā'ah is a divinely granted honor (karāmah) to the Prophet ﷺ, conditional upon divine will; it is not an independent power but a mediated mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: making a number even by adding one; metaphorical: the intercessor's elevation of the petitioner's rank before the king.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — The text employs extended vocative and prepositional structures (e.g., \"al-salāmu ʿalayka yā Rasūl Allāh\") where the syntactic pause after each invocation is permissible and rhetorically deliberate, mirroring the classical allowance for separation between the governing particle and its object for emphasis.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "82": [{"arabic": "وقال قتادة لم يجالس أحد هذا القرآن إلا قام بزيادة أو نقصان قال تعالى هو شفاء ورحمة للمؤمنين ولا يزيد الظالمين إلا خسارا الثامن التأثر وهو أن يتأثر قلبه بآثار مختلفة بحسب اختلاف الآيات فيكون له بحسب كل فهم حال ووجد يتصف به قلبه من الحزن والخوف والرجاء وغيره ومهما تمت معرفته كانت الخشية أغلب الأحوال على قلبه فإن التضييق غالب على آيات القرآن فلا يرى ذكر المغفرة والرحمة إلا مقرونا بشروط يقصر العارف عن نيلها كقوله عز وجل وإني لغفار ثم أتبع ذلك بأربعة شروط لمن تاب وآمن وعمل صالحا ثم اهتدى وقوله تعالى والعصر إن الإنسان لفي خسر إلا الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات وتواصوا بالحق", "english": "[Root: ذكر]\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور الشيء القلب أو القول، ويكون تارة بالقلب وتارة باللسان، والذكر باللسان تارة يكون حضور المعنى وبعده، وقد يقال: ذكرته إذا جرى على لسانه وإن لم يحضره قلبه، والذكر بالقلب هو حضور المعنى، قال تعالى: ولا تطع من أغفلنا قلبه عن ذكرنا [الكهف/ 28]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"ذكرت الشيء: نطقت به أو استحضرته بقلبك. وذكرت فلانا بخير وشر. واذكروا الله ذكرا كثيرا. والذكر: الشرف والصيت، قال تعالى: وإنه لذكر لك ولقومك [الزخرف/ 44]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الذكر: الكتاب الذي فيه تذكير الناس، قال تعالى: وأنزلنا إليك الذكر لتبين للناس ما نزل إليهم [النحل/ 44]، والذكر: القرآن، قال تعالى: إنا نحن نزلنا الذكر وإنا له لحافظون [الحجر/ 9]\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 229", "section_title_en": "Section 229", "section_index": 229, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "84": [{"arabic": "تعالى قال تعالى قل كل يعمل على شاكلته فربكم أعلم بمن هو أهدى سبيلا فكلهم مهتدون وبعضهم أهدى من بعض وفي الخبر الإيمان ثلاث وثلاثون وثلثمائة طريقة من لقي الله تعالى بالشهادة على طريق منها دخل الجنة (1) وقال بعض العلماء الإيمان ثلثمائة وثلاثة عشر خلقا بعدد الرسل فكل مؤمن على خلق منها فهو سألك الطريق إلى الله فإذن الناس وإن اختلفت طرقهم في العبادة فكلهم على الصواب أولئك الذين يدعون يبتغون إلى ربهم الوسيلة أيهم أقرب وإنما يتفاوتون في درجات القرب لا في أصله وأقربهم إلى الله تعالى أعرفهم به وأعرفهم به لا بد وأن يك", "english": "The fifth [category]: the ruler (al-wālī) — such as the Imām, the judge (al-qāḍī), and the one who administers the affairs of the Muslims. His undertaking the needs of the Muslims and their interests in accordance with the Sacred Law (al-sharʿ) and with the intention of sincerity (ikhlāṣ) is superior to the aforementioned litanies (al-awrād). It is fitting for him to occupy himself with the rights of the people by day, to content himself with the obligatory prayers (al-maktūbah), and to perform the aforementioned litanies by night — just as ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to do, for he said: \"What have I to do with sleep? If I sleep by day, I waste the Muslims; and if I sleep by night, I waste my own soul.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 269", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 269: On the Superiority of Transitive Acts of Worship and the Paths to God", "section_index": 269, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the quenching of thirst by reaching the source.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — theologically extended to mean the soul's approach to God; the \"litany\" (wird) is the appointed spiritual \"watering\" that sustains the traveler.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"ورد الماء\" (to arrive at water). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد عليه أمر لم يطقه\" (an affair befell him) and \"طرقني الخيال\" (the phantom visited me) — thus the wird is the appointed visitation of the heart by divine remembrance.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which breathes and has vital spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the immaterial reality of the human; in the verse {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} it denotes the very Essence (al-Dhāt), not a physical part.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس سائلة\" (flowing blood/life). Metaphorical: \"أصابته نفس\" (an evil eye afflicted him) — showing the soul's power to effect beyond its own bounds.\n- Root: طرق (t-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To strike, to beat, to knock; a path beaten by feet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطريق: السبيل الذي يطرق بالأرجل\" — then extended to every path (maslak) a man traverses in deed, whether praiseworthy or blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"طرق الباب\" (to knock at the door). Metaphorical: \"طرقني الخيال\" (the phantom came to me by night) — hence the \"ways to God\" (turūq) are the paths beaten by the feet of the seekers, each leading to the Door.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish the exclusive rank of the muwaḥḥid whose sole concern is God, and to restrict the claim of such a station to those who have first traversed the ordered litanies.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Insight"}], "85": [{"arabic": "وإنما هو أمر إلهي كما قال تعالى ويسئلونك عن الروح قل الروح من أمر ربي وكل المخلوقات منسوبة إلى الله تعالى ولكن نسبته أشرف من نسبة سائر أعضاء البدن فلله الخلق والأمر جميعا والأمر أعلى من الخلق وهذه الجوهرة النفيسة الحاملة لأمانة الله تعالى المتقدمة بهذه الرتبة على السموات والأرضين والجبال إذ أبين أن يحملنها وأشفقن منها من عالم الأمر ولا يفهم من هذا أنه تعريض بقدمها فإن القائل بقدم الأرواح مغرور جاهل لا يدري ما يقول فلنقبض عنان البيان عن هذا الفن فهو وراء ما نحن بصدده والمقصود أن هذه اللطيفة هي الساعية إلى قرب الرب", "english": "As for the attainment of happiness, none reaches it except the knowers of Allah the Exalted — and they are the brought-near (al-muqarrabūn), the blessed ones in the vicinity of Allah the Exalted, with repose and fragrance and the Garden of Delight. As for those who are barred below the summit of perfection, for them is deliverance and safety, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«فأما إن كان من المقربين فروح وريحان وجنة نعيم وأما إن كان من أصحاب اليمين فسلام لك من أصحاب اليمين»} (So if he is of those brought near, then repose and fragrance and a Garden of Delight. And if he is of the companions of the right, then peace to you from the companions of the right). And whoever does not set out toward the objective, nor rouses himself for it — or rouses himself toward it not with the intention of compliance and servitude, but rather for some immediate purpose — he is of the companions of the left and of the astray; for him is an entertainment of boiling water and a roasting in Hellfire.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 44", "section_title_en": "The Science of the Path: On the Threefold Division of Knowledge and the Journey to God", "section_index": 44, "anchors": "- Root: سلك (s-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To traverse, to pass through, to thread (as a needle through cloth); the path one takes through a terrain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السلوك: النفاذ في الطريق\" — the penetrating passage through a road; used both literally (traversing a physical path) and metaphorically (the soul's passage through spiritual wayfaring).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"طريق مسلوك\" (a trodden path); Metaphorical: \"ذهب في مسلك خفي، وخذ في مسالك الحق\" — going in a hidden course, taking the paths of truth. The term thus spans both physical travel and the interior journey of the soul.\n- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank; kinship and proximity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القرب والبعد يتقابلان... ويستعمل ذلك في المكان، وفي الزمان، وفي النسبة، وفي الحظوة، والرعاية، والقدرة\" — nearness and distance are opposites, used in place, time, relation, favor, care, and power. The theological sense here is nearness to God as station (ḥaẓwah) and favor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قرب منه وإليه\" (he drew near to him); Metaphorical: \"لقد قربت أمرا ما أدري ما هو\" (you have brought near a matter I know not what it is) — indicating that proximity can be by rank and honor, not merely spatial.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, perception, cognition; the root from which ʿālim (knower) and ʿilm (knowledge) derive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" (I did not know of your news: I was unaware of it); the term spans sensory awareness and intellectual certitude.\n- Root: الي (a-l-y) [as in ألى/إلى]\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of \"إلى\" (toward, unto) and \"أولى\" (more worthy); also related to \"آل\" (family, those who return to a head).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root carries the sense of direction and destination — the \"unto\" of a journey's end.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The prepositional force of \"إلى\" marks the terminus of motion, whether physical or spiritual.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التشبيه والتقسيم الثلاثي (The Chapter of Analogy and Tripartite Division) — The author employs a structured analogy (tamthīl) dividing knowledge into three categories, each mapped to a stage of the pilgrimage journey (preparation, traversing the wilderness, and the rites themselves). The syntax moves from the concrete (physical journey) to the abstract (spiritual wayfaring), with each division governed by a parallel construction (qism... qism... qism).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "سره للبشر فما أوتينا من العلم إلا قليلا ويكفينا في التصديق بذلك التجربة والمشاهدة فقد ورد الخبر به قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لو أن مؤمنا دخل إلى مجلس فيه مائة منافق واحد لجاء حتى يجلس إليه ولو أم منافقا دخل إلى مجلس فيه مائة ومؤمن ومنافق واحد لجاه حتى يجلس إليه وهذا يدل على أن شبه الشيء منجذب إليه بالطبع وإن كان هو لا يشعر به وكان مالك بن دينار يقول لا يتفق اثنان في عشرة إلا وفي أحدهما وصف من الآخر وإن أجناس الناس كأجناس الطير ولا يتفق نوعان من الطير في الطيران إلا وبينهما مناسبة قال فرأى ي", "english": "Nay, in the harmony of hearts there is a matter even more subtle than this. For affection may become firmly rooted between two persons without any comeliness of form, nor beauty of innate disposition or character, but solely due to a suitability that necessitates intimacy and concord. For the like of a thing is drawn to it by nature, and inward matters are hidden, having subtle causes that are not within human power to perceive. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, alluded to this when he said: {«الأرواح جنود مجندة فما تعارف منها ائتلف وما تناكر منها اختلف»} — \"Spirits are conscripted soldiers; those that recognize one another unite, and those that reject one another diverge.\" Thus, mutual rejection is the result of disparity, and union is the result of the suitability that he expressed as \"mutual recognition.\" In some wordings: {«الأرواح جنود مجندة تلتقي فتتشام في الهواء»} — \"Spirits are conscripted soldiers; they meet and sniff one another in the air.\"", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 404", "section_title_en": "On Love for Allah's Sake and Hatred for Allah's Sake: The Subtle Realities of Spiritual Affinity", "section_index": 404, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of love, affection, and endearment; the beloved (maḥbūb) is that toward which the heart inclines.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Love (maḥabbah) in the theological sense is the soul's inclination toward that which is pleasing — ultimately, the soul's attraction to Allah and His awliyā', distinguished from base desire (hawā).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): physical inclination and attachment. Metaphorical (majāz): the spiritual communion of souls and the intentional pursuit of companionship for religious ends.\n- Root: ص ح ب (ṣ-ḥ-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Companionship (ṣuḥbah) — association, sitting together, and enduring presence with another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Companionship as a voluntary act of association; in the religious context, brotherhood (ukhuwwah) in dīn is a deliberate covenant, not mere accident.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical proximity and sitting together. Metaphorical: the soul's affinity and harmonious union with another's character.\n- Root: ن س ب (n-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proportion, relation, and congruence; the basis of similarity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Munāsabah — the spiritual correspondence between created souls, preordained in the divine decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: genealogical or categorical relation. Metaphorical: the hidden congruity of temperaments that draws souls together.\n- Root: أ ل ف (ʾ-l-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Union, harmony, and the taming of what is disparate into concord.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): I'tilāf — the divinely ordained concord among souls, as opposed to tanāfur (mutual aversion).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to unite or reconcile. Metaphorical: the spontaneous attraction of like souls, as expressed in the prophetic tradition of \"spirits as conscripted soldiers.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: The Division of the Sentence into Conditional and Categorical Clauses (Bāb al-Sharṭ wa al-Jazāʾ) — governing the logical structure of \"if... then...\" constructions that articulate the causal relationship between love, companionship, and their objects.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال سبحانه ويسألونك عن الروح قل الروح من أمر ربي أو معنى كونه ربانيا أنه من أسرار علوم المكاشفة ولا رخصة في إظهاره إذا لم يظهره رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم // حديث أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم لم يظهر سر الروح أخرجه البخاري من حديث ابن مسعود وقد تقدم ولكنك قبل معرفة ذلك تعلم أن للقلب ميلا إلى صفات بهيمية كالأكل والوقاع وإلى صفات سبعية كالقتل والضرب والإيذاء وإلى صفات شيطانية كالمكر والخديعة والإغواء وإلى صفات ربوبية كالكبر والعز والتجبر وطلب الاستعلاء وذلك لأنه مركب من أصول مختلفة يطول شرحها وتفصيلها", "english": "As for the second cause — and it is the stronger one — it is because the spirit (al-rūḥ) is a divine command (amr rabbānī), by which God — exalted is He — described it, when He said, glorified and majestic: {«وَيَسْأَلُونَكَ عَنِ الرُّوحِ قُلِ الرُّوحُ مِنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّي»} (\"And they ask you concerning the spirit; say: The spirit is of the command of my Lord\") [Al-Isrā', 17:85]. The meaning of its being \"divine\" (rabbāniyyan) is that it belongs to the secrets of the sciences of unveiling (asrār ʿulūm al-mukāshafah), and there is no license to disclose it, since the Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not disclose it. [Regarding the hadith that he — peace be upon him — did not reveal the secret of the spirit, it is reported by Al-Bukhārī from the narration of Ibn Masʿūd, and it has preceded.]", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 797", "section_title_en": "The Second Cause of the Heart's Affinity for Sovereignty — The Divine Command and the Innate Love of Uniqueness in Existence", "section_index": 797, "anchors": "- Root: روح (r-w-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of breath, spirit, and that which animates; Al-Farahidi notes its usage for the vital principle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الروح\" is the divine subtlety (al-laṭīfah al-rabbāniyyah) that is the seat of knowledge and command, distinct from corporeal nature; its reality is from the unseen order of God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for the animating spirit; metaphorical (majāz) extension to \"command\" (amr) as a mode of divine origination without intermediary.\n- Root: رب (r-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Lordship, nurturing, and ownership; the root implies mastery and independent governance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الربوبية\" (rubūbiyyah) denotes exclusive sovereignty and self-subsistence, entailing uniqueness (tafarruf) in essence and attributes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for lordship over a domain; metaphorical for the soul's innate aspiration to solitary perfection, as in the reported saying of Pharaoh.\n- Root: كمل (k-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness and perfection; the absence of deficiency or peer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Perfection (kamāl) is the attribute of the divine essence; any participation in existence entails imperfection, for the unique alone is complete.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a thing's full realization; metaphorical for the soul's love of domination as a shadow of divine perfection.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, power, and dominion; the capacity to dispose of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاستيلاء\" (istīlā') is a form of mastery; knowledge is a mode of possession, for the known is encompassed by the knower.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical control; metaphorical for intellectual apprehension as a type of conquest.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The phrase \"إنما الكمال بعد أن يسلم التفرد بالوجود\" restricts perfection to the condition of secured uniqueness, governing the predicate's scope.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "يعرفون منها إلا قدرا يسيرا بالإضافة إلى ما في علم الله تعالى {وما أوتيتم من العلم إلا قليلا} فإذن كل من استعمل شيئا في جهة غير الجهة التي خلق لها ولا على الوجه الذي أريد به فقد كفر فيه نعمة الله تعالى فمن ضرب غيره بيده فقد كفر نعمة اليد إذ خلقت له اليد ليدفع بها عن نفسه ما يهلكه ويأخذ ما ينفعه لا ليهلك بها غيره ومن نظر إلى وجه غير المحرم فقد كفر نعمة العين ونعمة الشمس إذ الإبصار يتم بهما وإنما خلقتا ليبصر بهما ما ينفعه في دينه ودنياه ويتقي بهما ما يضره فيهما فقد استعملها في غير ما اريد به وهذا لأن المراد من خلق الخلق وخلق الدنيا وأسباب", "english": "...and there is no omnipotent one save the King, the Compeller. And when the covering is lifted from the eyes of the heedless, and they behold the matter to be thus, they will hear at that time the call of the Proclaimer: {«لِمَنِ الْمُلْكُ الْيَوْمَ لِلَّهِ الْوَاحِدِ الْقَهَّارِ»} — \"To whom belongs the sovereignty this Day? To God, the One, the Compeller\" {Q 40:16}. And indeed sovereignty belonged to God, the One, the Compeller, every single day — not exclusively on that day — but the heedless do not hear this proclamation save on that day. Thus it is tidings of what is renewed for the heedless of the unveiling of states, at a time when the unveiling will not benefit them. So we seek refuge in God, the Forbearing, the Generous, from ignorance and blindness, for it is the root cause of destruction.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 982", "section_title_en": "The Unveiling of Sovereignty and the Discernment of What God Loves from What He Abhors", "section_index": 982, "anchors": "- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; disclosure of what was hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Unveiling of the unseen realities (mukāshafah) — the lifting of the veil from the heart's eye to perceive things as they truly are.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): removal of a physical covering. Metaphorical (majāz): the disclosure of intellectual or spiritual realities to the percipient.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses is malik (king).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True sovereignty (mulk) belongs exclusively to God; all created dominion is borrowed and ephemeral.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: royal authority. Metaphorical: absolute, unshared dominion — as in the Qur'anic proclamation of sole kingship on the Day of Judgment.\n- Root: شكر (shakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gratitude; acknowledgment of beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Thankfulness is the recognition of the Benefactor and the employment of His blessings in that which pleases Him — not mere verbal praise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: requital of favor. Metaphorical: the use of blessings in obedience to the Giver, which is the true meaning of gratitude.\n- Root: كفر (kafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, concealing; ingratitude that veils the recognition of favor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kufr is the covering of blessings by failing to employ them in the manner intended by the Giver, or by employing them in what He abhors.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a seed in the earth. Metaphorical: the concealment of God's blessings through misuse or neglect.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — restricting the means of gratitude to two exclusive cognitive faculties, and the restriction of creation's purpose to the sole end of attaining unto God.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "86": [{"arabic": "له فقرأ الإمام {ولئن شئنا لنذهبن بالذي أوحينا إليك} فزعق الشبلي زعقة ظن الناس أنه قد طارت روحه وأحمر وجهه وارتعدت فرائصه وكان يقول بمثل هذا يخاطب الأحباب يردد ذلك مرارا وقال الجنيد دخلت على سرى السقطي فرأيت بين يديه رجلا قد غشي عليه فقال لي هذا رجل قد سمع آية من القرآن فغشي عليه فقلت اقرءوا عليه تلك الآية بعينها فقرئت فأفاق فقال من أين قلت هذا فقلت رأيت يعقوب عليه السلام كان عماه من أجل مخلوق فبمخلوق أبصر ولو كان عماه من أجل الحق ما أبصر بمخلوق فاستحسن ذلك ويشير إلى ما قاله الجنيد قول الشاعر وك", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: وجد (Wajd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root وجد encompasses multiple senses: finding/attaining (إدراك), intense love (الحب الشديد), grief (الحزن), and wealth/sufficiency (الغنى). Al-Khalil notes the range: \"الوجد: الحب. والوجد: الحزن. والوجد: الغنى. والوجد: إدراك الشيء\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجد: إدراك الشيء على خلاف ما يطلب\" — and in the technical terminology of the Sufis: \"ما يصادف القلب من واردات الأحوال\" (what the heart encounters of the influxes of spiritual states)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is finding/attaining; metaphorical extension (majāz) in devotional context: \"وجد فلان في العبادة وجدا: إذا أتى بها على وجهها مع حضور القلب\"\n- Root: طبع (Tabʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the innate disposition upon which one is created; also \"الختم\" (sealing)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطبع: السجية التي جبل عليها الإنسان\" — the original constitution\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes \"مطبوع\" (innate/natural) from \"متكلف\" (deliberately acquired) — \"فلان مطبوع على الكرم، ومتكلف له\"\n- Root: كلف (Kulfah/Takalluf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"التكلف: تجشم ما لا يلزمك\" — undertaking what is not incumbent upon you with effort and hardship\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التكلف: تجشم ما ليس من شأنه\" — burdening oneself with what is not one's natural concern\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تكلفت الأمر: تجشمته على مشقة\" — deliberate self-imposition of effort\n- Root: سمع (Samāʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Auditory perception; also what is heard — recitation, song, remembrance\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السماع عند الصوفية: ما يهيج الوجد من الأصوات الموزونة\" — that which stirs ecstatic finding from measured sounds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"السماع عند القوم: حضور مجلس يهيج الأحوال الشريفة\" — attending an assembly that stirs noble spiritual states\n- Root: عشق (ʿIshq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العشق: فرط الحب\" — excessive love; from \"العشقة\" — a plant that twines around another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العشق: فرط الحب، وأصله من عشقة النبات وهي ما التف منه على غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"العشق مجاز في حق الله تعالى\" — metaphorical when applied to God\n- Root: بكى (Bukāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping, shedding tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البكاء: إجراء الدمع من عين لحزن أو غيره\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تباكى: تكلف البكاء\" — deliberate self-induced weeping\n- Root: صعق (Ṣaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الصعق: الغشي. والصعق: الموت\" — swooning/loss of consciousness; death\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصعق: غشية تعرض من صوت شديد أو هول\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"صعق من هول الأمر: إذا غشي عليه من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: خشع (Khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخشوع: الخضوع والتذلل\" — humility and submissiveness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: التذلل والخضوع، وقيل: الخشوع في الصوت والبصر، والخضوع في البدن\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"خشعت الأصوات: سكنت. وخشع البصر: انكسر\"\n- Root: أنس (Uns)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأنس: نقيض الوحشة\" — the opposite of loneliness/estrangement\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأنس: حضور القلب مع المحبوب\" — the heart's presence with the Beloved\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الأنس بالله: سكون القلب إليه\"\n- Root: وجل (Wajal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\" — fear accompanied by cardiac agitation\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: الخوف مع اضطراب القلب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الوجل: أشد من الخوف، فإنه مع اضطراب\"\n- Root: طمأن (Ṭumaʾnīnah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الطمأنينة: السكون\" — tranquility\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطمأنينة: سكون القلب بعد اضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اطمأن القلب: سكن بعد اضطراب\"\n- Root: قشعر (Iqshirʿār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"القشعريرة: تقبض الجلد من الخوف\" — contraction of the skin from fear\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاقشعرار: انقباض الجلد وتقبضه من الخوف\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"اقشعر الجلد من الخوف: إذا تقبض\"\n- Root: لين (Līn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اللين: ضد الخشونة\" — softness, opposite of hardness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللين: نقيض الخشونة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لان قلبه: إذا سكن وخشع\"\n- Root: غشي (Ghashy)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الغشي: الإغماء\" — loss of consciousness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغشية: ما يغشى القلب من شدة الوجد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"غشي عليه: إذا أغمي عليه من شدة وجد أو خوف\"\n- Root: زعق (Zaʿq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\" — violent screaming\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الزعق: الصياح الشديد\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"زعق: صاح بأعلى صوته\"\n- Root: أزيز (Azīz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل\" — the sound of fire and boiling pot\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الأزيز: صوت النار والمرجل ونحوهما\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أزيز الصدر: صوت يسمع من شدة البكاء والوجد\"\n- Root: شهق (Shahīq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\" — drawing breath inward with sound\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشهيق: رد النفس إلى الداخل بصوت\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شهق الرجل: إذا خرجت نفسه بصوت من شدة الوجد\"\n- Root: حزن (Ḥuzn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الحزن: ضد السرور\" — grief, opposite of joy\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الحزن: ألم النفس\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الحزن: ألم النفس، وهو مجاز من حزن الأرض\" (metaphorical extension from rough ground)\n- Root: شيب (Shayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\" — whitening of hair\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الشيب: بياض الشعر\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"شيبتني هود: أي: كبرتني وأحزنتني حتى شاب شعري\"\n- Root: ذرف (Dharf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الذرف: سيلان الدمع\" — flowing of tears\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"ذرفت العين: سال دمعها\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ذرفت عينه: إذا سال دمعها من حزن\"\n- Root: فيض (Fayḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفيض: سيلان الماء\" — overflowing of water\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفيض: جريان الماء بكثرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"فاضت العين بالدمع: سالت. وفاض القلب: امتلأ\"\n- Root: نقر (Naqr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النقر: الضرب على الشيء حتى يصوت\" — striking something until it sounds\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الناقور: الصور\" — the Trumpet\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الناقور: الصور الذي ينفخ فيه إسرافيل\"\n- Root: رعش (Riʿshah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الرعدة: الاضطراب\" — trembling\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الارتعداد: الاضطراب\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: اضطربت من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: فرص (Farīṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\" — the flesh between the side and shoulder blade that trembles at fright\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الفريصة: لحمة بين الجنب والكتف ترعد عند الفزع\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ارتعدت فرائصه: من شدة الخوف\"\n- Root: عما (ʿAmā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العمى: ذهاب البصر. والعمى: ذهاب البصيرة\" — blindness of sight; blindness of insight\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"العمى: آفة في البصر، وقد يستعار لآفة البصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"عمي القلب: ذهبت بصيرته\"\n- Root: أبصر (Abṣara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"البصر: حاسة الرؤية. والبصيرة: العلم\" — sight; insight\n  *Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الإبصار: إدراك البصر، وقد يستعار للبصيرة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أبصر الحق: علمه. ومنه: البصيرة\"\n- Root: كأس (Ka's)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\" — the cup when it contains drink\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الكأس: الإناء المعروف، وقد يقال لما فيه\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الكأس: القدح إذا كان فيه شراب\"", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 511", "section_title_en": "Section 511", "section_index": 511, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Insight"}], "88": [{"arabic": "وكان ينادي بين أظهرهم أن يأتوا بمثله ولو كان بعضهم لبعض ظهيرا وقال ذلك تعجيزا لهم فعجزوا عن ذلك وصرفوا عنه حتى عرضوا أنفسهم للقتل ونساءهم وذراريهم للسبي وما استطاعوا أن يعارضوا ولا أن يقدحوا في جزالته وحسنه ثم انتشر ذلك بعده في أقطار العالم شرقا وغربا قرنا بعد قرن وعصرا بعد عصر وقد انقرض اليوم قريب من خمسمائة سنة فلم يقدر أحد على معارضته فأعظم بغباوة من ينظر في أحواله ثم في أقواله ثم في أفعاله ثم في أخلاقه ثم في معجزاته ثم في استمرار شرعه إلى الآن ثم في انتشاره في أقطار العالم ثم في إذعان ملوك ال", "english": "Know that whoever witnesses his states, peace be upon him, and lends ear to the hearing of his reports — which comprise his moral characters, his acts, his states, his customs, his natural dispositions, his political management of the various classes of creation, his guidance in disciplining them, his drawing together of the various classes of creation, his leading of them to his obedience — along with what is related of the wonders of his answers in the narrow straits of questions, the novelties of his deliberations concerning the interests of creation, and the excellences of his indications in the detailing of the outward Sacred Law, whose initial subtleties the jurists and the intelligent are incapable of grasping in the entirety of their lifetimes — no doubt or skepticism remains for him that all of that was not acquired by any stratagem by which human power could accomplish it. Rather, that cannot be conceived except by drawing upon a heavenly reinforcement (taʾyīd samāwī) and a divine power (quwwah ilāhiyyah). And all of that cannot be conceived of a liar nor of one who confuses and obscures. Rather, his natural dispositions and his states were decisive evidences (shawāhid qāṭiʿah) of his truthfulness — so much so that a pure Bedouin Arab, when he saw him, would say: \"By God, this is not the face of a liar!\" Thus he would testify to his truthfulness by the mere sight of his natural dispositions. How then [would it be] for one who witnessed his moral characters and practiced his states in all his comings and goings?", "book_title": "Etiquette of Living and Prophetic Character", "section_title_ar": "Section 571", "section_title_en": "** Section 571 — On the Evidences of His Truthfulness (Peace Be Upon Him): The Breaking of Custom (Kharq al-ʿĀdah) and the Manifestation of Miracles", "section_index": 571, "anchors": "**\n- Root: شهد (sh-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To witness, to testify, to be present; shāhid (witness) — one who attests to what he has perceived\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shahādah in the theological sense is the testimony of certainty that establishes truth; shawāhid (evidences) are the proofs that compel assent\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — physical witnessing by presence; Majāz — the witnessing of the heart through certain knowledge (ʿilm al-yaqīn)\n- Root: خرق (kh-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To tear, to rend, to pierce through; kharq al-ʿādah — the rending of the customary order\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The breaking of the established pattern (ʿādah) as the divine suspension of secondary causality — a sign (āyah) of prophetic truthfulness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — tearing a garment; Majāz — the divine act of suspending the customary nexus between causes and effects\n- Root: معج (m-ʿ-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Muʿjizah — that which renders others incapable (iʿjāz); the miracle as the demonstration of prophetic truth through the incapacity of creation to produce its like\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The muʿjizah is distinguished from mere kharq al-ʿādah by being conjoined with the challenge (taḥaddī) and the absence of any countervailing claim\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — incapacity; Majāz — the miraculous act that manifests incapacity in others\n- Root: تواتر (t-w-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Tawātur — successive transmission by multiple chains that preclude collusion upon falsehood\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The epistemological force of tawātur in producing necessary knowledge (ʿilm ḍarūrī)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — sequential following; Majāz — the cumulative certainty produced by mass transmission\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of \"إنما\" and restrictive constructions to delimit the scope of predication, as in the author's restriction of the discussion to what has been widely transmitted (mustafīḍ)\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Etiquette of Living and Prophetic Character'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "94": [{"arabic": "الأسباب وقالوا متعجبين أبعث الله بشرا رسولا {وقالوا} لولا أنزل علينا الملائكة وقال تعالى أو عجبتم أن جاءكم ذكر من ربكم على رجل منكم الآية السبب الخامس الخوف من فوت المقاصد وذلك يختص بمتزاحمين على مقصود واحد فإن كان واحد يحسد صاحبه في كل نعمة تكون عونا له في الانفراد بمقصوده ومن هذا الجنس تحاسد الضرات في التزاحم على مقاصد الزوجية وتحاسد الأخوة في التزاحم على نيل المنزلة في قلب الأبوين للتوصل به إلى مقاصد الكرامة والمال وكذلك تحاسد التلميذين لأستاذ واحد على نيل المرتبة من قلب الأستاذ وتحاسد ندما", "english": "I say: God is Knower of what is in the breasts. {«إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ تَسُؤْهُمْ»} — \"If good touches you, it grieves them\" {«وَدُّوا مَا عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضَاءُ مِنْ أَفْوَاهِهِمْ وَمَا تُخْفِي صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ»} — \"They wish for your hardship; hatred has already appeared from their mouths, and what their breasts conceal is greater.\" And envy (ḥasad), arising from hatred, may lead to contention, mutual fighting, and the spending of one's entire life in removing another's blessing through stratagems, slander, the tearing of veils, and whatever runs in that course.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 722", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Rebuke of the Self: The Seven Causes of Envy (Section 722)", "section_index": 722, "anchors": "- Root: سبب (sabab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The rope by which one climbs; every means by which one reaches a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Every means by which one arrives at something is called a sabab\" — thus the causes enumerated here are the psychological \"ropes\" by which envy ascends in the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord. Metaphorical: any linkage or occasion — here, the inward occasions (asbāb) that give rise to ḥasad.\n- Root: بغض (bughḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hatred, aversion of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The opposite of love (maḥabbah); a disposition of the heart toward another's harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inner aversion. Metaphorical: its \"appearance from the mouths\" (Quranic) as outward speech betraying the concealed hatred.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Greatness; pride; disdain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kabīr and al-ṣaghīr are relative terms\" — pride is the claim to greatness relative to another, deeming oneself above him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bigness in stature or age. Metaphorical: \"takabbara 'alayhi\" — to deem oneself greater than another and refuse submission to him.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Straightness of path; intending a goal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qaṣd: istiqāmat al-ṭarīq\" — the straight road; also moderation between extremes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to head toward a place. Metaphorical: \"maqāṣid\" — the aims and objectives toward which one directs oneself; here, the worldly goals whose pursuit breeds rivalry and envy.\n- Root: نزل (nazal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To descend from a height; to alight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-nuzūl fī al-aṣl huwa inḥiṭāṭ min 'uluww\" — descent from above; the sending down of revelation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: descending from a high place. Metaphorical: \"anzala Allāhu al-kitāb\" — God's sending down of the Quran; and \"mā unzila\" — what was revealed, as in the Quranic citation regarding the Qurashites' objection to revelation upon a mere human.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Causal Coordination (al-'aṭf al-sababī) — The enumeration of the seven causes proceeds by coordinated clauses (fa-... thumma ...), each introduced as an independent proposition linked by causation (sabab), requiring the translator to preserve the logical sequence of psychological motivations without collapsing them into one another.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "100": [{"arabic": "له ما فعل الله بك قال غفر لي وقال قل لأيوب قل لو أنتم تملكون خزائن رحمة ربي إذا لأمسكتم خشية الإنفاق وقال بعضهم رأيت في الليلة التي مات فيها", "english": "...you would dwell. She said: \"Every joyful, merry heart.\" He said: \"Then I awoke and pledged myself that I would not laugh except when overcome [by it].\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1332", "section_title_en": "The Visions of the Righteous After Death — Dreams of the Prophet's Companions, the Pious, and Iblis (Section 1332)", "section_index": 1332, "anchors": "- Root: نوم (n-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sleep and dreaming; the state wherein the soul perceives unseen realities.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النوم: فسر على أوجه كلها صحيح بنظرات مختلفة... وقيل: النوم موت خفيف، والموت نوم ثقيل\" — Sleep is a light death; visions in sleep (ru'ya) are a channel for divine communication and post-mortem disclosures.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رأى في المنام كذا\" (he saw such-and-such in a dream). Metaphorical: \"نامت السوق: كسدت\" (the market slept = stagnated) — here, dreams are literal vehicles for veridical spiritual encounters.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, shielding from defilement; forgiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران والمغفرة من الله هو أن يصون العبد من أن يمسه العذاب\" — Divine forgiveness is God's shielding of the servant from touching chastisement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"اللهم غفرا\" (O God, forgiveness). Metaphorical: beasts \"not forgiving\" a lapse = devouring the young — here, God's forgiveness is literal, absolute, and gracious.\n- Root: سعد (s-'-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Felicity, divine aid toward good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السعادة: معاونة الأمور الإلهية للإنسان على نيل الخير\" — Happiness is the cooperation of divine matters with man to attain the good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أسعده الله\" (God made him fortunate). Metaphorical: \"ساعد الدهر\" (time aided) — here, the blessed state of the deceased is literal divine favor.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — In the phrase \"وإنما يخاف من نور يكون في القلب\" (\"but rather he fears only a light that resides in the heart\"), إنما restricts fear exclusively to the spiritual light, negating all corporeal means.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "108": [{"arabic": "لا إله إلا الله سبحان الله إن كان وعد ربنا لمفعولا قال فعجبت حين عرفني ولا والله ما رأيته قبل ذلك ولا رآني فقلت من أين عرفت اسمي واسم أبي وما رأيتك قبل اليوم قال نبأني العليم الخبير وعرفت روحي روحك حين كلمت نفسي نفسك إن الأرواح لها أنفس كأنفس الأجساد وإن المؤمنين ليعرف بعضهم بعضا ويتحابون بروح الله وإن لم يلتقوا يتعارفون ويتكلمون وإن نأت بهم الدار وتفرقت بهم المنازل قال قلت حدثني رحمك الله عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بحديث أسمعه منك قال إني لم أدرك رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ولم تكن لي م", "english": "And the gatherings of passion are five matters, which are what Allah, the Exalted, has gathered in His saying: {إنما الحياة الدنيا لعب ولهو وزينة وتفاخر بينكم وتكاثر في الأموال والأولاد} — \"The life of this world is but play and diversion and adornment and boasting among yourselves and increase in wealth and children\" (57:20). And the corporeal entities (al-aʿyān) from which these five are obtained are seven, gathered by His saying, the Exalted: {زين للناس حب الشهوات من النساء والبنين والقناطير المقنطرة من الذهب والفضة والخيل المسومة والأنعام والحرث ذلك متاع الحياة الدنيا} — \"Adorned for mankind is the love of desires — of women and sons and heaped-up treasures of gold and silver and branded horses and cattle and tillage. That is the enjoyment of the life of this world\" (3:14).", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 744", "section_title_en": "The Discipline of the Soul and the Restraint of the Passions — The Life of Uways al-Qarani and the True Boundary of the Worldly Life", "section_index": 744, "anchors": "- Root: ن-ه-ي (N-H-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"نهاه عنه نهياً: كفّه وردّه عنه. والنهي: خلاف الأمر.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النهي: الزجر عن الشيء، ويستعمل في الكفّ عن الفعل. قال تعالى: {ونهى النفس عن الهوى} — أي: كفّها وزجرها عن متابعة الشهوات.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نهاه عن كذا: كفّه. ومنه: انتهى عن الأمر إذا كفّ نفسه. والتناهي: التوقف عند الحد.\"\n- Root: ه-و-ي (H-W-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الهوى: ميل النفس إلى الشهوة. وهويته: أحببته. والهوى: ما تهواه النفس وتنزع إليه من غير هدى.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الهوى: ميل النفس إلى ما تشتهيه، ويذمّ إذا لم يكن بعقل وشرع. قال تعالى: {أفرأيت من اتخذ إلهه هواه}.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هويته: أحببته. وهوًى: سقط. والهوى: اسم لما تهواه النفس من غير رشد. وقد غلب في الشرع على الميل المذموم.\"\n- Root: د-ن-و (D-N-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دنا يدنو دنواً: قرب. والدنيا: نقيض الآخرة، سميت بذلك لدنوّها وتقدّمها.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات أو بالحكم. والدنيا: الحياة العاجلة، سميت دنيا لدنوّها من الإنسان أو لتأخر الآخرة عنها.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"الدنيا: الحاضرة العاجلة. وتقول: هو من أهل الدنيا إذا كان مقبلاً على شهواتها.\"\n- Root: ن-ف-س (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النفس: الروح، والذات. ونفس الشيء: عينه. والنفس: الدم. والنفيس: الثمين.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح، وتستعمل للذات. قال تعالى: {ويحذركم الله نفسه} أي: ذاته. وقد تكون النفس بمعنى الهوى والشهوة.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نفس الشيء: عينه وذاته. وأنفس الشيء: صار نفيساً. ومن المجاز: نفس عن كذا أي: فرّج عنه.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين المبتدأ والخبر (Interposition between Subject and Predicate) — The periodic structure allows for extended parenthetical insertions between the subject and its predicate, as in the long narrative sequences here, where the subject (أويس) is introduced and its predicate follows after extensive descriptive clauses.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "109": [{"arabic": "المستكبرين عن أمرك أو على أوليائك وإذا قرأ قوله تعالى {ويخرون للأذقان يبكون ويزيدهم خشوعا} فيقول اللهم اجعلني من الباكين إليك الخاشعين لك وكذلك كل سجدة ويشترط في هذه السجدة شروط الصلاة من ستر العورة واستقبال القبلة وطهارة الثوب والبدن من الحدث والخبث ومن لم يكن على طهارة عند السماع فإذا تطهر يسجد وقد قيل في كمالها إنه يكبر رافعا يديه لتحريمه ثم يكبر للهوي للسجود ثم يكبر للارتفاع ثم يسلم وزاد زائدون التشهد ولا أصل لهذا إلا القياس على سجود الصلاة وهو بعيد فإنه ورد الأمر في السجود فليتبع فيه الأمر وتكبيرة الهوى أقرب للبداية وما عد", "english": "If he recites without ritual purity, reclining upon his bed, he also has merit, but it is below that [of the one who is pure and seated properly]. Allah Most High said: {الَّذِينَ يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ قِيَامًا وَقُعُودًا وَعَلَىٰ جُنُوبِهِمْ وَيَتَفَكَّرُونَ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ} (\"Those who remember Allah standing, sitting, and upon their sides, and reflect upon the creation of the heavens and the earth\") — and He praised all of them, but He mentioned standing first, then sitting, then remembrance while reclining.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 223", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Qur'an Recitation — Section 223: On the Manners of the Reciter, the Measure of Recitation, Division into Portions, Orthography, Tartīl, Weeping, Rights of the Verses, Invocations, and Vocalization", "section_index": 223, "anchors": "- Root: قرا (Q-R-A)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To read, recite, gather together; the core act of assembling letters into articulated speech.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Recitation (tilāwah) is the following of the Qur'an in obedience and belief; qirā'ah is the articulation of the letters. The term carries both the physical act of vocalization and the spiritual act of devotion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): to read from a written text or from memory. Metaphorical (majāz): \"qara'a al-rajul\" — to become religious, devoted, as the reciter is characterized by the state of worship.\n- Root: سجد (S-J-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bow, prostrate, lower oneself; the physical posture of placing the forehead on the ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Prostration is of two kinds: voluntary (ikhtiyārī) — exclusive to humans, by which reward is deserved; and subjugated (taskhīrī) — universal to all creation, as in {وَلِلَّهِ يَسْجُدُ مَنْ فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of prostration. Metaphorical: \"shajarah sājidah\" — a tree bending over; \"ʿayn sājidah\" — a languid, drooping eye. The prostration of recitation is the literal act performed in obedience to the command of the verse.\n- Root: تلو (T-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To follow, to recite; the reciter \"follows\" the letters in sequence and \"follows\" the commands contained therein.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tilāwah is following the Qur'an in deed as well as in word; the true reciter is he whose recitation is a following of the divine command.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The conditional and restrictive structures in the prophetic sayings (e.g., \"من قرأ القرآن في أقل من ثلاث لم يفقهه\") establish a causal link between the act and its consequence, requiring precise parsing of the conditional clause to extract the legal ruling.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "110": [{"arabic": "ادعوني أستجب لكم إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين} وقال عز وجل {قل ادعوا الله أو ادعوا الرحمن أيا ما تدعوا فله الأسماء الحسنى} وروى النعمان بن بشير عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إن الدعاء هو العبادة ثم قرأ ادعوني أستجب لكم (1) الآية وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم الدعاء مخ العبادة (2) وروى أبو هريرة أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم قال ليس شيء أكرم على الله عز وجل من الدعاء (3) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن العبد لا يخطئه من الدعاء إحدى ثلاث إما ذنب يغفر له وإما خير يعجل له وإما خير يدخر له (4) وقال أبو ذر رضي الله عنه يكفي من الدعاء مع البر ما يكفي الطعام من الملح وق", "english": "Allah the Exalted has said: {«وَإِذَا سَأَلَكَ عِبَادِي عَنِّي فَإِنِّي قَرِيبٌ أُجِيبُ دَعْوَةَ الدَّاعِ إِذَا دَعَانِ فَلْيَسْتَجِيبُوا لِي»} (And when My servants ask you concerning Me — indeed I am near. I respond to the invocation of the supplicant when he calls upon Me. So let them respond to Me — Qurʾan 2:186). And He the Exalted said: {«ادْعُوا رَبَّكُمْ تَضَرُّعًا وَخُفْيَةً إِنَّهُ لَا يُحِبُّ الْمُعْتَدِينَ»} (Call upon your Lord in humility and privately; indeed, He does not like transgressors — Qurʾan 7:55). And He the Exalted said: {«وَقَالَ رَبُّكُمُ ادْعُونِي أَسْتَجِبْ لَكُمْ إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَسْتَكْبِرُونَ عَنْ عِبَادَتِي سَيَدْخُلُونَ جَهَنَّمَ دَاخِرِينَ»} (And your Lord said: Call upon Me; I will respond to you. Indeed, those who disdain My worship will enter Hell humiliated — Qurʾan 40:60). And He, Glorified and Exalted, said: {«قُلِ ادْعُوا اللَّهَ أَوِ ادْعُوا الرَّحْمَنَ أَيًّا مَا تَدْعُوا فَلَهُ الْأَسْمَاءُ الْحُسْنَى»} (Say: Call upon Allah or call upon the Most Merciful. Whichever [name] you call — to Him belong the Most Beautiful Names — Qurʾan 17:110).", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 241", "section_title_en": "The Book of Invocation — Section 241: The Second Chapter on the Etiquette of Supplication (Duʿāʾ), Its Virtue, the Virtue of Certain Transmitted Supplications, the Virtue of Seeking Forgiveness (Istighfār), and Invoking Blessings upon the Messenger of Allah (Peace Be Upon Him)", "section_index": 241, "anchors": "- Root: دعو (Daʿā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الدعاء: الرغبة إلى الله تعالى، والاستعانة به. وأصل الدعو: الإقبال على الشيء بالرغبة والطلب. والدعوة: الكلمة التي يدعو بها. والمدعوّ: الذي يجيب. والداعي: الفاعل. واستدعاه: طلب منه الدعاء.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدعاء: الرغبة إلى الله تعالى في قضاء الحاجة، وهو ضربان: دعاء عبادة، ودعاء مسألة. فدعاء العبادة: هو التوجه إلى الله تعالى بالطاعة والإخلاص، ودعاء المسألة: هو طلب الحاجة. قال تعالى: {وقال ربكم ادعوني أستجب لكم} — فجعل الدعاء عبادة، إذ قال: {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي} فعطف العبادة على الدعاء، فدلّ على أنهما واحد.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"دعوته دعواً ودعاءً: ناديته وصحت به. ودعا الله: ابتهل إليه بالسؤال. ودعا الرجل: استنصره. ودعا الناقة: استدبرها.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الدعاء في العبادة: استعارة من دعاء المسألة، لأن العابد كأنه يسأل الله الثواب والقبول. ومنه قوله تعالى: {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي} — أي عن دعائي، فسمّى العبادة دعاءً.\"\n\n- Root: سحر (Saḥara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السحر: طرف الحلقوم والرئة. وقيل: السحر: آخر الليل عند الصبح. والسحور: الأكل في السحر. والسحرة: السحر الأخير من الليل.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السحر يقال على معان: الأول: الخداع وتخييلات لا حقيقة لها... والثاني: وقت السحر، وهو آخر الليل، سمي بذلك لأنه وقت يكون فيه تنفس الصعداء واسترواح من ظلمة الليل، أو لأنه وقت خفاء الناس وسكون الأصوات.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"لقيته سحراً وسحرة وبالسحر وفي أعلى السحرين وهما سحر مع الصبح وسحر قبله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"السحر في قوله تعالى: {وبالأسحار هم يستغفرون} — هو الوقت الأخير من الليل، وهو أشرف الأوقات للدعاء، لأنه وقت غفلة الناس وخلوة القلب.\"\n\n- Root: عرف (ʿArafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"العرف: الطيب. والمعرفة: العلم بالشيء على وجه التدبر. وعرفة: جبل بمكة، سمي بذلك لأن الناس يتعارفون فيه، أو لأن جبريل عليه السلام عرّف إبراهيم المناسك فيه.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"عرفة: اسم للموقف الذي يقف فيه الحجاج، سمي بذلك لأن آدم وحواء تعارفا فيه بعد الهبوط، وقيل: لأن جبريل عرّف إبراهيم المناسك فيه.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"يوم عرفة: اليوم التاسع من ذي الحجة، وهو يوم الموقف.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"عرفة في قولهم: يوم عرفة — اسم للموقف، سمي بذلك لتعارف الناس فيه، أو لتعريف جبريل إبراهيم المناسك.\"\n\n- Root: غفر (Ghafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المغفرة: ستر الذنب والتجاوز عنه. والغفر: الستر. والمغفر: ما يستر الرأس في الحرب. والغفور: الكثير المغفرة.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران: ستر الذنب والتجاوز عنه، وأصله من الغفر وهو الستر. قال تعالى: {وإن تغفر لهم فإنك أنت العزيز الحكيم}. والاستغفار: طلب المغفرة، وهو ضربان: استغفار باللسان، واستغفار بالقلب واللسان معاً، وهو النافع.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"غفرت الذنب غفراً ومغفرة: سترته وتجاوزت عنه. واستغفرت الله: طلبت مغفرته.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الاستغفار باللسان مع الإصرار: استعارة للكذب، لأن المستغفر الحقيقي هو التائب المقلع عن الذنب.\"\n\n- Root: صلو (Ṣalā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الصلاة: الدعاء، وقيل: العبادة، وقيل: الرحمة من الله، والاستغفار من الملائكة. والصلاة من الله: الرحمة، ومن الملائكة: الاستغفار، ومن المؤمنين: الدعاء.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصلاة في الأصل: الدعاء، قال تعالى: {صلوا عليه وسلموا تسليماً} — أي ادعوا له. ثم غلب استعمالها في العبادة المخصوصة، والصلاة من الله: الرحمة، ومن الملائكة: الاستغفار.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"الصلاة: الدعاء. ومنه قوله تعالى: {وصل عليهم إن صلاتك سكن لهم} — أي ادع لهم.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الصلاة على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: استعارة للدعاء له بالرحمة والتعظيم، لأن أصل الصلاة الدعاء.\"\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication)\n  * Governing Rule: \"إنما\" تفيد الحصر والقصر، فإذا قيل: \"إن الدعاء هو العبادة\" — فالمبتدأ والخبر معرفتان، والجملة تفيد الحصر والتأكيد. وإذا قيل: \"الدعاء مخ العبادة\" — فالإسناد فيه تشبيه بليغ، حيث شبه الدعاء بالمخ الذي هو لبّ العظم وجوهره، فالدعاء هو اللبّ والجوهر الحقيقي للعبادة.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "وقالت عائشة رضي الله عنه في قوله عز وجل {ولا تجهر بصلاتك ولا تخافت بها} (11) أي بدعائك وقد أثنى الله عز وجل على نبيه زكرياء عليه السلام حيث قال {إذ نادى ربه نداء خفيا} وقال عز وجل {ادعوا ربكم تضرعا وخفية} الخامس أن لا يتكلف السجع في الدعاء فإن حال الداعي ينبغي أن يكون حال متضرع والتكلف لا يناسبه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم سيكون قوم يعتدون في الدعاء (12) وقد قال عز وجل {ادعوا ربكم تضرعا وخفية إنه} {لا يحب المعتدين} قيل معناه التكلف للأسجاع والأولى أن لا يجاوز الدعوات المأثورة فإنه قد يعتدي في دعائ", "english": "[Root: دعو]\n  • Lisan al-Arab: \"الدعاء: الرغبة إلى الله تعالى، والابتهال في المسألة. والدعوة: الكلمة التي يدعو بها. وأصل الدعاء: طلب الإقبال من الغير.\"\n  • Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدعاء: ضربان: أحدهما دعاء العبادة، والآخر دعاء المسألة. فدعاء العبادة: هو توجه العبد إلى الله تعالى بالطاعة. ودعاء المسألة: طلب الحاجة من الله تعالى. قال تعالى: {وقال ربكم ادعوني أستجب لكم}.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"دعا فلان ربه: ابتهل إليه بالسؤال. ودعا الله: عبده. ومنه قوله تعالى: {إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي} أي عن دعائي.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"دعا عليه: سأل الله أن يصيبه بمكروه. ودعا له: سأل الله أن يحسن إليه. ومن المجاز: دعا الأمر إلى كذا، أي أدى إليه.\"", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 242", "section_title_en": "Section 242", "section_index": 242, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}], "111": [{"arabic": "يألفه في الملأ ولا يكون حضور الغير هو السبب في حضور الخاطر كما لا يكون حضور البهيمة سببا فما دام يفرق في أحواله بين مشاهدة إنسان ومشاهدة بهيمة فهو بعد خارج عن صفو الإخلاص مدنس الباطن بالشرك الخفي من الرياء وهذا الشرك أخفي في قلب ابن آدم من دبيب النملة السوداء في الليلة الظلماء على الصخرة الصماء (1) كما ورد في الخبر ولا يسلم من الشيطان إلا من دق نظره وسعد بعصمة الله تعالى وتوفيقه وهدايته وإلا فالشيطان ملازم للمتشعرين لعبادة الله تعالى لا يغفل عنهم لحظة حتى يحملهم على ال", "english": "The fourth degree, which is finer and more hidden: That people look at him while he is in prayer, and the Devil is unable to say to him, \"Be humble for their sake,\" for he knows that he has become alert to that. So the Devil says to him: \"Reflect upon the majesty of Allah the Exalted and His glory, and upon Whose presence you stand. Feel shame that Allah looks upon your heart while it is heedless of Him.\" So by this he summons his heart [to presence] and his limbs become submissive, and he imagines that this is the very essence of sincerity — but it is the very essence of deceit and trickery (makr wa-khidāʿ). For if his humility were due to his contemplation of divine glory, this thought (khāṭir) would accompany him in seclusion, and its presence would not be restricted to the presence of others. The sign of safety from this defilement is that this thought be familiar to him in seclusion just as it is familiar in public, and that the presence of another not be the cause of the thought's presence, just as the presence of a beast is not a cause. So long as he distinguishes in his states between the sight of a human and the sight of a beast, he remains outside the purity of sincerity, his inner being defiled by the hidden polytheism (al-shirk al-khafī) of ostentation. This polytheism is more hidden in the heart of the son of Adam than the creeping of a black ant upon a solid rock in the pitch-dark night {«كما ورد في الخبر»} — as has come in the report. None is saved from the Devil except him whose insight is penetrating and who is blessed by Allah's protection (ʿiṣmah), success (tawfīq), and guidance (hidāyah). Otherwise, the Devil clings to those who adorn themselves for the worship of Allah the Exalted, never neglecting them for a single moment, until he drives them to ostentation in every movement — even in applying kohl to the eye, trimming the moustache, perfuming oneself on Friday, and wearing garments. For these are Sunnahs at specific times, yet the self has in them a hidden share (ḥaẓẓ khafiyy) due to the linkage of creatures' gaze to them and the soul's familiarity (istī'nās) with them. The Devil calls him to do them, saying: \"This is a Sunnah; it should not be abandoned.\" But the heart's inner impulse toward them arises only for that hidden desire, or is mixed with it by an admixture that removes it from the bounds of sincerity. Whatever is not saved from all these defilements is not sincere. Rather, even one who secludes himself (iʿtikāf) in a mosque that is well-populated, clean, and beautiful in construction — to which the temperament feels drawn — the Devil makes it attractive to him and multiplies for him the virtues of seclusion, while the hidden motive in his secret heart is the comfort of the mosque's beautiful form and the soul's rest in it. This becomes evident in his inclination toward one of two mosques, or one of two places, when it is more beautiful than the other. All of this is a mingling with the impurities of temperament (shawā'ib al-ṭabʿ) and the turbidities of the self (kudūrāt al-nafs), and a nullifier of the reality of sincerity. By my life, the alloy (ghishsh) that is mixed with pure gold has varying degrees: some predominates, some is slight, but easy to perceive; and some is so subtle that none perceives it save the discerning assayer (al-nāqid al-baṣīr). The alloy of the heart, the deceit of the Devil, and the malice of the self are far more obscure and subtler than that.", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 1223", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Hidden Defilements Corrupting Sincerity (Āfāt al-Ikhlāṣ)", "section_index": 1223, "anchors": "- Root: ر-ء-ي (r-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"seeing\" (ru'yā) and \"showing\" (murā'āt); riyā' is the act of displaying deeds for the gaze of others.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyā' is a form of shirk khafī (hidden polytheism) — the soul's inclination toward created beholders (khalq) as the motive for worship, displacing exclusive orientation toward Allah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): visual perception; Metaphorical (majāz): seeking social standing (jāh) through ostentatious piety — the \"eye\" of the beholder becomes the qiblah of the heart.\n- Root: خ-ل-ص (kh-l-ṣ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Purity from admixture; the clear essence after dross is removed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ikhlāṣ is the purification of intention (niyyah) from every motive other than Allah; it is the state of the deed being unalloyed (khāliṣ) as gold is freed from alloy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical refinement (taṣfiyah); Metaphorical: \"akhlāṣa al-dīna lillāh\" — dedicating religion exclusively to Allah, with no trace of created purpose.\n- Root: ش-ي-ط (sh-y-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burning, scorching, or spoiling by fire; the one who is \"burned\" (shāṭ) — hence Shayṭān as the one consumed by divine wrath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shayṭān as the personification of insidious suggestion (waswasah) that infiltrates the heart's intent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: meat scorched on the fire; Metaphorical: \"ashāṭa damahu\" — to nullify or waste blood (i.e., to destroy a life); likewise, Shayṭān \"wastes\" the deed's reward by corrupting its sincerity.\n- Root: ن-ف-س (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which breathes and has desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The nafs as the seat of blameworthy inclination (hawā) — its hidden pleasures (shahawāt khafiyyah) are the raw material Shayṭān exploits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul's essence; Metaphorical: \"nafsu-hu lā tasmaḥu\" — the soul's refusal to permit an act, revealing its covert attachment to created opinion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — the subtle insertion of a clause or motive between the agent and the act; syntactically, the \"hidden\" element (the beholder's gaze) interposes itself within the structure of the deed, altering its grammatical and moral valence.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "{لقد صدق الله رسوله الرؤيا بالحق} إلى آخرها (7) وقوله سبحانه الحمد لله الذي لم يتخذ ولدا (8) الآية وخمس آيات من أول الحديد (9) وثلاثا من آخر سورة الحشر (10) وإن قرأ المسبعات العشر التي أهداها الخضر عليه السلام إلى إبراهيم التيمي رحمه الله ووصاه أن يقولها غدوة وعشية فقد استكمل الفضل وجمع له ذلك فضيلة جملة الأدعية المذكورة فقد روي عن كرز بن وبرة رحمه الله وكان من الأبدال قال أتاني أخ لي من أهل الشام فأهدى لي هدية وقال يا كرز اقبل مني هذه الهدية فإنها نعمت الهدية فقلت يا أخي ومن أهدى لك هذه الهدية قال أعطاني", "english": "Now, the first watch (al-wird al-awwal) is that which extends from the dawn prayer (ṣalāt al-ṣubḥ) until sunrise (ṭulūʿ al-shams). When the servant has completed his obligatory prayer, he should remain seated in his place of prayer, occupying himself with the remembrance of God most High, until the sun has risen. For it has been reported that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"That I should sit in my assembly, remembering God most High therein, from the morning prayer until sunrise, is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.\" {«لأن أقعد في مجلسي أذكر الله تعالى فيه من صلاة الغداة إلى طلوع الشمس أحب إلي من أن أعتق أربع رقاب»} And it is related that he, peace and blessings be upon him, when he had prayed the morning prayer, would sit in his place of prayer until the sun rose; and in some versions [it is added] that he would pray two rakʿahs—that is, after sunrise. Concerning the virtue of this [practice], an incalculable number of reports have come down. Al-Ḥasan related that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, used to mention, among what he recounted of his Lord's mercy, that He said: \"O son of Adam, remember Me for an hour after the dawn prayer and for an hour after the afternoon prayer, and I shall suffice you for what lies between them.\" {«يا ابن آدم اذكرني بعد صلاة الفجر ساعة وبعد صلاة العصر ساعة أكفك ما بينهما»}", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 260", "section_title_en": "The Daily Liturgy of the Devout Servant: The First Watch (After Dawn Prayer until Sunrise)", "section_index": 260, "anchors": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, invocation, mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Remembrance of God (dhikr) is the heart's presence with the Divine, ranging from the tongue's utterance to the heart's contemplation; it is the noblest act when accompanied by presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): verbal mention. Metaphorical (majāz): the heart's steadfastness in knowledge of God, which is the reality of dhikr.\n- Root: صلا (ṣallā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prayer (ṣalāh); root meaning of supplication and blessing; also \"to kindle a fire\" (as in ṣalā al-nār).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh is the servant's standing before God in humility and devotion, comprising praise, recitation, bowing, and prostration; its perfection lies in the heart's presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the ritual prayer with its pillars. Metaphorical: supplication (duʿā') and blessing (taṣliyah) upon the Prophet.\n- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To arrive at water; a portion or share of something that comes regularly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The daily portion (wird) of devotion—a set liturgy of prayers, recitations, and remembrances that the servant regularly performs at appointed times.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arriving at a watering place. Metaphorical: the soul's regular approach to the fountain of divine grace through prescribed acts of worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — governing the precise enumeration of the four functions (adʿiyah, adhkār, qirā'ah, fikr) after the dawn prayer, each specified by its own governing verb and object.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "وميدان رحب وعلى أول الميدان عتبة هي مستقر ذلك الأمر الرباني فمن لم يكن له على هذه العتبة جواز ولا لحافظ العتبة مشاهدة واستحال أن يصل الميدان فكيف بالانتهاء إلى ما وراءه من المشاهدات العالية ولذلك قيل من لم يعرف نفسه لم يعرف ربه وأنى يصادف هذا خزانة الأطباء ومن أين للطبيب أن يلاحظه بل المعنى المسمى روحا عند الطبيب بالإضافة إلى هذا الأمر الرباني كالكرة التي يحركها صولجان الملك بالإضافة إلى الملك فمن عرف الروح الطبي فظن أنه أدرك الأمر الرباني كان كمن رأى الكرة التي يحركها صولجان الملك فظن أنه رأى الملك ولا يشك في أن خطأه فاحش", "english": "...the entire body, and its lights which it used to derive from the spirit have departed from it — namely, the lights of the senses, the faculties of power and will, and all that the meaning of the word \"life\" encompasses. This too is a brief allusion to another world among the worlds of God's blessings and the wonders of His craftsmanship and wisdom, so that one may know that {«لو كان البحر مدادا لكلمات ربي لنفد البحر قبل أن تنفد كلمات ربي»} — \"If the sea were ink for the words of my Lord, the sea would surely be exhausted before the words of my Lord were exhausted\" — Glory be to Him, the Exalted. So perdition to him who disbelieves in God; perdition and ruin to him who denies His blessing.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1006", "section_title_en": "On the Spirit as a Divine Command — The Limits of Reason in Comprehending the Soul", "section_index": 1006, "anchors": "- Root: روح (rūḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes breath, wind, and that which gives life; al-Khalil notes its application to both the physical breath and the animating principle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الروح والروح في الأصل واحد، وجعل الروح اسما للنفس\" — the spirit and the breath share one origin, with the spirit made a name for the soul/self (nafs), distinguishing the corporeal vehicle from its immaterial source.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal sense (ḥaqīqah) applies to the physical subtle body; the metaphorical extension (majāz) points to the divine command (amr) which is not spatially extended nor corporeal.\n- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root signifies binding, restraining, and comprehending; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from error.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم\" — the intellect is the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and also the knowledge acquired thereby; it is a created, limited instrument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes rational apprehension; metaphorical usage shows its confinement — the intellect is \"bound\" within the categories of substance (jawhar) and accident (ʿaraḍ), unable to exceed its proper domain.\n- Root: وصف (waṣf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Description is the mention of a thing by its attributes and qualities.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description may be true or false; the divine command (amr rabbānī) admits of no descriptive definition by created intellects.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: describing an object's attributes; metaphorical: \"لسانه يصف الكذب\" — a tongue that describes falsehood, showing that description is limited to what the describer can encompass.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The verse {قُلِ الرُّوحُ مِنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّي} employs restrictive predication: the spirit is not defined by essence but only attributed to the divine command, confining the answer to relation (nisbah) rather than quiddity (māhiyyah).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 1558], [2, 1558, 3156], [3, 3156, 4448], [4, 4448, 5661], [5, 5661, 6817], [6, 6817, 7599], [7, 7599, 8823], [8, 8823, 10183], [9, 10183, 11022], [10, 11022, 12246], [11